        <p>ALSO MOLYNEUX's BEEF &amp; PORK SAUSAGES.</p>
        <p>These will to found to agree with those that find all Pork too rich.</p>
        <p>Everyone should try these EXCELLENT SAUSAGES (both kinds), to ascertain which are most suitable for them. ALSO HOME-MADE Pc K K PIES AND BRAWN • CHINA 'TEAS. Tea el la Sir Andrew Clarke,' For years, whenever my Custon.ers have asked me for a Mild Tea, I have advised the Pure. old Fashioned China which I keep, and the advice has been apmeniated. It is very satisfactory to them that saelt an elainent authority as Sir Andrew Clarke, the London Physician, corroborates opinion. Here is an extract froni an interesting lecture, delivered by Sir Andrew the other day, to the Students of the London Hospital : "Teals a blessed beverage. , I dor, know what I should do without it. But there is tea and tea, and. one of the teas I have in my mind is the repreo-ntation of all that is physiologically wicked. Igo about town a good deal holding consulta- tions here and there, and about five o'ciock, when I get into a place, the lady of the house will say to me : 'Sir Andrew, you look tired, do let me give you r „flip of tea.' I say thank on very much. But the tea has stood for half-an-hour, and she remarks, ' I know you do you ms, i 3 strong, :lir Andrew. and she then pours about a tablespoonful of the tea into a cup and fills the cup up with wat, Now f call it positive cruelty to give tea like that to anybody. and I hope you, gentlemen, will always set your face ftgaikSt it.: Tea, to he useful, should be first of all Black China Tea ; the Indian Tea which is being cultivated has become so powertul lie its effects upon the nervous system that a cup of it taken early in the morning„ as many. do, so disorsl us ers the nervo system that those who take it actually get into a state of tea intoxication, and it produces a form of nervous cb fturbanee, which is most painful to witness. It you want to have, either for yourselves or for your patients, tea which will not injure and which will refresh, get Black China Tea, putting in the right measure—the old fashioned teaspooxful for ,telf person and one for the blessed pot. Then pour en briskly boiling water, and within five minutes you must pour it off ealn, or it will become wicked instead of good." Such is Sir Andrew'Clarke's opinion and advise. A few words of warning, however, are necessary to those who have for- gotten what this old fashioned tea is like. The colour of the tea after i. is brewed will be found toe much paler than that now in use, and the taste may not be acceptable at first owing to its apparent insipidness ; but slits use is persisted in, it will very soon be found to be much more suitable for the system, euil if, inn week to two after its use, a trial is again made of the kind now in ordinary use, you will wonder however you coul t have been using stand you will endorke Sir Andrew's statement. This old fashioned China by sir Andrew Clarke, can be obtained now, as always or the last 35 years, as Ilir • 031 AS MOLYNEU X 414401:41, • ' HOUSEHOLD STORES, • . • 37, 39, Si 41, MARKET -STREET, EARLESTOWN. • • •</p>
