      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>Tnz most lasting stuff for a silk gown.—A Chancery Suit. —Punch. MosT men have, like Achilles, a vulnerable spot; but it is in the head, not the heel. DicitsNs, in Bleak House, aptly designates pawnbrokers' duplicates as "turnpike tickets on the road to p6verty."</p>
        <p>AN Israelite witness being asked his Christian name, naively replied, "I have no Christian name! rm a Hebrew." _</p>
        <p>IF the earth take twenty-four hours to get round the sun, how many hours will it take for a son to get round an angry father? A PEDLAR indignantly demanding why a man cannot hawk goods without a licence in this land of liberty, was informed by the justice of the peace, that liberty is not a licence. A POOR Irishman offered an old saucepan for sale. His children gathered around him, and Inquired why he parted with it. "Ah, my honeys," answered he, "I would not be after parting with it, but for a little money to buy something to put in it."</p>
        <p>SAYING SOMETRINI Sorr.—A " foin " young gentleman, in turning swiftly on his heel, the other day, ran his head against a young lady. He put himself in a position to apologise. "Not a word," said the quick-witted beauty; "it isn't hard enough to hurt anybody." VERY PROPER DIET FOR TUTS HOT WEATHER. —Mrs. Turtledove: Dearest Alfred ! Will you decide now what we shall have for dinner 2—Mr. Turtledove: Let me see, Poppet. We had a wafer yesterday. Suppose we have a roast butter- fly to-day. —Punch. A CONTEMPORARY, opposed to the use of tobacco says :—" The women ought to make a pledge not to kiss a man who uses tobacco, and it would soon break up the practice." It would be broken up much sooner were the ladies to pledge themselves to kiss every man who does not use it. AN Irish nobleman, who had expended a large</p>
        <p>fortune in (as he said) the cause of his country, when dying summoned his heir to his bedside, and told him he had a secret to communicate which might prove some compensation for the dilapidated condition of the family property. It was—that crab sauce is better than lobster sauce.</p>
        <p>"MY tenants are a world of bother to me," said a testy landlady to her nephew.—" Quite likely, ten aunts might be considered enough to bother anyone," was the reply. A GENTLEMAN who takes in the morning journals, and whose attention is apt to be monopolised by them, remonstrated one day with his wife for coming down to breakfast in curl-papers; when the lady replied, "If you indulge in your papers, I don't see why I shouldn't enjoy mine I"—Punch. AN Irishman observing a dandy taking his usual strut on the Fort promenade at Margate, stepped up to him and inquired, "What rent do you ask for these houses ?"—" Why do you ask me such a ques- tion?" said he.—" FAith, and I thought the whole terrace belonged to ye," replied the Irishman. A CANINE DILEMMA.-" What dogs are these, Sandy ? " inquired a gentleman the other day, of a lad who was dragging a couple of wasp ,ish-look- ing terriers along a street in Edinburgh. I dinna ken, sir," replied the urchin, "they cam' wi' the railway, and they ate the direction, and dinna ken whar to ging." -</p>
        <p>A PROMISING young solicitor of Carey Street intends going to Prussia, to be present at the experiments that are going on there with the needle gun and the Minie rifles. He thinks this is the best way of passing the long vacation, as he may be able to derive from it a few notions as to sharp practice. —Punch. "It's a wonderful place, is Australia," said a South Shields glassman the other day to a friend; "it's thirteen times as big as Europe."—" How can that be, man," replied the other, "since Europe's one of the quarters of the globe?"—" I'll tell thee how It is ; it wasn't discovered till after the globe was quartered, and so it's made a fifth quarter." POPULAR IGNORANCE. —At a recent missionary meeting in Nottingham, a Mr. W. Allen, of Sheffield, told a story of a woman in the latter town wto lately astoniblied a neighbour with the</p>
        <p>intelligence that the devil was aced ! They at length reached a dyer's shop, in the window of which was a placard with the inscription—" Satin dyed here I " A YOUNG and beautiful, but poor widow, was about to be married to a rich old widower. Her friends wished to know what she wished to marry him for? She replied, "Pure Love; I love the ground (meaning farm, probably) on which he walks, and the very house in which he lives." There is platonic love for you I There is none of your school-girl romance in that. PROSY old gentlemen think that James Watt</p>
        <p>was a greater genius than Shakespeare, and that the only motive which should move society is the locomotive. It was one of these who once said of Milton's Paradise Lost, that it "was a very good book, but it did'nt prove anything." Practical men are useful, but they are shockingly utilitarian ; and, like a tailor, see much more beauty in a lot of " cabbage " than in all the rose-bushes that ever grew.</p>
        <p>ELECTIONEERING HARMONY.—Bands of music, consisting of drums and trumpets, we are informed by the newspapers, paraded sundry towns at the late elections. Where this was not exactly the case, the trumpet was generally blown on the hustings by the proprietor of that instrument, and the drum was for the most part also very audible; not the kettle-drum, nor the big drum, but the hum-drum, wherewith the various candidates regaled the ears of the constituencies to an unpre- cedented extent. —Punch. WHEN Viscount B. was put in nomination for the county of Somerset, there was on the day of nomination a great uproar in the crowd, in which an Irishman, evidently newly imported, was tak- ing an active part. A gentleman on the hustings, thinking to put him down, called out, "Go to the d—l, Paddy." Paddy, looking up, with great good-humour and a nod of his head, replied, "And much good luck to yer honour; I have been six months in this country, and you are the first gentleman that has invited me to see his father." MATRIMONIAL.—Emily M. Bennet, whose hus- band posted her in the newspapers, and then had the impudence to run away with another woman, returns the compliment in the following spirited manner, through the columns of the Woonsocket paper :—" As no one would have trusted him for as much as a peck of meal, I therefore forbid any person or persons trusting him on my account, as I shall pay no debts of his contracting after this date: I have worked to support him in gambling and laziness as long as I mean to, although, should he die soon, I will pay the funeral expenses with pleasure." ADVICE TO GIRLS —Girls, do you want to get married, and do you want good husbands? If so cease to act litre fools. Don't take pride in say- ing you never did housework—never cooked a pair of chickens—never made a bed—and so on. Don't turn up your pretty noses at honest industry— never tell your friend that you are not obliged to work. When you go a shopping, never take your mother with you to "carry the bundle." "JACK," newly off a voyage, and elevated with a glass of grog, is a queer animal. One of this class was passenger the other day in a railway carriage between Greenock and Port Glasgow, in which was a clergyman. Jack was not scrupulous in his phraseology, and the clergyman in a solemn tone, expressed his fear that the young man was on Abe road to the d--l. " Well, it don't matter much," replied Jack, "I have got a return ticket." The Ma.ilachusats Spy shows how the Yankee amazed the Indian :—" The water at Mackinaw is very clear and cold, so cold as to be almost unendur- able. A gentleman lately amused himself by throwing a small gold coin in twenty feet of water, and offering it to any Indian who would bring it up. Down they plunged, but upon descending ten or twelve feet, they came up so chilled, that after i?evearl attempts they gave it up. A Yankee stand- ing by, observed that 'if he would give it to him for getting it, he'd swing it up quicker than lightning; to which he consented; when Jonathan, instead of plunging in as was expected, quietly took up a setting-pole and dipped the end in a tar barrel, , reached it down to the coin and brought it up, and , slipping it into his pocket walked off, to the am I went of the Indian divers, and the no small chagrazia of the donor."</p>
        <p>PUBLIC NOTICE.—Not one but BUNDREBS of people f both sexes with wrecked and debilitated constitutions ar% yearly restored to sound and robust health again by Messrs. Wilkinson. 3 and 4, Fitzallan Square, Sheffield. Write to them for advice free when suffering from weakness, loss of flesh, appetite, disturbed rest. Pimples, blotches, indiges tion, pallid skin. nervousness, and general debility. posi. live care for all Blood and Skin Diseases, acquired or consti- tutional. Sixty years' experience. One address (as above) for over 30 years. The " A 1 Mixture," 4s. 9d. the case. The Magic Mirror in envelope, four stamps. Sufferers disap- pointed with Electric Belts should write to us at once.-44 t</p>
      </div>
