      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>" CAPITA_L " CHAT.</head>
        <p>" CAPITA_L " CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Marchioness of Bute, who, it is well known, spends a large sum of money yearly among the Catholic poor, is now extending her charitable work in many directions. Her latest project is to estab- lish a residence for trained nurses, who are to attend to the want% of the sick Catholic poor in their own homes in London. The Marchioness has purchased a house in Holland-st. for this purpose, which will undergo a complete transformation in the builders' hands. Another extension will be,the erection of a Catholic chapel at Eastwell Park, Nshford, the for- mer residence of the Duke and Duchess of Edin- burgh. Lord Gerard has purchased the estate for .E 220,000 and will spend another £lOO,OOO on altera- tions and improvements. It is said that Cardinal Vaughan will undertake the ceremony of consecrat- ing the chapel.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Dr. R. R. Kane, of Belfast. or, as he is known throughout Ireland, "Rev. Roaring Bane," is regarded even by his own people as the most bigoted parish clergyman in the Church of Ireland. As Grand Master of the Belfast Orange Society, his name is known throughout the Empire. His aggressive spirit as a politician first displayed itself in 1881, at the time the Land League agitation was in full awing, when he raised the cry of "No surrender." As a speaker he is very powerful, and in appearance is not unlike his friend the member for West Birmingham. In social life he is credited with many good qualities.</p>
        <p>Lady Jeune gave a second crowded reception in Harley-st., on Saturday night, the chief feature of which was the strength of the Opposition, who were headed by Lord Salisbury, Lord Randolph Churchill Mr. Joseph Chamberlain, and Sir Edward Clarke. The Marchioness ot Salisbury, the Dowager Coun- tess of Westbury and Miss Nevill represented the lady official element of the Primrose League. The numerous diplomatists present were headed by their doyen Rustem Pasha in his red fez, the French Ambassador, and the Belgian Minister. Among the many beauties visible were Miss Morris (daughter of Lord Morris), Lady Griffin. Mrs. Robert Webster, Mrs. Richard Chamberlain, Miss Joliffe, Mrs. Frede- rick Beer, Lady Londonderry, and many others. Among other distinguished people present were Julia Marchioness of Tweeddale, the Dowager Countess of Airlie, Lady Jane Taylor, Mr. W. Astor (of New York), Sir Eyre Shaw, Sir George Baden- Powell, the Hon. Helen Henniker, and Sir Richard Paget.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. S. Caine's daughter, Miss Hannah Caine,is to be married shortly to Mr. John Herbert Roberts, M.P. for the Western division of Denbighshire. Miss Caine took part recently in the beautitul clas- sical and ronaantic tableaux vivants at the Clapham School of Art, where she has been a student for some time. Mr. Roberts,wbo is barely thirty years of age, is the son of Mr. John Roberts, who was at one time member for the Flint disgict, and was educated at Trinity College, Cambri,Pge. His busi- ness is that of a timber merchant. In 1884 and 188.5 he visited India and our colonies, and pub- ished a volume of travels ealled "A World's Tour." As a politician he is an out-and-out Liberal and Home Ruler.</p>
        <p>Mr. John W. Mackay, the "Bonanza King," who has narrowly escaped death at San Francisco, has had as many "escapes' as Othello boasted of, though Mr. Mackay's deliverances have been from the perils of the land. In the old days of the Bonanza Silver Mines, Mackay led what seemed a charmed life. He always refused even then to carry a revolver, nor has he ever yielded to the per- suasions of his friends that a "seven-shooter" should be included in his ordinary daily accoutre- ment at San Francisco. He has always replied to such suggestions," My stick will do for four-legged dogs and my fist for two-legged ones." In his memorable affray in the managerial apartment of an American bank with Mr. J. M. Bonynge, his dearest foe, he showed that he had attained almost pugilistic perfection at fisticuffs. The two men fought until the furniture was in fragments, and the bank was, in one sense at least, "wrecked." Mr. Mackay spends most of his time at San Francisco, piling up his millions, though he comes to Mrs. Mackay at Carlton House-terrace at regular intervals.</p>
        <p>Sir Andrew Barclay Walker, who died on Monday was supposed to be the wealthiest man in Liverpool. Ile started business in a small way, managing one of his rather's public-houses, 1,-,t soon displayed extraordinary business capacity, ‘nd built up a brewing business second only to those of the Guinnesses and the Basses. When, two years ago, the breweries in Liverpool and Warrington were turned into a limited liability company, the capital was three millions of money. Sir Andrew was a generous man. He founded an art gallery in Liverpool at a cost af £40,000, and a few years ago spent £25,000 more in connection with Victoria College.</p>
        <p>The deceased baronet was a middle-sized, trim- looking man, with white moustache and side whiskers. His home was at Gateacre Grange, near Liverpool, but he had another superb seat at Osmas- ton Manor, in Derbyshire, for which he outbade the Australian Crossus—Sir Samuel Wilson—before that light of the colonies settled down at II ughenden. The price paid for Osinaston was £206,000; and here his son,the new bachelor-baronet,made his home under the shade of the smoke tower 150 ft. high, that stands in the garden. Sir Andrew had a yacht, the Cuhona, on which he spent .£50,000. It was a dream of luxury.</p>
        <p>Brewing baronets stud Burke and Debrett. The first of them was Lacon, of great Yarmouth, to whom followed Meux, of Theobald's-rd., and Buxton of Bellfield. Of living brewers who became baronets the first are Sir Benjamin Guinness, now Lord Ardilaun, and Sir Dudley Coutts Marjoribanks of Meux's brewery, now Lord Tweedrcouth. Sir Gilbert Greenall, another Lancashire beer-king. became a baronet in 1876. Sir Henry Allsopp, now Lord Hindlip, got his first step in 1880. Mr. Bass became Sir Michael in 1882, and Lord Burton in 1886. Sir Edward Guinness, now Lord Iveagh, was also a baronet, and, in addition to the late Sir Andrew Walker, the late Tory Government made the head of the Kinahan whisky firm a baronet, and did the same last year for Mr. Seeger Hunt.</p>
        <p>What is involved in the removal of the Court,evan from London to Windsor after but a few hours in the capital, was shown to her Majesty's faithful subjects assembled in Hyde Park on Monday. First there came two large vans containing the baggage ; after an interval of precisely fifteen minutes, there was a whiff amongst the mounted police, and a string of four large carriages rolled by with the ladies and gentlemen of the Courti the private secretaries, major-domos, maids of honour, and personal attendants, the latter of whom included two dusky, turbaned personages, who represented the British Empire beyond the seas. Fifteen minutes later there rumbled by a single bag- gage van, with a man in scarlet inside and a boy outside. This, it was suggested, was Queen Vic- toria's own box. Finally, fifteen minutes afterwards the brass helmets of an escort of Life Guards were descried approaching at a smart trot. Then came a closed carriage, protected by more Life Guards. In this carriage sat her Majesty. During the stay of the Queen and her family and suite at Buckingham Palace, her Majesty is her own private suite of apartments, gon the north side of the palace, and looks wotochwcivacprhdYiisPniccadilly. over the Greenpark. The Em- press Frederick is occupying the Belgian suite, which is on the west side of the Palace, and looks out on the garden: Princess Beatrice and her husband are lodged in the suite specially reserved for them, which is in the east front of the palace over the State apartments, and looks out on the Mall. Sir John Cowell (Master of the Household) is staying in his official suite, which is in the south East corner of the palace. and has a. special private entrance. Sir Henry .Ponsoby (Private Secretary) is not lodged in Buckingham Palace at all, but is staying at the house in St. James,s Palace, which the Queen has granted him for life.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Manchester and Salford Trades Council held oR Tuesday evening it was resolved to hold a labour demonstration in that city on the first Sunday in May. _ _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>MEDICAL.</head>
        <p>MEDICAL.</p>
        <p>A CARD. p EVER EN D JOSEF El 110 L MES' improved -Rib Mexican Prescriptian, as discovered in the het table- lands of Old Mexico, is a guaranteed cure to all those wh !Allier from the errors and indiscretions of youth, nekvous weakness, physical exhaustion, and early decay. To intro- duce it into England, the Mexican Prescription will be sent free of charge, upon receipt ef a self addressed stamped envelope. Only address: JOSEPH HOLMES' Remedy Co.. Bloomsbury Mansions, Bloomsbury-square, London, ,W.C, Mention this paper.</p>
