      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 4, 1893. LADIES' LETTER. DARIO/VC:IOD.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 4, 1893. LADIES' LETTER. DARIO/VC:IOD.</p>
        <p>MY DRAB NELLIE,</p>
        <p>Although small bonnet shapes are still retained, hats are growing larger. These are now being made of coloured straws, with wide flat brims and high crowns. The poke bonnet is also brought before our notice. This is in a medium size with an ample brim, as you know, and sets out well over a pretty face. A pretty example of an empire bonnet was composed of chestnut-brown velvet, lined underneath with black velvet, and edged with mink. Three black plumes was fastened in with a paste buckle at one side. If carried out in "penitential violet" and black velvet, it would make a becoming head-gear for an elderly lady.</p>
        <p>The new ideas for the spring already begin to disclose themselves in certain quarters. There is -nothing more certain than that dressy English- women will not adopt anything new simply because it is the rage in Paris. Individualism has done so mach for our dress that we are emancipated in this matter, and will wear only what suits us per- sonally. The wide skirt is, of course, an accom- plished fact, but it is a garment very far in advance of anything our ancestors knew. It is a marvel of cut, and is, when cleverly built, a pretty and graceful thing. The newest are double and treble. 'Utley are cut in such a way that no seam is seen. They are put on quite lit, and the fulness is all in epriug outward attained entirely by skill in out. They necessitate, of course, the use of a founda- tion.</p>
        <p>a particularly effective toilette is made of plain summer cloth trimmed with braiding, for which gimp or a band of velvet or silk may be substituted, and beige or cashmere are suitable materials. A slightly trained umbrella skirt is trimmed at the fret with a narrow flounce headed with braiding. The skirt is fitted to the figure in front with pleats at the waist, while the back is gathered at the top. The bodice is fastened wit buttons in the centre of the front, and is trimmed here and at the back with braiding at the neck, tapering to a point at the bust. The short basque is pointed both back and front in a bow at the back. A high collar finishes the neck. The sleeves are braided at the wrist and finished with a deep puff from the shoulder to the elbow.</p>
        <p>There is every likelihood just now that real lace is returning to favour after a long eclipse. Toe Parisian leaders have set this movement going, and all the best dressmakers are in favour of the re• vival. Point lace lappets are to be worn at the Queen's Drawing Rooms this season, and preoata times are beicg made for an adequate supply ot real lace ones, both modern and antique, Lace will be very largely used on mantles and capes for spring wear, as well as for the trimmings of sleeves and bodices, and many of the beautiful imitations in which such perfection has been attained of late will be utilised for this purpose. One of the very newest of evening gowns has a lace frill round flog edge, falling below the dress itself. Whether this curious innovation is likely to endure is open te some doubt. It is safe to say, however, that the. lace is in a very inconvenient position; and one that is fraught with danger to its own delicate meshes.</p>
        <p>A very elegant and tasteful bodice is made ol cashmere trimmed with gimp, while nun's cloth oi merveilleux silk is also suitable, and the zouave may, if preferred, be of contrasting goods. The bodice is gathered at the neck and waist over a close-fitting foundation, and is fastened with hooke at the back. A ribbon sash tied in a knot at the back and a bow in front conceals where the bodice and skirt meet. The neck is corded round. The zmave is pointed at the back and round in front, with wide revers at the utck, and is trimmed entirely round with gimp. The full puff elbow sleeves are gathered at the armhole over a plain foundation, and also just above the elbow, where they are finished with a band.</p>
        <p>As spring develops, it is probable that green, yellow, and pale bright shades of lilac will quite replace the more rich and imposing but deeper tints of the present. Full skirted coats are rendered a necessity by the width of the dress skirts now worn. A coat of velvet in a deep rich shade of purple was recently worn over a dress of light drab fancy cloth. The skirt was trimmed with four quilled niches of the cloth; the coat was untrimmed save for a folded collar and jobot of black satin. A wide-brimmed black chip hat was worn, with rosettes of pale purple satin at the back and front, holding ends of a spray of wisteria, which fell to either side over the crown. The feature of the costume was the width of skirt shown in both dress and coat. The latter was quite tight- fitting save in the skirt, and the sleeves, though full, were yet of modest proportions.</p>
        <p>Yours sincerely,</p>
        <p>JENNIE.</p>
        <p>Brocade velvets are seen in scroll, running vines, and flower patterns, coming in two shades of the same colour, and are to be used in .combination with silks of all kinds.</p>
        <p>Box plaits appear upon some of the newest dress skirts, and some of the plaits show at the back only, others in front, and on still others they form a Watteau fold that reaches from the neck to the hem in the back.</p>
        <p>Now is the time when the prudent woman makes her preparations for spring work. Notwithstanding all of the abuse to which the bargain counter and its frequen- ters have been subjected, it remains just the same and is mote and more visited by sensible women with every passing season. Now is the time when the judicious mother buys and fits up all of the pretty dainty summer plumage for her dear little birdlings. It is so much nicer to get things made up ahead, and as the new ging- hams, zephyrs, and cbambreys are coming in very rapidly, and there is no lack of current styles on hand, this is one of the wisest of moves. Lighter and brighter colours than usual are to be worn the coming season and also great quantities of lace.</p>
