      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>TALE OF LONEMOOR.</head>
        <p>A TALE OF LONEMOOR.</p>
        <p>BY MRS. HARRIETT LEWIS, Author of "The Sundered Hearts," "The Bailiff's; Scheme," " The Double Life," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XIV. A CHANGE OF FORTUNE.</p>
        <p>We must now introduce to the readers a person whose connections with Gwen and her fortunes will become apparent in the course of our narrative. The time was morning, a week or so before Mr. Quillet's resolve to put Mr. Chilton's regard for Gwen to the proof. In his chambers in London a man was seated at a table, with pencil and paper, summing up a formid- able array of figures. He was Captain Fabian Tollish, of the army. He was forty years of age, tall and stout, inclin- ing to obesity, with a round full face, closely shaven, a low and narrow forehead, small, bright, oeering eyes of a steely colour, and hay-coloured hair. He was big, round, and oily, slee and secre- tive, with a massive under jaw, expressive of a cruel ' and relentless disposition. He was a man who did not form a purpose lightly ; but when he did form one he was 1i...e1y to carry it to its fulfilment, whatever obstacles stood in his way. No scruples of conscience ever troubled him. Airy and graceful in his manners in st)ciety, capable of throwing over his real character a cloak of apparent frankness and integrity, he had laid his mask aside in his solitude, and was just now moody and scowling, a decided ugly exprersion dis- figuring his visage. "The sum total is appalling," he muttered. "Two thousand pounds los, on that confounded Harefoot at the last Derby—two thousand pounds ; the note I gave for it fully matured to day—and I hav'n't fifty pounds in the world I These losses are debts of honour. If I don't pay the bill when it is pre- sented to-day I shall be hooted out of society. Garsfield has written me that a renewal is impossible. There's not a money lender in England that would advance me a farthing—l have no security to offer. Two thousand pounds ! I can't pay it, and I shall have to get out of England at once. My reputation will follow me. Our mess will be sure to cut use. I shall be forced into sellinz my commission and retiring into obscurity. Ten thousand curses on the luck r _ _</p>
        <p>He touched a small callbell which stood on the table, His valet—a smooth, stealthy, darkfaced Maltese —appeared from an inner room. Pietro had been in Captain Tollish's service for more than twenty years. The two were upon the most confidential terms. They were congenial souls, and the master in many a nefarious piece of work had found ready and willing assistance in his man. "Pietro," said the captain, "you can pack my boxes. We set out for Malta this evening.' . .</p>
        <p>"Yes, Signor Capitano," replied the servant, who liked England, and did not like to leave it; "but the leave of absence is not expired—" "Bother the leave of absence l" ejaculated Cap- tain Tollish, with an oath. "It's all up with me, Pietro. Those confounded Derby bills have matured, and I've no money to pay them. We must get out of the country, though I hate Malta and the dingy old barracks, and I wish I might never see them again." Pietro understood the situation.</p>
        <p>"If we must go, we must I" he sighed. "But you'll see trouble out of this, signor Capitano. You'll have to sell out, and then what will you do? Why don't you apply to your rich cousin, my Lord Dark wood ? He might help you out to save the family credit." "He might," responded Captain Tollish, moodily; "but he's not in England. The truth is, I wrote to him at Dunholm Castle a week ago about this very matter, asking assistance; but not getting an answer I called upon his lawyer, Mr. Sutton, yester- day, in the city, and there I learned that he's cruis- the Mediterranean in his own yacht, the Sylphide. He's enjoying himself while I am on the verge of ruin. The first time he lands at a civilized port he'll probably read in some English newspaper of my ruin."</p>
        <p>"You've done my lord more than one nasty turn, Signor Capitano, which no one knows but you and I, and which if he knew he'd kill you," observed the valet; "but as he don't know you might possibly get the money out of him. Couldn't you hunt him up ?" "Impossible. Pack the boxes, Pietro. First Malta—then ruin."</p>
        <p>The captain spoke moodily. His prospects were decidedly gloomy at that moment. . . .</p>
        <p>, Pietro set to work packing the boxes. The captain lighted a cigar and puffed it gloom- ily. "I wish I was quit of the old life," said Captain Tollish, after a long silence. "I don't like my Malta entanglement. If I had the chance I'd cut lose from it on the instant. I've done some evil work, Pietro, as you have just intimated, but I can't see that it has benefited me any as yet. If my love— the only love I ever felt for any woman—had prospered I should have been a very different man. If Clare Markham had lived, and if she had loved me as I loved her, I should have been But away with vain regrets—she's dead. You saw her grave, Pietro 2" "Yes," answered the valet, "she is buried in a corner of the churchyard at Penistone, in little better than a pauper's grave. There's a stone at the head of her grave, with one werd 'Magdalen,' upon it." "And they dared out that word on her tomb- stone ?" he exclaimed. "She a Magdalen l' Clare Markham was tho noblest, purest, best of women. Don't I know? And what a fate was hers! I'm not superstitious, Pietro, but sometimes in cold winter nights I think of her as she spent the last night of her life wandering in the snow on the pathless moor, and I believe in my soul that I shall suffer some awful judgment from the hand I had in her death."</p>
        <p>He shivered, and a strange apprehensive look gleamed for a moment in his steely eyes. "You are sure that grave in Penistone churchyard is hers, Pietro 2" he asked presently. "Oh, yes, Signor Capitano. It was nearly seven- teen years ago, I remember, that she disappeared," answered Pietro, reflectively. "She fled, and you searched for her everywhere. It was not until the following spring that it occurred to you that she might have gone to her father. Not wishing to be seen in that vicinity, you sent me to Yorkshire to inquire out secretly if she were there. It was then May. I inquired cautiously in Penistone. And then I heard that in the preceding November a young woman, who was crazy, appeared one stormy night at Lonemoor, was taken in, and gave birth to a child. She was supposed to be a vagrant. No one areamed of her real identity. Miss Markham is believed to have died in Berlin, and to be buried there. No one, unless it were her father or her nurse, even suspected that that vagrant creature was the once proud Miss Markham." "Yet that vagrant was Clare. I know and feel it." muttered Captain Tollish. "You drove Miss Markham crazy. The girl who came there was crazy. She never spoke an intelli- gible word while at Lonemoor. The doctor who attended her told rile. And one awful night. when she had been .there a month, she fled, was lost in the storm, and perished miserably. The old butler of the house had her buried, and put up that stone to her memory." "With the name Magdalen' upon it. That proves that he knew her. And for nearly seven- teen years she has been lying in that dishonoured grave, her father a wanderer upon the face of the earth. The child—Pietro, the child must be nearly a woman now," exclaimed the captain, in a voice that was suddenly startled. "She'll be seventeen in November." "I'd like to see her," said Captain Tollish, musingly. "A woman Brought up by servants —a servant herself, without doubt—a mere clod. Yet I'd like to see her, Pietro. I've a mind to run don to Yorkshire and see her before we start for Malta. I've never seen her in all my life. Of course, I should'nt betray any interest in her, yet I'd like to look upon her face." He was considering the subject:seriously, when a knock, loud, abrupt, and commanding, sounded upon the door. "The officers l" gasped Pietro, in a panic. "It's the bills, capitano 1" Captain Tollish made a movement to retreat into tll6- inner room, his one thought being of flight; but before he had taken three steps the door swung open, and a grey-haired old man, with keen and kindly visage, stepped into the chamber. He was Mr. Sutton, Lord Darkwood's family law- yer. Captain Tollish paused in the act of flight. , There was something strange in the old lawyer's manner. He looked like one who staggers under a blow. He took off his hat, and the captain noticed that a piece of crape had been newly fast- ened around it. "You seem to be in trouble, Mr. Sutton," said Captain Tollish. "What is the matter? Have you lost a friend I" "Yes, sir, the best and truest friend man ever had," said the old lawyer; " the noblest, grandest Foul ever created r</p>
        <p>;".9;</p>
        <p>"Not your wife, I hope, sir r "Not my wife, thank heaven, but one wno came next to my own family," said Mr. Sutton. "I have but just received the news, my lord. A tele- gram—" "My Lord l" echoed Captain Tollish, growing white.</p>
        <p>Pietro leaped to his feet. "Yes, my lord" said Mr. Sutton, his features quivering. "Lord Darkwood is dead—drowned in the Mediterranean—in the wreck of the Syl- phide 1" "Lord Darkirood dead Impossible!" "He is dead—in his prime—in the vigour of his manhood. And you, my lord, being his next heir, are now Lord Darkwood !"</p>
        <p>Captain Tollish sat down. "And master of Dunholm Castle and other estates, and one of the richest peers in England!" cried Pietro. "My lord marquis, I congratulate you I" "Master of Dunholm Castle !" repeated Captain Tollish. "One of the richest peers in England! I can hardly understand it. Sit down, Mr. Sutton, and tell me all about it. Who telegraphed to you?" _</p>
        <p>"The master of the yacht. He alone was saved. The Sylphide was wrecked on the coast of Sardinia, and all but the captain were lost 1" The lawyer sat down, still weak with the blow that had come upon him, and covered his face with his hands. Captain Tollish covered his face also, and well he might, for his face was the face of an exulting demon!</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XV ' THE NEWS CONFIRMED.</p>
        <p>The news of the death of Lord Darkwood came apon Captain Tollish like a great burst of light to one who had been wandering in great darkness. Years berore he had counted upon the death ot the marquis, and his own succession to the Darkwood wealth and grandeurs ; but of late years he had given little thought to such a possibility save in unusually speculative moments. And now, in the hour of his greatest need, when he had stood upon the very verge of ruin, he had risen at one swift and iighty bound from his low estate to the position oflbne of the wealthiest and mightiest peers of the realm. He was staggered by his good fortune. He could scarcely believe in it. Lord Darkwood had•been two years younger than himself, with a more vigorous constitution, and with habits of life more conducive to longevity than those he practised. He might. have been expected to live many years, to marry, and to leave an heir to his title and estates.</p>
        <p>But he was dead, leaving no obstacle between Captain Tollish and all this noble inheritance. He was dead, and Captain Tollish was Lord Darkwood in his stead.</p>
        <p>A realisation of the truth came but slowly to the captain's dazed brain. Pietro was much quicker to comprehend the full import of the news. He sprang up like a Jack-in- the-box and gave a kick at the shabby old portman- teau he had deen engaged in packing, his long slender legs, like exaggerated black candles, dis- playing an unexpected agility in the operation. A new order of affairs was about to be instituted. Captain Tollish's old effects would hardly serve Lord Darkwood.</p>
        <p>"It seems like a dream," muttered the new lord, "and I fear that I shall awaken from it. Let me see your telegram, Ma Sutton." The lawyer extended the slip of paper in silence. The new Lord Darkwood seized it eagerly, and read it burning eyes. It was dated Maud°, Corsica, and read as follows :</p>
        <p>"Yacht Sylphide, lost off south coast of Sardinia, in storm on night of the 3rd. All on board lost except myself. Lord Darkwood drowned with the rest. Am on my way to England." This message was signed with the name of the master of the yacht—Richard H. Foster. "There can't be any mistake—Lord Darkwood is surely drowned," said Captain Tollish. "I , shall wait here in London for Foster's appearance." "I have no hope—no hope whatever," said the lawyer, sorrowfully. If Lord Darkwood had sur- vived the wreck, he would have telegraphed Ing immediately to forestall any report of his death. I would go tollardinia but that Foster will soon be here, and bb can give me more information than I should be able to obtain there."</p>
        <p>The new marquis began to swell with a growing sense of impqrtance. "I will order my mourning this very day," he said. "As you say, there's no hope.. My poor cousin is dead. I know how he valued your service and coun- sel, Mr. Button, and I shall avail myself of them. I hope that You will continue to study the interests of the Darkwood estates and their owner as heretofore. I shall look upon you, not only as a faithful counsellor, but as a friend."</p>
        <p>The ma;quis arose and extended his hand. ThE lawyer arose also and put out his hand, said a few final words and departed, muttering, as he went downstairs— "Sad times have come for Dunholm when a schem- ing scoundrel like Captain Tollish becomes its master. A noble soul has gone hence to make room for an igunble one. A great nature has vanished, and a mean and plotting one has taken its place. Ali! if my lord had only left a child to succeed him." -</p>
        <p>Pietro closed the door after the departing lawyer, and master and man gave expression to their great exultation at their good fortune, for the good for- tune of Captain Tollish included the good fortune of Pietro. The man knew-too many of his master's secrets to be fearful of a discharge. "It wouldn't look just the thing for me to go out, Pietro," observed Lord Dark wood, with a proper regard for appearances. "Go to Poole's and tell them to send a man to take my measures and orders. I shall have a well-fitting coat after many bad ones, I hope. And, Pietro, as soon as I put on my mourning you are welcome to my present posses- sions."</p>
        <p>The valet hurried out upon his errand. Tradesmen followed each other in rapid succes- sion throughout that day into the presence of the new Lord Darkwood. Tailor, shoemaker, draper, jeweller, and others came and went. The marquis gave orders lavishly. Unwonted luxuries appeared upon the tables and in his dressing room. He dark. ened his windows, and assumed a semblance of deepest mourning, but all the while his heart was rejoicing in the wildest exultation. The next morning several newspapers contained the notice of Lord Darkwood's death, and the new marquis was overwhelmed with calls and congratu- lations from hosts of former friends who had found it convenient to completely ignore his exis- tence of late years. For two or three days he maintained the strict- est seclusion, not leaving his chambers; but when his new garments came home he issued forth in the most irreproachable mourning garb, with a count- enance toned down to a proper expression of grief, and was seen of men in all his new dignity and honours.</p>
        <p>A week passed, and he had grown very anxious and irritable, when Captain Foster of the lost yacht Sylphide, appeared in London. Captain Foster went first of all to Mr. Sutton, and the two came together to call upon Lord Dark- wood.</p>
        <p>The sailor's story was brief, but its accuracy could not be doubted.</p>
        <p>He was a bluff, honest fellow, who had been deeply and sincerely attached to his late employer. He had been in the service of Lord Darkwood for many years, the marquis having been devoted to yachting. His story was told with all the simplicity of a sailor, and with equal straightforwardness. He said that the Sylphide had been cruising the Med- iterranean for several months, that Lord Darkwood had visited Greece, had spent a week in Algiers, and was on his return to England when the yacht encountered a great gale upon the night of the third of September, when off the southern coast of Sardinia. A sudden blow had snapped the main- mast short off like a pipe-stem. "And then, while the deck was encumbered with delnais, while all hands were in a banie, a quicker, sharper blast—a sudden fury of tl% elements—had driven tlie yacht over on her beam ends. The sailors had caught at any object to float them, but Thad been swept into the inky yeast of waters. Lord Darkwood had clutched at a broken piece of mast, and had been carried overboard. Captain Foster had seen this in the very moment when he, grasping at a chair that chanced to be on deck, was swelot overboard also.</p>
        <p>Captain Foster had managed to keep himself afloat for several hours with the aid of the chair he had providentially seized, and had been picked up the next morning about daybreak by a fishing smack belonging to Corsicans.</p>
        <p>He had made search for his late companions, and had only too well assured himself that they were drowned.</p>
        <p>The remnant of the Sylphide had been cast ashore at Cape Teulada, on the Sardinian coast, as also had several dead bodies.</p>
        <p>Lord Darkwood's, the mate's, and two others had not been among them. Captain Foster had cruised abou:. the vicinity for tyro or three days; then finding their search</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <p>absolutely vain, ancl hope being no longer able, he he had set out upon his return to with the the mournful tidings. Mr. Sutton and the new marquis made Ina% inquiries of Captain Foster. Ile answered all in the most straightforward manner. Th_e:,7o could be no possiblb doubt of Lord Dar death. And so the last vestige of hope wbicb Sutton had unwraciously cherished died out of Jai'</p>
        <p>mind. The new Lord Darkwood breathed more freell when his visitors were gone. "I was afraid that there'd be some doubt all uncertainty about my cousin's death," he retearkFs`,l, "By Jove, Pietro, I couldn't stand the merielilig return now, you know. l've had a taste grandeurs, and I wouldut give them up, net evell.s if he stood here in the flesh before rae at moment." cs/e And the ugly expression of his visage double effect to his words. Pietro laughed softly. "My lord is dead, safe enough," be declared,: "When all those sailors perished he wasn't spareeS He is out of the way, and there are no faugaa expenses even to pay," and ne laugned " Shall you stay in town longer ?" "Not I. I've lived in barracks for years, rgieotrfc: Now Pll try a home of my MIL I ~ Nu•••• t to Dunholm Castle to-morrow. I ara examine my rentroll, to see ray farms and reS antry, to wander through my park, te.„,,riagent,too horses, to enjoy my new possession&amp; will go go down with me." eonf ' "My salary, of course, is increased observed Pietro, sulkily. —rood "Yes, that goes without saying. You have me faithfully, Pietro, and you'll not find MO aeei;„ I ful. All my dirty work may not be done Yz.," d a notice that when a man has once done that 1...'48.0 thing he is likely to have more of it on his 1',,8r,„d of Pietro smiled, showing his teeth. An,' 'if-Lord work well paid for was acceptable to his, an,,7,01 be Darkwood was a perfect mine of wealth, ssr"" meant to work at for his leisure. "We were speaking about that'girl the other dais you know, signore," he said; "Miss garighalla child. Shall you go and see her 7" o "I think not "said the marquis, careleBsl9; changed my mind. I don't care to see her. t, nangi "She may be poor," suggested the servant in her grandfather's house. le- ",tie immensely rich. You can well afford to se" small income upon her." • and " But in doing so, I should aro inquiry. No, she can go onasussleie's sti bespeicil lO i said his lordship. "I'll leave her to hersec.. she'll marry a footman, or a groom, or pos...lnytes farmer's son, and will live and die contented la very ignorance." istea "Then you won't do anything for her 7" Pesr the valet, gal° net "Nothing. I'll not go ,there. I'll ws after her. Why, she may Ile dead! Who kne We'll let her fate remain a mystery- I don't to solve it!" A Odd° The valet's face was turned away. Dot gleam appeared in his eyes, but his roaster notice it. If the new lord was a schemer, s° was the Maltese. And in that moment he to ascertain for himself the fate of Clare garYjTife child. The knowledge might prove use te thereafter, and greatly advance his fortnnee'f the might as well secure for himself a portion o Darkwood wealth to himself as an independent tune? iv and He resolved to give the matter his cal.'" earnest attention. ; "I shall go to Dnnholm," said the new lord, "1 • shall live In splendour; I shall fill the Tor. with guests; I shall by and by make a o°l, I'll riage. It's lucky for me that my wife is deem- eer, make a grand match next time, Pietro. I'm a Po, and I shall ally myself to some noble facall' a grand life opens before me I" ta r Shall you bring the signorina home irons gal inquired the valet. eet• The marquis made a quick, impatient inovela of The question evidently suggested some aearered annoyance which he had forgotten. He mat a curse, and walked to and fro hurriedly. ered' '" No I'll net bring her to England," he allsw eta "She'd be an incqmbrance and injure my prosPeher No, let her stay in Malta. I never want to see again." imed Pietro did not pursue the subject, but mak: himself in packing his master's new warar,o .e. the commodious boxes newly bought for the pe'L'i The next morning Lord Darkwood, accompai3O`; 1 Mr. Sutton, and attended by Pietro, set out 04 lordship's inheritance in Shropshire. As they journeyed northward by the mail tre'"„jat d valet was very busy with his own Plaa,a „;- of schemes, which were quite set apart from his" employer. eirl elle'El "I can make my fortune out of that - up alive," he .thought. "She has probably as a servant. Whatever she is, she is underb-ersel▪ f• den of disgrace, and cannot think highll ostlfntlefean' She certainly can't expect to marry I'll find her out' as soon as I-can leave P_.ded Castle, and I'll marry her. She can't• iie already. Rut if she should refuse to ree'w.t see I can still make a fortune out of her. j lat'',o a the girl, marry her if possible, amine gentleman. In any case, she wand setin be tl2. of riches to me."</p>
        <p>To be continued.</p>
