        <p>RUNCORN FREE LIBRARY LECTURES.</p>
        <p>THE SHIP CANAL A-ND RUNCORN'S FUTURE.</p>
        <p>The fourth of a series of lectures promoted by the Free Library Committee was delivered in Bethesda Lecture Hall on Tuesday evening, by Mr. C. A. Timmins, of Messrs. Timmins, the well-known engineers and iron founders. There was a crowded audience, and Mr. Marshall Stevens. manager of the Ship Canal occupied the chair. The subject was "The Manchester Ship Canal," one of much interest to the locality, and the great mass of facts with regard to tne construction of the waterway submitted by the lec- turer, were illustrated by 180 lantern views of photo- graphs taken by Mr. Timmins. Mr. Timmins is credited with being one of the most expert and artistic amateur photographers of the day, and a number of the pictures thrown upon the screen by the lantern were veritable works of art in their delicacy of light and shade. There was displayed a succession of pictures giving an idea of the construction of the gigantic under- taking from the cutting of the first sod at Eastharti up to the present time, and there were also representations of old land marks now obliterated from the landscape because they had stood in the line of the waterway. The great engineering feats of the undertaking were described by Mr. Timmins, who said the language of superlatives was constantly necessary in dealing with the construc- tion of the Canal. It was the widest canal in the world, the Weaver sluices the largest in the world, and the mountain of earth at Ince the largest artificial mound in the world, while difficulties had to be overcome such as had never been encountered in any previous waterway. In the course of his lecture Mr. Timmins let the audience into a" secret," which was that his firm secured the contract to make the trial borings to ascertain the nature of the strata through which the canal would have to be cut; and he (the lecturer) cut the first sod of the preliminary works at Arpley meadows, Warrington, where he made the first boring. He had made some two hundred borings along the line of the canal and in the Mersey Valley and there was an abundance of good water suitable for manufacturing purposes at all points. There was no finer site for works in the whole of Eng- land than along the banks of the Manchester Ship Canal. At intervals during the lecture, the Chairman interjected scraps of interesting information, and quite a furore of applause was caused when he announced that a ship building yard had been established at Manchester, and that at the present time there was more building going on there than there was upon the Clyde. A ship repair- ing yard had also been opened at Ellesmere Port. At the conclusion of the lecture Mr. F. Wiswall proposed a hearty vote of thanks to Mr. Timmins for his instructive lecture on a most inter- esting subject. The Committee were very deeply indebted to Mr. Timmins for his services, and it must be as pleasing to him as it was to them to see such a large and appreciative audience.—Mr. Pritchard seconded the motion.—The Chairman ia supporting the motion said the first object of the Ship Canal was to provide access to the sea for the populatioa of two millions of people who were within a certain distance of Manchester. But in doing that, a very much larger function was served. The Ship Canal at one or other points along its length served a popula- tion, as the nearest ocean steamer port, of nearly eight millions of people, that was one fifth of the whole population of the United Kingdom, who would be nearer to the Ship Canal than to any other ocean steamer port. ' (Applause.) Whilst Manchester had been making this canal for itself, it had opened out for them in Runcorn district possibilities which had never been opened before by any undertaking for any district. (Hear, hear.) They all knew far better than the generality of England, the advantage of access to water. Here in Runcorn they had been enabled, with a small depth of water, to do some really tremendous things—to serve the whole of the potteries with their raw materials. That was a fact that one could always point out to show the advantage of water communication. Nearly the whole of those materials came from the South of England, where the distance by railway was prohably not as much as one fourth of the distance traversed by the small sailing vessels,which had often to wait eight and ten days,to get up to Runcorn,where the materials were transhipped into boats and taken to the potteries by canal. And yet the railways could not compete against it. That was what had been in the past. See what they had before them. There was now a greater depth of water at the entrance to Eastham Locks than there was to any dock in Liver- pool and probably at the end of next month they would have four feet more, which would be 113 feet below the old dock sill Liverpool, and they would eventually get the entrance twenty feet below the old dock sill : and any vessel crossing the Bar would be able to continue her voyage and enter the Ship Canal at any state of the tide. Take the case of two ships crossing the Bar at high water, one bound for Liverpool and the other bound for Runcorn. The one bound for Liverpool would get to the Mersey too late to enter the dock until next tide, and would have to wait in the river eight or ten hours ; while the ship bound for Runcorn would be able to come along the Ship Canal and would probably be half discharged before the Liverpool ship could get into berth. (Applause.) He was int there to speak agaiast Liverpool, and he hoped they would not do Liverpool any harm. Look in the other !direction. Runcorn was nearer to Wolverhampton than any other steamer port ; Birmingham was just outside the limit being three or four miles nearer Sharpness Point on the Servern than to Runcorn. Just to mention how that was working for Runcorn, he had within the past two or three weeks taken a gentleman to the site of the old thethe mm d Ta nh de sg, eau and dl gel mill at taeatnhnseaawhs raamrtspoaondtnehe seesuocort oohati sinhst te, ,malwainnrhdedi cehhts he was mill at Old Quay,a view of which the lecturer had shown wwa mt aa hsis el aeemti r t sti iu the in ert dgooinsogmtoetphiuntgatmo entrance to Gloucester, or on the Ship Canal. He had shown him the site of the old mill and also a site at Wes- ton Point. He could not say what would happen. The Chairman referred to the great changes which had taken place since his first visit to Runcorn twenty-five years ago, and looking ahead he was reminded of a quotation from Shakespeare, " There is a tide in the affairs of men which taken at the flood leads on to fortune." There was no question that it was high tide for them in Runcorn. Whether they took advantage of it was their matter, but they had the assistance of the Ship Canal Co. (Applause.)—The motion was carried by acclama- tion and the Lecturer briefly replied.—Mr. P. Speakman, CM., moved a vote of thanks to Mr. Marshall Stevens for his services in the chair. He felt sure that the hard work of Mr. Marshall Stevens in the early itstitution of this great work would some day bear very great fruit for Runcorn. (Hear, hear.) Ship building was going on in Manchester, An d ship building and repairing was taking place near Ellesmere Port, but neither place was Runcorn. He was, however, very glad to tell them that the Runcora Improvement Commissioners in their wis- dom had made piovision in the Old Quay Dock for the repair of some of the largest :hips that could come under Runcorn Bridge, and this would be to the future advantage of Runcorn.—Dr. McDougall seconded the proposition and said the Ship Canal was one of the most splendid waterways in the world, and if Runcorn people were energetic and alive to their own interests there was a prospect before the town stch as it had never had before, (hear, hear.)—ln acknOw- lodging the vote, the Chairman referred to the proposed dry dock at Runcorn and said Mr. Speakman did not know that there was a gentleman who was anxious to get these dry docks into his hands. Messrs. Fisher, Ren- wick and Co. of Newcastle, the owners of the steamers plying between London and Saltport, and also directors of a large dock company, had the boat building yard at Manchester and the repairing yard at Ellesmere Port, but it did not say that the Messrs. Fisher would have the dry dock at Runcorn. The Ship Canal Company hoped that some Runcorn firm would come forward and do the business at Old Quay. (Applause.) So that they might have some little competition in the business.—The meet- ing then terminated. •</p>
