      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>" LIBERATOR " FINANCE.</head>
        <p>" LIBERATOR " FINANCE.</p>
        <p>, EXTRAORDINARY REVELATIONS.</p>
        <p>In the London Bankruptcy Court on Monday an ex- amination took plxce before Mr. Registrar Emden with regard to the failure of the London and General Bank. At the date of the winding up the Liberator Society I owed the bank nearly £100,000; and Mr. Stewart, the I official receiver, said that tins bank, though not the fountain head, was the channel through which the Lily.;r- ator Society and other companies had conducted tneir operations. Mr. W. Johns, the secretary, was examined, and he detailed Mr. Jabez Balfour's connection with the various societies. Re said that in 1883 the paid-up share capital of the bank was £85,724, and the investments I £36,531. In September last year the share capital had increased to £322.687, and the investments had gone ' down to £1,365. The bank at that time, he said, was getting" hard up." The witnesses made many extra- ordinary statements as to the management of the group of societies, and said it was an understood thing that all the Balfour companies were allowed to overdraw—there was a kind of unwritten law to that effect. fie believed It was feared that in the event of a cheque of one of the companies being dishonoured the whole of them would tumble to the ground. .</p>
        <p>_ The examination of Mr. G. E. Brock, a director of the London and General Bank, Limited, was resumed on Tuesday in the London Court of bankruptcy. The wit- ness made some remarkable admissions with regard to the manner in which the funds of the bank were em- ployed to" finance" the "Balfour group' of companies. He was closely questioned as to the means which the bank had adopted to get money from the "poorer class "of ;nvestors, and by which the shareholders were imduced to increase their holdings. . Three years ago the bank issued a circular calling attention to the "sound and profitable business" which it was doing, and to the " large access of profitable business which had resulted from the increase of capital." More money was asked for, although the witness admitted that 63 per cent, of the cash and assets shown in 1890 represented advances to the "Balfour group " and to one or two private per- sons, and whose accounts had resulted in the loss of L 70,000.</p>
