      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>TH E EXAMINER, SATURDAY,</head>
        <p>TH E EXAMINER, SATURDAY,</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Secretaries of Football Clubs are requested to note that reports of matches played on Saturday must be sent to the head office, Bewsey-street. Warrington, not later than ten o'clock on Wednesday morning.</p>
        <p>CRICKETERS should procure the 1893 Pocket Diary and Companion published by GEO. G. BI7SSEE &amp; CO. It is full of Information, and has the requirements of an ordinary Di4ry. Leather bound, gilt edges, post free 6d., from the Sports Manufactory, Peckham Rye, London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>RUGBY. LANCASHIRE RUGBY CUP COMPETITION. WARRINGTON v. BROUGHTON.</head>
        <p>RUGBY. LANCASHIRE RUGBY CUP COMPETITION. WARRINGTON v. BROUGHTON.</p>
        <p>The record has been broken, and Warrington on Saturday claimed their first victory over Broughton at Cliff Point. For seven years have our local team been trying to effect the downfall of Broughton on their , native heath, and the first essay saw them defeated by the narrow =win of a try, whilst the following sea- son a similar point decided the play in favour of the Manchester team. A draw has resulted once, but last 1 year so indifferent had been the form of the Broughton men that our locals were pointed to as certain winners, but the unexpected came off, and the Warringtonians had to retire defeated by two pals to a try. At Wild- erspool, Warrington, with one exception, have always accounted for their popular rivals. However, for Saturday's match, form certainly pointed to the War- ringtoniens coming off victorious, for Broughton had cut a Most indifferent figure in the League, their aggre- gate reading, as having only won one match out of 13 played, which, with two drawn, gave them the small- est total on the list—four points. The Broughton ground was covered with a thin layer of snow, and when the teams lined up it was seen that both were strongly represented. Shepherd started towards the city goal, and the start was indeed sensa- tional. Nevins' response was piloted back by S. Craven to the Warrington quarters, and from the scrimmage formed Whiteley and the Roberts commenced to pass beautifully The visitors' goal appeared certain to fall. when Webster raced across from his wing, and Craven being apparently unaware of his presence the tall War- ringtonian intercepted a pass, and racing away he de- fied the weak opposition offered by Frost, and, after a I run almost from goal 0 goal he Grounded _beneath the.posts.</p>
        <p>amidst ringing cheers from the small muster of War- rington enthusiasts. Evans failed at the easy place. Whiteley showed up on the restart, but Warrington were smarter in the scrimmages and loose play, and Jolley and Berry leading the way, the Warringtonians were attacking in strong force. James Bate tricked his rivals, but S. Craven responded brilliantly to the kick, and Boscow negotiated touch well within the Brough- tonians' 25. Frank Turner, Ashton, and Nevins dribb- led neatly, and then a capital hit of work by Webster and Evans resulted in the former forcing a minor. The visitors still pursued their aggressive tactics, and Fair Turner got away from one scrimmage, which was suc- ceeded by Shepherd neutralizing. Pretty passing by the visiting backs saw Webster dropping at goal, which sent up the second minor. Aided by off-side play on the part of the Broughton men. The Visitors Still Attacked, .</p>
        <p>and had the hardest of luck in not adding tangible points. The forwards were playing splendidly, and as a consequence the backs had a distinct advantage. By degrees the homesters reached half way, and still further ground was gained when Craven punted to Boscow, who was floored near his own posts. This was the only occasion they were really dangerous this half, and then their stay was but of brief duration, for James Bate, in one essay, reached the half way. Then cs me a capital display by Evans, but a rush by the Brothers Roberts was well returned by Boscow. Webster now effected a splendid tackle of C. Craven when he appeared likely to score. Evans and John Bates passed neatly, and the venue was changed, and a spell of exchange kicking finally ending to Lawless's advantage. The visitors now maintained the aggressive up to the interval. Frost met a kick by James Bate cleverly, and then sharp work by Berry and Webster neutralized the ground gained by Shepherd and Tomlinson. The Turners, Thorniley, and Ashton were the leaders in another rush, and a run by Jolley saw the county forward grasped by the jersey. John Bate again passed to Jolley, and play was taken dangerously near the home posts. Nevins accounted for S. Craven splendidly, and passing by the three-quarters saw Lawless upset a few yards from the line. Again Broughton cleared, A. C. Tomlinson and Whiteley effect- ing one good dribble. Evans made his mark, and James Bate looked certain to score in a subsequent rush, but Frost tackled splendidly. Westall punted to Webster, but ao material point resulted, for Webster piloted back, and an over-eagerness on the Warrington forwards' part rainute later lost a certaia try. Berry, John Bate, and Nevins each shone in turn, but one c3mbined effort, in which at least a dozen pair of hands were concerned, resulted in the Visitors Defying Opposition, and ended in James Bate scoring near the corner. Jolley _again dribbled well, but no' addition was:made to' the score, which read at half-time—Warrington, 0-2-2; Broughton, 0-0-0. On resuming, Warrington allowed their 3pponents no quarter, and the order of attack was again resorted to. Fair. Turner and Craven exchanged kicks with advantage to the former. Passing by the Warrington backs eventually travelled to Webster, who failed to take one a trifle too high, and although the home forwards slightly relieved, Webster beat them back, and Higham was all but in. Evans passed well to Lawless, who failed to hold, but the movement led up to a scrimmage near the line, and the Broughton backs being outwitted, James Bate Ran in Without Opposition, but again the kick at goal was a failure. The visitors attacked harder than ever, and a spell of forward play saw Webster essaying another futile drop at goal. Boscow was called upon for the first time, and had little difficulty in forcing Broughton back to their lines. James Bate, Evans, and Lawless again passed, and the oval was hovering near the home posts, but Bronghton now displayed improved form, and G. Roberts eventu- ally reached Boscow, who recovered at the second time of asking. Donohue and Berry dribbled well, and Frost i was just n time to kick dead. Following the drop out came a splendid dribble by Jolley, and the oval was taken over, Donohue Claiming the Point, but the kick at goal was again a failure. The issue was never in doubt, and although the Cravens made desper- ate efforts to score near the close, the tackling of Boscow was very safe, and the end came with ringbtn having won easily by 0-4-4 to Broughton nil.</p>
        <p>Teams :</p>
        <p>Warrington J. Boscow, back ; P. R. Lawless, J. W. Evans, and H. Webster, three-quarter backs ; John Bate and James Bate, half backs; H. Ashton, Fair. Turner,Frank Turner J. Jolley, W. Nevins, T. Dono- hue, J. Berry, J. T. ThOrniley, and J. Hlgharn, forwards. Broughton ; G. H. Frost, back ; C. E. Craven, S. Craven, and J. T. Westwall, three-quarter backs ; D.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>ARCH 4, 1893.</head>
        <p>ARCH 4, 1893.</p>
        <p>ball, and Warder filled up the breach nicely, and kick- ing well, passing and repassing among the forwards took place, which resulted in Hampson gaining a try, but Warder failed at the place. Following the drop-out the home line was again threatened. A free kick eventu- ally relieved, and A. G. Wood further sup- plemented, when Houghton got possession, and punted well down to Clause. Later Houghton had a shie at goal, but missed the mark, and following the drop out Warder punted strongly, and once again. the Parkites were driven to their line, when A. G. and Taylor Wood dribbled nicely. Nicholson picking up and racing grandly down, the Runcorn line was crossed, but Faulkner collaring, a five yard scrimmage was ordered, and on the break up half-time was called, when the teams changed ends with the score—Runcorn, 1 goal, 1 try, and 1 minor ; Park, nil. Nicholson restarted, and there being no return, play hovered round the central line, when Hutchinson punted nicely down into touch, and for a time the home side more than held their own, but lacked finish. Morris at length broke away, but was finely collared by Clauss, but a free kick against the Park resulted in the pack being contested in the home 25. Fenton somewhat eased the pressure, but the home forwards tackled very tamely, and eventu- ally the Runcorn forwards got close to the line. Spence dribbled clear, and after Houghton had attempted a drop, Clauss cleared under difficulties, though Warder recovered the ground from a mark in midfield, and after Abrams had feinted to pass, and all but scored, Kenion luckily saved by touching down. Exchanges between Clauss and Houghton brought the play to the centre. At this juncture Warder retired hurt, but, despite being short of numbers, the visitors attacked, and after smart passing Morris scored near the corner, Houghton mak- ing a good but unsuccessful bid fer goal. Houghton, who filled Warder's position at centre, put in some capi- tal kicking into touch. Robinson eventually had an easy chance, and when near the line passed to Houghton who had no one to pass and scored easily, Taylor kicking the goal. A minor point to the Park followed immedi- ately afterwards, and dropping out Clauss passed to Fenton, and on the latter parting to Hutchinson the last- named player scored a grand try behind the posts, but the Scottish international failed to place a goal. Result—Runcorn, 2 goals and 2 tries (14 points) ; Birken- head Park, 1 try (2 points). Teams : Birkenhead Park —Kenion, back ; Clauss, Fenton, and Hutchinson, three quarter backs; Anderson and Lumgair,half backs; A.G. Wood, Taylor Wood, Lockwood, Chadwick, Spence, White, Baxter, Anderson, and Nicholson forwards. Runcorn—Houghton, back •, Morris, Warder, and J. Faulkner, three-quarter backs; Robinson and Abrams, half backs; Evans, Farmer, Langley, Derbyshire, Williams, Davies, Stubbs, Harnpson, and Taylor, for- wards. RUNCORN V. BIRKENHEAD PARK "A " TEAHB.—Played at Runcorn, on Saturday, before a moderate attendance of spectators. The home team were short of several of their usual players, who were assisting the let team at Birkenhead. Passing between the home backs caused the visitors to defend, Robinson and Molyneaux each making efforts to get over. Evans and Nickson, with tricky passing, outwitted their opponents, and Moly- neaux receiving, dashed over, the same player kicking a goal. From the restart, the home forwards quickly asserted themselves. Robinson, getting a good pass,ran finely over,no goal resulting. The visitors now making a spurt, caused the game to be more interesting. Ker dribbled beautifully away from a scrimmage and reached the centre before being stopped by Dunbavand. Evans got the ball away, and passed te Molyneaux,who ran and threw to Robinson, the latter dodging easily over. The game still continued to be one-sided. Again getting in between the posts, Robinson kicked a goal. From a centre kick the Park forwards made a good effort, and took the ball over the home lines, but failed to take the chance given them. Pretty passing between Evans, Nickson, Molyneaux, and Robinson gave that player a chance ; but Lynch foiled him at a critical moment, Harrison getting over from the ensuing scrim- mage. The kick failed. Court made a splendid run, but foolishly passed, and the opportunity was lost. The interval arrived with the score Runcorn, 2-3-3, to nil. Woods re-started. By short, sharp rushes, the Runcorn- ians forced the Parkites on their lines, Jordan and Welbe taking the ball over, the latter completing the try ; Dunbavand placed a goal. Hardly had the ball been put in motion than Welbe dribbled splendidly from the centre, and scored a good try. The game became very exciting. 'f aylor was seen sprinting along in magnifi- cent style, and Lynch effected a miraculous save. From now up to call of time the home team had matters all their own way, the final score being Runcorn, 3-5-4 ; Birkenhead, nil. ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN V. PENIEETH.-At Runcorn, on Saturday. The teams were :—Runcorn : Goal, W. Mainwaring ; full backs, W. Blower and A. Paterson; half backs, J. A. Bate, A. Richards, and Alb. Limb; forwards, A. Wright, Arthur Limb, H. Pye,J. Hampson, and C. Limb. Penketh : Goal, W. Higham ; backs, Elliot and J. Lewis ; half backs. Higham, Jackson, and Whitfield ; forwards, H. Bowyer, J. Bowyer, Dumbill, StaMey, and Moore. The homesters had the best of the play in the I first half, and at half time the •score stood—Runcorn two goals ; Penketh, nil. On resuming, the visitors' for- wards played much better, and succeeded, from a nicely placed corner kick by Lewis, to get the ball beneath the posts, beating the Runcorn goal-keeper. The play was then even for some time, Pye and Arthur Limb at for- ward and Blower at back playing well. C. Limb for the homesters, put through a nice shot from the left, which was, however, ruled back by the referee. Runcorn, to- wards the end, had a little the best of the game, which ended in a victory for the home eleven by two goals to one.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CRICKET A.F.C. v. HILLSBORO' FROD- SI:UAL—PIayed at Frodsham. The visitors arrived with only ten men, so a substitute was found for them. Hills- boro' kicked off, but the Cricket Club got the ball and pressed, being very near scoring once or twice, when, at length, Francomb got in a long shot which just got underneath the bar, this being the first point for the Cricket Club. After this Reid scored and two minutes later Hoare put on the third and the Hillsboro' goalkeeper saved luckily on more than one occasion. Connor soon after scored a fourth, and before half time Reid and Isher- wood each registered another, the score at half time being Cricket Club 6, Hillsboro' nil. The second half was simply a repetition of the first, the home team being simply out classed, and the visitors custodian was quite idle at the other end. Connor, Feneley and Hoare each scored again. Final result :—Cricket Club 9 goals, Hillsboro' nil. Cricket Club team: Goal Forrest; full back J. S. Fran- comb, W. H. Hannah, halves J. Dunn, F. Williamson and a substitute, forwards, Reid, Feneley, Connor, Hoare, Isherwood.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. Sr. ELPHIN'S.—There has been great interest in Association circles in Warrinton with regard to the meeting of these old rivals, last Saturday, on the St. Elphin's ground. The day was dull, and snow was on the ground in places. There was a good number of spectators. Warrington kicked off, and for a time the teams seemed to be equally matched, but Thornton was accidentally tripped and fell on his knee, making him practically useless for the remainder of the game. Davies missed his kick, and Batton centred nicely, and Scarisbrick beat Bannon with a good shct. This was the only score in the first half. On the re-start, the homesters played in a very determined manner, but J. Mather, made some brilliant saves. Warrington now got possession add Ball raced across the field with no- body to face but Marshall and the goalkeeper, Marshall</p>
        <p>robbed him and transferred play far down the field. Tay- lor, who had on repeated occasions been" conspicuous by some very effective runs, careered down the field, and sending the ball across the goal mouth S. Colley scored just in the nick of time. The same players repeated their performance a short period afterwards. The War- rington players, if we except Mather,Dutton,Thompson, Norman, and the goalkeeper, seemed to collapse at this period of the game, and the above players had their work cut out for them to stave off a big defeat. They were successful in this to some extent, but towards the close of the game Cook with a splendid shot put in the fourth goal amid vehement cheering. The game ended with the result—St. Elphin's four goals, Warrington nil. The following were the teams :—*ar- rington : Goal, Bannon; full backs, J. Mather and Davies; half backs, Norman, Thompson, and Dutton; forwards, Ball, Thornton, Pierpoint, Forbes, and Ros- tron. St. Elphin's : Goal; Bates; full backs, Marshall and the Rev. T. W. Cook; half backs, R. Colley, J. Phcenix, and Clayton; forwards, S. Colley, nicker:- bottom, Scarisbrick, Hatton, and Taylor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>WARRINGFON JUNIOR LEAGUE COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGFON JUNIOR LEAGUE COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH V. WIRRINOTON ST. MARY'S.—This match took place at Stockton Heath, on Saturday, in the presence of about 400 or 500 spectators. The home- eters kicked off, and after several exchanges play rested in the home 25, some tight scrimmaging taking place. The home forwards, by a dribble, transferred play to the visitors' half, Worthington having hard lines. Here the home centre collided with an opponent, and had to retire. From the ensuing scrimmage the visitors rushed the oval to the home 25. and a penalty kick saw Burton making a bid for goal, but only a minor resulted. Play from now up to half time remained even, when no minor point had been scored. In the second half Cross, amidst cheers, again took his position at centre, but ap- peared; very weak. The homesters now adopted the four three-quarter system. Cross returning the kick- off, play remained at half-way. Lawless securing ran splendidly, and scored a brilliant try close to the up- rights, and Lord making no mistake with the kick credited the Heathens with three further points. On the restart play became very exciting, and Hampson securing, just managed to get in at the corner flag, but the place proved too difficult for Burton. The drop- out found the homesters pressing, Hankey having hard lines. Two penalty kicks now fell to the homesters, but nothing came of them. Just on the call of time the homesters made a desperate attempt close to the posts, but failed to add to their score. On the whistle sounding nothing further had been scored, and the homesters ran out winners of a bard fought game by five poiats to two</p>
        <p>BIRKENHEAD Pamir v. Rimcorm.—Played at Birken- head on Saturday, before a big crowd of spectators. Runcorn opened the play, and Clauss replying, Morris ran nicely down the left, but a free kick to the Park re- lieved to midfield, and Morris again got possession, and carried close to the line. On the play becoming loose the ball was kicked over the tine, Kenyon mulling it, thereby letting in Robinson near the post. Warder took the place, and had no difficulty in securing a goal. Re- starting, Warder returned strongly, and following some even play at the centre the Runcornians were awarded a free kick, but not much ground was gained when Anderson opened out the play, which resulted in Hut- chinson carrying to the Runcorn 25. Relief was followed by Clauss taking a penalty kick, but the ball fell short, but the Parkites got in good position, and packs were fought near the visitors' line. The forwards relieved, and Hutchinson got possession, but was unable to get round Morris. Some capital footwork favoured the Runcornian forwards, and Farmer led to the centre, fol- lowing which Morris made tracks down the left as far as the home 25. The Parkites carried the pack but not the ball, and the visitors were again close on the line. Clauss kept his men at bay, and A. G. Wood led on a grand dribble to the visltors' quarter line. The Park fielding was at fault, and the Runcornians were not slow to take advantage. Screwing the scrimmages was a source of trouble to the Park van, but a moment later Morris lost by passing forward, and Clauss ran well along touch to the Runcorn 25. On the play opening out Fenton gave Hutchinson a capital chance, but Houghton tackled grandly. Anderson failed to hold the</p>
        <p>•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <p>M</p>
        <p>Roberts, G. H. Roberts, and W. Whitley, half-backs ; E. W. Broadbent, C. Barrett, J. Munro, .1. E. Shepherd, G. Skidmore, F. Frost. G. Tomlinson, and A. C. Tomlinson, forwards. Referee. Mr. T. Hunter. WARRINGTIII4 " A " v. BaouonroN "A.'—The visi - tors were handicapped by having to call in several re- serve men for this return fixture at Warrington. Soon after the start a fine dribble by Hampson placed the Broughtonians on the defensive, until Auby relieved smartly to centre. Here Ashuret got the ball away, which was kicked across to Massey, and this player taking it splendidly ran in grandly. The home forwards still pressed the visitors, and in a very short space of time Ashurst again secured the leather, and getting beautifully away threw wide to ;Barnes, who passed at the right time to Massey for hini to secure his second try. After this episode Auby and Smith were the means of Broughton getting away to midfield, the last-named supplementing this effort by making a fine run into home quarters. A long kick returned the ball, and a vigorous rush by Holmes, Edwards, Randles, and Bailey took the oval over the line, a minor resulting. The cleverness of the home backs was again very evident shortly afterwards, for Lally, Ashurst,and Barnes mani- pulated the leather so well that Massey ran in splendidly a third time, Barnes kicking a goal. Some very fast play ensued. Broughton dribbled into Warrington lines, Potts replying with a run and a pass, which caused his side to again attack, only to see Smith make a splendid run to the opposite 25. Teare, Eden, Monks, and Molyneaux cleared a grand bout of passing by the home backs taking the warfare further down the field. Broughton were subject to a heavy pressure, but beyond two minors nothing more was scored up to half time. Upon the resumption of hostilities the home team forced their opponents on their lines, a drop by Potts going dead. Fluctuating play, in which Randles was con- spicuous by a run and kick, followed, and then Robin- son ran to midfield. A fast run by Edwards neutralized the effort, and from the following loose play Lally ran in smartly. Broughton were showing signs of dis- tress, a strong rush headed by Hampson, Teare, and Edwards overpowering the defence, and a kick being mulled by Quinvey, the ball was whipped over the line by Lally. The visitors could not break away, Potts making a fine run and pass to Bailey. This player transferred to Holnaes,who ran to the line, where Barnes threw to Massey for him to drop a pretty goal. Smith, who played finely for Broughton, made another splendid run, but the game was soon at the other end, the ulti- mate result being that Lally and Ashurst gave Barnes a chance, which he utilized by eting a very clever try, which Ashurst converted. Final—Warrington 3-4-5 to nil.</p>
        <p>WARIZINOTON 2ND V. BROUGHTON 2ND.—The return match between the above clubs took placeon Wilderspeol Cans( way, on Saturday. The visitors came short- handed, but good substitutes were found. Warrington kicked off, and at once pressed, a rush by the forwards headed by Freeth and Dakin taking play to the visitors' lines, but Daniels: with a nice run and kick, relieved to half way. Alter a bout of passing by Houghton and Mannion, Gibson got hold, and knocking his opponents off in grand style, scored a clever try, Bolesworth kick- ing a capital goal. Broughton now attacked, but the fine play of Bolesworth checked their progress. From a scrimmage Rowe got hold, and after tricking his oppos- ing halves, kicked up the field, and Lawless following up in good style scored under the posts, Bolesworth again converting. Warrington again pressed, and a dribble by the forwards ended in Dakin scoring, the kick being a failure. Half time Warrington, 2-1-1 ; Broughton, nil. Wilding started for the visitors, Man- nion replying with a nice rutfand kick. Welsby for the visitors was next prominent with a nice run and kick to ! Allen, who mulled the ball. Houghton next passed to Rowe, who was tackled with the ball in his possession. Broughton were here allowed a free kick. Wilding took the kick and landed a beautiful goal. The remaining portion of the game proved to be in favour of the home team, Gibson being pulled up by W. Daniels on the line several times. Mannion next had a chance, but pre- ferred to drop at goal, the ball going dead. The game ended with Rowe scoring and Gibson failing. For the visitors all the subs, played well, especially Daniels, who with his daring saves and tackling saved his side from a bigger defeat, and Bolesworth, Gibson, Houghton, and Dakin were the best for Warrington. Final result : Warrington, 2.24; Broughton, one geal (penalty).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>WARRINGTON INFIRMARY AND DISPENSARY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON INFIRMARY AND DISPENSARY.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING OF THE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Infirmary and Dispensary Committee, was held in the Council chamber of the Town Hall, on Tuesday afternoon. Among those present were the Mayor, the Rector of Warrington (Rev. F. W. Willis), Revs. E. C. E. Carleton, and H. V. Pigott, Major Pickmere, Drs. Fox and Bowden, and Messrs. John White, F. Monks, J. FaircloKgh, J. Roberts, Thomson, R. Dobson, Mrs. F. W. Willis, Mrs. Carleton and other ladies and gentlemen. On the proposition of Major Pickmere, the Mayor took the chair. A letter was read from Mr. J. Charlton Parr, stating his inability to attend.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>HOUSE SURGEON'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>THE HOUSE SURGEON'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Dr. E. Bowden senior house surgeon, submitted his annual report. During the course of it,he observed that during the year 1892, 269 patients were admitted to the Infirmary, and which 177 were males' and 92 females. Of these, there were 17 deaths. Forty-three operations had been performed. There was an increase of 41 in the number of patients treated in the Infirmary. Of the 17 deaths which occurred, 13 were , due to accidents. The old out door department had been converted into Wards which were now in use ; the Halton and Hardy wards had been enlarged, and a new and improved system of heating and ventilating had been introduced, the new Beamont ward was almost completed. By these alterations and enlargements, the number of beds would be increased from 23 to 40, thus increasing the utility of the infirmary, by providing for the admission of many cases which formerly had to be treated at home. The number of patients treated at the dispensary, reached a total of 4,757. There were 365 more patients than last year, but the number of deaths —l22—was less. The Rector moved the adoption of the report, saying that there were two satisfactory items in the report, first an increase in the number of the beds, and a decrease in the number of deaths.—Mr. Jona Fairclough seconded, and the motion was passed unani- mously. THE TREASURER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>The treasurer'l7;ePOTi--;;Tili-et;--Put before the meet- ing showing the income of the past, year to be £1,761 193. 2d., expenditure £1,775 83. 2d., being a deficit of £l3 93.—Mr. John White moved the adoption of the accounts. He said that the expenditure kept increasing every year, and he was afraid that this year it would increase by leaps and bounds. For the first time the receipts were less than the expenditure. There were several causes for this. In the previous year, there had been £52 received from a concert, which they had not received during the past year. Several items also had fallen a little. He thought that handsome contribution re- ceived from the workingmen of this town, (a sum of £440) reflected great credit upon them.—Mr. John Fairclougn seconded the motion and the Mayor pointed out that owing to the enlarging of the Infirmary for the benefit of the town more help would be required. The motion was passed unanimously.</p>
        <p>111S-CELLANEOUS.</p>
        <p>Mr. George Eakin— was re-elected treasurer, Major Pickmere, austitor, Messrs. Joseph Davies and J.L. 'Fun- stall, Hon. Secs. for the ensuing year. The Hon. Medical Officers were also re-elected. The Board of Management, House Visitors, finance committee, build- ing committee, ladies committee, and ball committee, were thanked for their services. Special thanks were given to the Workingmen's Committee and those they represent, for the handsome sums they had subscribed to the Institution during the past year, and they were encouraged to continue their praiseworthy labours. Thanks were also given to Messrs. E. M. Crosfield F. W. Monks, and A, J. Jolley for their work in connection with this Committee.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomson said he thought that more money could be got from the workingmen if the matter were brought before them in the proper way. Mr. F. Monks, agreed with Mr. Thomson and observed that one-third of the total subscription were taken from a very few firms. He suggested that Id. per week be subscribed from the wages of each workman as was done at his firm. A vote of thanks was passed to the proprietors of the several newspapers in which lists of contributors and weekly returns of patients had from time to time been published. Mr. J. CHARLTON Pena was re-elected president, Mayor, Col. J. I. Blackburne, T. H. Lyons, the Rector of 'Warrington and J. R. Buckton were elected vice-presi- dents. The old comi...ittee were re-elected, and Messrs. Burton and D. Fanshaw were elected to the committee, in the place of the late Mr. Wm. Sharpe, and the Mayor who becomes vice-president. The TREASURER said that the estimate for the altera- tions and extension of the Institution was £3,900, and it was found necessary to expend £5,210 7s 7d, and there was still a deficiency for this scheme of £1,328 13s. The usual vote of thanks closed the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>ON FOOTBALL</head>
        <p>ON FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>(Suggested thro' readin' a recent Medical Journal).</p>
        <p>Eh ! Football is a glorious game, Especially wen th' wind's Nor'-east; It's such a 'elth-presarvin' sport, An' quite a intellecshul feast ! Yo stond an' gaup two mortal heaurs I' drizzlin' sleet er drenchin' rain ; An' catch a tooth-warch, er, wot's woss, A damdable roomatic pain ! 0, its nice? Then, wen aw's o'er, vo hirple worn, Soaked to the skin -fro."yed to feet ; To hear yore better-hauf exclirim— " Yo bustin' too, it serves thi reet Ger off to bed, and strip aw off, Theau's not one haul this trubble met; But don't thee poo that freetful face, Theaur't noau cut eawt fer deein' yet !" 0. its nice! Wen Rugby scrummages are on, An' th'fun gets furious an hot, Wot dun thay favver like? Why, nowt But worms aw rigglin' in a pot! Then, aw at oncet, one bowts full speed, But gets stopp'd sudden wi' a trip ; Be flies ftle air, ez if *spangfued, An' drops wi' dislocated hip I 0, its nice ! Ez laughter ripples softly reaund, A " miner " " tries " to score a " goal ;" But gets sum brokken ribs istid— lie'd better ha' bin gettin' coal ! Then! cheers reseaund o'er hill an' dale, An'sheauts like thunder rend the sky; Ther's one mon less to feygbt fer" goal," One less to struggle fer a " try I' 0, its nice! But still th' Association game Is best o'th' two, it mita be sed ; Fer if thay connot reych the ball Wl' toes, thay let um use ther yed ; An', if the styles are weel compar'd, Th' advantages are plain to view; Fer wile yore practisin' to purr, Yore larnin' th' " Art o' Tuppin' "tool 0, its nice! Sum chaps there be that say an' swear, That football's England's noblest game; But then, no deaubt, these chaps ne'er risk 'd A chance o' be&amp; Welting lame! Taste's ev'rythin' ; but ez fer me, Aw want tuf brokken ribs er legs; To fly i'th' face o' Providence, Is wot ow wain't do! TUM 0' PEGS. * This is heaw wi used to serve frogs, wen aw wur a lad. Rose Bonk, March 3rd, 1893. A BIG COLLECTION.</p>
        <p>r Lord Salisbury, as Chancellor of Oxford CniversitY, presided on VS. ednesday at a meeting in the Sheldonian Theatre in aid of the building fund of the Radcliffe Infir- mary. There was a large gathering, and his Lordship was most Cordially received. The Marquis, having made an earnest appeal for generous assistance to the infirmary spoke at some length on the study of the science of medicine, and at toe conclusion of his address a collec- tion was taken which amounted to between £4,000 and £5,000.</p>
