        <p>MR. S. CHAMBERLAIN AND THE WELSH SUSPENSORY BILL.</p>
        <p>WRY HE DID NOT VOTE FOR IT. Replying to an inquiry as to his reasons for refraining from voting on the Welsh Suspensory Bill, Mr. Chamber- lain, after explaining that he was in Birmingham keeping a long-standing engagement,says :—" I think I ought to add that although lam, as I always have been,in favour of the principle of disestabliihment, and opposed to all State Churches,whether in England, Scotland, or Wales, I do not approve of the tactics of the present Govern- ment in bringing in a Suspensory Bill which would ham- per the work of the Church without the possibility of dealing finally with the subject for, at any rate, a con- siderable time to come. In my opini4n the bill is merely a sop to the Welsh members to keep them quiet while the Rome Rule Bill is under consideration, and is not in any way a serious attempt on the part of the Govern- ment to deal with this most important question. Under these circumstances I should probably have refrained from voting even if I had been able to be present on the occasion of the debate."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>LANCASHIRE RAILWAY COLLISION,</head>
        <p>A LANCASHIRE RAILWAY COLLISION,</p>
        <p>--- ANOTHER CASE OF AN OVERWORKED SIGNALMAN. Major Marindin's report to the Board of Trade as to tte circumstances attending the collision which occurred on January 20 last at Hulton's Siding, Chequerbent, on the London and North-Western Railway, batween a down passenger train from Bolton to Earlestown and a goods brake van attached to a tank engine which was stand- ing on the down line, has just been issued. Twenty passengers complained of injury, and the guard of thZ passenger train was slightly hurt. The inspector says the collision, which would probably have been a very serious one if the driver of the passenger train had not been keeping a lookout and been prompt in applying his continuous brake as soon as he saw the red light in front of him, was principally due to a mistake of Robert Ennon, signalman at Hußon's siding cabin, by not pay- ing proper attention to the rules. The brakesman, Henry Houlding, in charge of the engine and brake van, must also bear part of the blame for coming out on to the main line with only his tail lamp lighted and omit- ting to signal to the signalman when his train was clear over the siding points. Major Marindin considers the signalman was subjected to a great deal too much interruption in the fulfillment of his duties by having to fill up enginedrivers' requisitions and sign goods guards' shoes, and, in conclusion, says the accident would have been prevented by the use in its complete form of one of the "lock and block" systems' for the crossing of the engine and brake van on to the down line would have locked the block instrument for that line and also the starting signal at Daubhill until the line had been cleared by the passage of the brakeasran into laultoll,s</p>
