      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, MARCH 4, 1893,</p>
        <p>Local THE Government continue to un- Opti„ fold their programme of reforms • with a celerity which contrasts very favourably with the temporising and dilatory 113ethod8 of their predecessors in office. Little over a 13:1„ °nth of the Parliamentary session has elapsed, notwithstanding the time-wasting debate on t" Address, already several measures of the arat Magnitude have been introduced and advanced ittage, including the Home Rule Bill, the Welsh tnePensory Bill, the Registration Bill, and the itrafPloyers Liability Bill. On Tuesday evening ell to the lot of Sir WILLIAM JIARCOURT to eXPo nnd a measure which, when we consider the 154113 ant evils it is intended to counteract, cannot jue ancOUnted second in importance to any of those t(1-st Mentioned. The Liberal party are pledged vh, temperance reform, and the alacrity with the "Liquor Traffic (Local Control) " has been submitted to the House ,eonunons is a practical proof of the pi,,Bire of the Government to redeem the ,47,ges that have been made. Though the title ti 'Jae till to which the Chancellor of the Exchequer sPunsor sounds formal and unfamiliar, it te,,:ealitY embodies the " local option" scheme of h 'Perance reformers. "The general proposal I :ye to submit to the House is," said Sir Wm. Iteonar, in his admirable and conciliatory, Zeeh, "that the question of the continuance of t:lhouse for the retail sale of intoxicating liquor, b or or the premises, within limited areas, shall tal:letermined by the direct vote of the ratepayers, a en at stated intervals." That is a concise and description of the main object of the ''l in answering an interrogation, Sir Wm. 4,,is COURT explained that grocer's licenses for the of intoxicating liquors would be affected by ll just as much as those of publicans. The IteebnodShy which the objects of the Bill may be I,,e°Mplished are simple and devoid of complexity. 1,7.6 voters in each district—the voters being those ng have the right to vote at municipal or Council elections—are to be empowered to for themselves whether or not there shall pu.blic houses in their particular district. The ofs,tpets,it should be explained,will usually consist wct'ne parish, but in towns that are divided into r(18) each Ward will be a district, and in small</p>
        <p>boroughs which are not divided into wards, the whole borough will be the area. Before a vote can be taken in any district it will be necessary for a tenth of the electors to sign a requisition to that effect. After that has been done, and the re- quisition has been presented to the Town Council, or other local authority, a poll will be taken, and if two-thirds of the electors who record their votes I are in favour of prohibition, the public houses of the district will be closed. The public-houses will, however, not be closed immediately, for the publican will be given three years in which to pre- pare for his exit. In the meantime no further poll can be taken, and, indeed, so far as the par- ticular district is concerned, the question is settled for the three years, and for other three years after the public-houses have been shut up. At re expiration of these six years another poll may be held, and if the electors have by that time changed their minds public-houses may again be opened. If a poll that has been taken results in less than a two-thirds majority, no fur- ther attempt can be made to disturb the publicans till after the expiration of three years. The three years period has been selected, and we think wisely," in order that the communities may not be vexed every year by elections upon a highly controversial subject." As to compensation to publicans—or rather to brewers—it is scarcely necessary to say that the bill in no way counten- ances any proposal of that kind. The late Government, acting in the interests of the liquor trade, tried to but den the country with stupendous financial obligations under the name of "compen- sation," but they failed in that sinister design, and the present Government do not, as Sir WILLIAM HAROOURT said, admit any claim to pecuniary compensation. That the brewers of this country should be compensated for having been enabled by what is, to all intents and purposes, a legal monopoly, to amass wealth with a rapidity almost unknown in any other trade, is a preposterous notion which has been emphatically condemned by the good sense of the people. As Sir WILLIAM HARCOURT pointed out, the House of Commons has taken away one-seventh of the publicans' trade in certain parts of the United Kingdom by Sunday closing, and the question of compensation has never been raised; and in 1890-91 magistrates had refused the renewal of over one hundred liquor licenses of one kind and another and no compensation had been given, While refusing compensation to the brewer—for in most instanees it would be the brewer who would receive the benefit of compensation— the Government do not, however, deal inconsiderately with the publican. No publican will be turned out under the present Bill with less than a three years notice, for that, as we have already explained, is the length of time which must elapse before a vote for closing can take effect. It is a much longer notice thanl many of the so-called tenants of public-houses receive from the brewers who own the houses, and whose beer they are expected to sell. The Bill also deals with Sunday closing in England, but not in the same way as it has been dealt with in Scotland, Ireland and Wales. In those countries it is universally compulsory; in England it will be left for each district to decide for itself. To effect Sunday closing, however, a bare majority of the voters in any district will be suffi- cient, and the closing will begin dire ctly after the poll, and not at the end of three years. Such, in brief, are the main features of the Government's Bill for regulating the liquor traffic That it will be bitterly opposed by the supporters of the trade concerned, is absolutely certain. There is, therefore, all the more need for tem- perance reformers to respond to the call of an influential meeting held on Tuesday in the offices of the United Kingdom Alliance "to support, by every means in their power, the second reading of the Bill."</p>
        <p>IT seems that there are far more .‘ Lady " "emancipated women" then most Smokers. people dream of. An "emanci- pated woman," let us explain, is a woman who smokes. The editor of the "Cigar and Tobacco World," who ought to be an authority on the sub- ject, assures us that no one who has taken the trouble to inquire will deny that smoking amongst ladies is on the increase, and it is only the fear of Mrs. GRUNDY that prevents many women owning allegiance to "my lady nicotine." Even the names of the EMPRESS of RUSSIA and PRINCESS MAUD of Wales have been connected with this practice, and there is certainly a "soft impeach- ment" in the allegation that the PRIACESS LOUISE Some time ago ordered a costly cigarette-tube from a well-known Metro- politan tobacconist. There are, it is said, special brands of cigarettes placed on the market for the consumption of the fair sex. One of these is, suggestively enough, called the "Lady," and no fewer than forty of the dainty rolls are re- quired to weigh an ounce. It is not every lady who smokes that has the courage of her convic- tions, or rather her practices. Sometimes when they are purchasing cigars they throw out mysterious hints about " sweethearts " and brothers," but tobacconists are not without penetration, and, as a Putney vendor of the weed once remarked on the evening of a University boat race, "Bless their hearts, we see through it." On the other hand, Mrs, AMELIA ARNOLD, the hon. secretary of the London branch of the Man- chester Anti-Narcotic League, seems to argue that there are no lady smokers. She arrives at this conclusion by a summary process of logic. Her contention appears to be that no woman who smokes is a lady ; therefore,it follows as a matter of course, that no lady smokes. Much misapprehension prevails on this subject, she says, the term " lady " having no longer the same signi- fication as formerly. " Ladies " now-a-days figure in police courts for shop-lifting and disorderly conduct, and " ladies " are present at murder trials and divorce cases, and,according to a jocular reporter, two " ladies " recently quarrelled at a christening and one of them belaboured the other about the head with a fire- shovel. The word is, says Mrs. Aanorm, con- stantly applied to women of the lowest grade, and" the ladies who smoke as constantly as men do are probably some of the above-men- tioned." In conclusion Mrs. Arnold asks "is it probable that a blooming girl would defile her breath, decay her teeth, and damage her complex- ion, for surely most people are aware that tobacco is more destructive of personal beauty than any other agent? If this is doubted let them look at the stunted forma and sallow cheeks of our young smokers, and be convinced."</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON UNION ASSESSMENT COMMITTEE. A meeting of this Committee was held yesterilay at the Town Hall, Mr. R. Heaton in the chair. There was no business of public interest beyond a state- ment by Mr. W. Owen, valuer, that Messrs. Green- ing, Scotland Bank Works were being enlarged and the rateable value would be assessed at £l5O instead of £6O.</p>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Sr. AMBROSE CHURCH, WIDNES. The Rev. J. R. Glover, late curate of this church, preached his farewell sermon to a large congregatioa on Sunday night.</p>
        <p>- - STEWARD'S-LANE CHAPEL, DITTON. The bazaar in aid of the funds of this place of wor- ship, held in Lacey-at. school, on Thursday and Friday last week, realised £73.</p>
        <p>MOSS BANE CHAPEL, WIDNES. A public meeting in connection with the Band of Hope was held on Monday evening. Councillor Bu'lough pre- sided, and there was a good attendance. A miscellaneous programme was gone through.</p>
        <p>MILL BROW WESLEYAN BAND OF HOPE. Miss Amelia Austin, who is leaving the town, was the recipient on Monday erening of a gold brooch, given to her by the working committee of the above Band of Hope, of which she was a member. Go ID PRITCHARD'S for the beat live guinea watch a work- ingman can have.—Advt.</p>
        <p>SMALLPDX AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Another case of smallpox occurred at Whines. In this instance it is Dr. Partler of Victoria-road, assistant to Dr. O'Neil, and he has been removed to the hospital. It is thought he contracted the disease while attending the case which ended fatally a fortnight ago. There are now three patients in the hospital.</p>
        <p>DWELLING HOU- SE FIRE_ AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>_ On Thursday morning, one of the bedrooms at 30 Viaduct-st., Widnes, was discovered to be on fire. Buckets of water were immediately poured on the smouldering flames, and in this way the fire was soon put out. Damage to the extent of about £8 was done. 'the cause of the fire is unknown. SACRED CONCERT AT FARNWORTH. On Wednesday evening a very successful sacred con- cert was held in the National Schools, Farnworth, in aid of the school repair fund. The Rev. J. Wright Williams presided. An excellent programme was admirably sus- tained by Mrs. Haddock, Misses M. A. Jones, M. Irlam, and Simkin, Rev. J. T. Evans, Messrs. J. R. Lea, R. Morris, and Thorpe. JOSEPH PRITCHARD, Watchmaker and Jeweller, 51, High- at., and 4, Bridge st.. Runcorn.—Adq. PLEASANT SUNDAY EVENING AT RUNCORN. A good congregation assembled in Bethesda Congre- gational Chapel on Sunday evening, on the occasion of another of the series of pleasant people's services. The choir rendered several anthems during the service in good style and the Rev. J. S. Simpson delivered an ad- dress on "Your lives are not your own." Mr. J. Clough who for some years has acted as organist to the chapel, is leaving Runcorn for Liverpool.</p>
        <p>INTERNATIONAL HONOURS FOR A RUNCORN FOOTBALLER. In consequence of R. E. Lockwood, the Yorkshire Captain, having met with an accident, he will be un- able to take his place in the match England v. Scotland to-day, and J. R. Crompton the Runcorn captain has been notified by telegraph that his services will be re- quired as centre three quarter. Crompten's numerous admirers' have long argued that his merit deserved higher recognition than that accorded to him and there is no doubt that, fit and well, he will aniply justify his selection for international honours. JOSEPH PRITCHARD keeps spectacles to suit all sights from a shilling a pair.—Advt. LIMELIGHT LECTURE BY A RUNCORNIAN. On Friday evening the members ef the Beaconsfield Club, Sandon:terrace, Liverpool, spent a very pleasant hour in listening to an account of a tour in Norway, given by Mr. C. A. Timmins, of Runcorn. The lecturer took his hearers from the Mersey by means of the City of Richmonds to the fjords, and gave a humorous narrative ot the proceedings during the trip, in addition to showing some beautiful reproductions of photographs taken by himself. Mr. Timms was accorded a hearty vote of thanks at the finish of his highly interesting lecture. JOSEPH PRITCHARD'S sale shop is 51,Ifign-st., nis workshop 4, Bridge-st.—Advt. Sr. JOHN'S PRESBYTERIAN CHURCH, RUNCORN. The annual meeting of the church and congregation was held on Monday evening, in Ste. John's Schoolroom. The proceedings commenced with tea, and the after meeting was under the presidency of the pastor, the Rev. J. Pulford Williams, who was supported by the Rev. Calvin lhomas (Moderator of the Presbytery) and Mr. Robert Roberts (Liverpool), who attended as dele- gates on behalf of the Lancashire and Cheshire Presby- tery. There was a good gathering. The statement of accounts for the year ending December 31st last showed that the income has been £238 153. 7d., and the expen- diture £230 Us. lid., leaving a balance in hand of £s3s. Bd.</p>
        <p>When in want of a chaste and elegant wedding or birthday present go IA PRITCHARD'S, 51, High-st.— Advt. HALTON-ROAD CHAPEL AND MILL Bow SUNDAY SCHOOL. _ . .</p>
        <p>On Tuesday night the .011iit-ii;i iea was hold. Mesdames Frith, Pope, Hindley, Sowerby, J. M. Firth and Caygill, and Miss 1,. Moore presided at the tables. After tea the usual meeting was held, the Rev. W. Arrowsmith pre, siding. Mr. W. Graham presented a report of church contributions, and the Rev. J. Hanby, and Messrs. Firth Haddock, Temperley, and Caygill gave short addresses. A very interesting programme of music was excellently rendered by Mesdames &amp;Angler, jun., Temperley, A. Caygill, Misses Moores and Pope, Mr. A. Caygill, Master Charlie Rawsden, Mr. W. Moore, and the Band of Hope choir.</p>
        <p>If you want a lucky wedding ring go (051, Irian-sr.,whare you will geta present with it.—Adet.</p>
        <p>CRUELTY TO A HORSE AT WIDNBS. At Widnes Petty Sessioes on Thursday, Win. Hewitt, was summoned by the R.S.P.C.A., for cruelly illtreating his horse on the 17th inst. A boy named Richard Callan proved that the defendant had struck the animal on the head with the butt end of his whip until it had fallen down,aml P.C. Davidson who was called to the spot had to help to raise it. Inspector Dowty, R.S.P.C.A., said he had learned that the defendant had since sold the horse for 455. The defendant had been previously been convicted and fined 20s. and costs. The boy Callon was complemented on his conduct and paid a shilling for attendance at Court. PRITCHARD'S have now stood the test for 50 years, andquality and first-class workmanship are still guaranteed.—Advt. ROBBERY FROM THE WIDNES MAREET. At the Widnes Police Court, on Monday, before Messrs. J. Brock, and H. Wade Deacon, a young woman of very respectable appearance, named Elizabeth Kavanagh, 19 years of age, Duke-st.,Ditton was charged with stealing a silk handkerchief from a stall in the Wid- nes market, the property of Sarah Watt,draper,Everton- rd.,Liverpool,on Saturday night. The prosecutrix saw the prisoner come up to her stall and take the handkerchief which she put under her jacket. The prisoner pleaded not guilty, and called her sister to prove that she paid for the handkerchief to a girl at the stall.—The Bench fined the prisoner 10s. with the alternative of seven days' imprisonment. The Chairman cautioned the pri- soner's sister about giving false evidence. JOSEPH PRlTCHARD,Watchmaker and Jeweller, 51, High - et., and 4, Bridge-st., RuncOrn.—Ad. THE WIDNES STABBING OUTRAGE.</p>
        <p>On Monday morning, at the Widnes Police Court, be fore Messrs. Brock and H. Wade Deacon, a married woman named Elizabeth Bushell, of Edmund-street,was brought up on a remand charged with unlawfully wound- ing a labourer named Daniel M`Dermott by stabbing him on the 18th ult. The prosecutor deposed that he and his wife resided with the prisoner at 5, Edmund - street. On the Saturday in question he returned to his lodging about six o'clock in the evening, and found his wife drinking with the prisoner in his room. He had a drink with them, and then the prisoner asken him to pay for a pint of beer, but he refused. The prisoner then began to quarrel with him, and afterwards picked up a kettle from the hearth and threw it at him. Bushell then took a knife which was lying on the table, and stabbed witness in the head with it, saving '• ni do you one." After he had been stabbed witness shouted, “ Oh, you have done it."—Mrs. M‘Dermott corroborated her husband's statement.—The prisoner was committed for trial.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, S A TIIR DA Y, lIARCH 4. 1893.</p>
        <p>WIDNES LIBERAL CLUB.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening, a meeting was held in the Assnn- bly Room, of the above club, when an address on Hone Rule was delivered by Mr. E. R. Russell, editor of Lae " Liverpool Daily Pest." Mr. Stuart Deacon preside, and amongst those present were—Messrs. H. Wade-Dea• con, E.K. Muspratt, E. Carey, J. Nlorrison, F. Hartland Dr. J. McLennan, H. IL Shores, &amp;e.</p>
        <p>SALE OF WORK A'r WIDNES. On Thursday afternoon a salo of work was opened in Oakland-street Wesleyan School in aid of the funds of the Widnes Wesleyan circuit. There were four stalls heavily laden with a variety of articles. The opening ceremony was performed by Mr. Councillor Smith, who gave a donation of 25. Donations were also received from Mr. Rogers and Mr. Fl. Wade Deacon. The takings amounted to £69 14s. 3d. The sale was continued yes- terday, when the proceedings were opened by Mr. T. Jackson.</p>
        <p>BETHESDA CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH, RuNcoax. The annual tea party was held on Wednesday night. About 200 were present. The Rev. J. Simpson presided at the entertainment afterwards, when an excellent pro- gramme was sustained by the choir, Mr. Outhvraite's band, Misses Johnson, Ainsworth, and Smith,Mrs. Outh- waite,Messrs.Worrall.F.Smith,and Co.,andC.LeCouteur. Addresses were delivered by the Chairman, Rev. E. R. Mines (Liverpool), Rev. R. G. Hughes (Widnes), and Rev. J. Harris (Stockport). During the evening, the Chairman, on behalf of the congregation, presented a handsome marble timepiece and an *illuminated address to Mr. J. Clough in recognition of his services to the church as organist. Mr. Clough is removing to Liver- pool. Both PRITCHARD'S Shops are under personal supervision .ansl customers may rely upon recei Ong every attention.—Ada.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>SHOCKING .NEGLECT OF CHILDREN AT WIDNES.</head>
        <p>SHOCKING .NEGLECT OF CHILDREN AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Michael Mulligan and Bridget Mulligan, husband and wife of 19 Water-st., were brought before the Widnes Bench on Thursday, charged with .cruelly neglecting their five children, aged respectively 13, 11, 9,5, and one year. Mr. Buckton, of Warrington, prosecuted on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C. The evidence showed a shock- ing state of things. The whole of the children were insufficiently clothed, their skin was in a dreadful filthy condition. Their hair was matted with filth and full of vermin. The children were all very ill-nourished. There was no furniture in the house and an old chaff mattress laid upon the floor and covered with a small dirty quilt served for a bed. The neglect had been going on since May last.—The male prisoner said he gave his wife 255. per week and both protested that they did their utmost for the children.—The Bench imposed a fine of 40s. upon each of the defendants, or in default a months' hard labour. They went to gaol. If you wantyour watch repaired by skilful workmen, take It to PRITCHARD'S.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>RUNCORN RATEPAYERS ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RATEPAYERS ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this association was held on Thursday night in Bethesda schoolroom, for the purpose of electing officers and considering rules. Mr. James Kirkham was elected president ; Rev. Dr. Gastaldi and Mr. R. Roberts vice-presidents ; Mr. 1. Bowyer, treasurer, and Mr. Jas. Langshaw, secretary pro tent. The Committee was con- stituted as follows :—Messrs. J. K. Digges, S. Coventry, J. Lunt, S. Walton, Cooke (Brunswick-street), Cringle, Temperley (Ashridge-street), J. Smith (pawnbroker), George Burrows, W. Collier, T. Booth, D. Worthington, ,J. Byrne, J. Wilson, and T. C. Williams. The objects of the association are to protect the interest of the association by a strict supervision over all local expendi- ture of public money, to record the votes of representa- tives on important questions' to promote the return of representatives unbiassed by political motives to the governing bodies of the town, and to protest purity of voting at elections of commissioners and to guard the interests of the ratepayers generally, and for other purposes condusive to the interests of the town. A code of rules was adopted and then an animated dis- cussion took place on hi proposed street making ad the action of Commissions was severely criticised in several particulars. One grievance was the disturbing of parapets that had only recently been made and passed by the surveyor. A deputation was appointed to wait upon the Commissioners in regard to the matter, and it was arranged that an endeavour should be made to induce the Liberal Association to join in the protest. JOSEPH PRITCHARD is prepared to wind and keep in repair, private clocks from 10a. per annum.,—Adet. PREFERMSKT OF THE VICAR OF •WIDNIS.</p>
        <p>The Rev. G. H. St. Patrick Garrett, 8.D., Vicar of Widnes has been appointed vicar of St. Paul's, Princess Park, Liverpool, in succession to the late Rev. Canon McNeile. The living is worth £6OO. Mr. Garrett took charge of the Vicarage of Widnes, which is worth a little short of £4OO, in 1888, his predecessor being the Rev. J. Wright Williams and during the past few years the parish has been admirably worked, and while offering their congratulations to him on his advancement many will heartily regret his removal from their midst. Al- though not what may be called an eloquent preacher, Mr. Garett has an easy flow of words, aud an extensive vocabulary, and some of his discourses were powerful and inspiring. His sermon to the Mayor and Corpora- tion on the occasion of their first visit to Church some months ago, was a good example of pulpit oratory, and was highly praised by those privileged to hear him. The new vicar of St. Paul's is a man of scholarly attainments and had a distinguished University career. He is a late scholar (1877) of Trinity College, Dublin, and when he i took his B.A. degree n 1878, was classical student, senior moderator classics, and senior moderator mental and moral philosophy. In the following year he was Archbishop King's and Bishop Forster's divinity prize- man, first in each case, while in the two succeeding years he carried off further honours. Ho took his B.A. degree in 1882, and his 8.D., three years later. Ile is a member of the Senate of Trinity College, Dublin. In October last, Mr. Garrett was elected a member of the Widnes School Board, and it is presumed that on his translation to Liverpool he will resign his seat. JOSEPH PRITCHARD has the beet stock of ladies' silver and geld watches in the town.—Advt. RUNCORN SONS OP TEMPERANCE. •</p>
        <p>A meeting of this order was held on Saturday evening under the presidency of Mr. Thomas J. Holt. Tnere was a good attendance of officers and members. An interesting paper was read by Mr. Daniel Bisbrown on " Blaine Providence." He explained the meaning of the word Providence and then proceeded to show that by God's bounty, the wants of the entire animal creation are daily supplied. He stated that righly to understand the providence they must view it in connection with the act of creation, with the fall of man with redeem- tion of the world by Jesus Christ, and with a future state of rewards and punishments. The providence of God upholds and goveras every created thing. Its , operation is as extensive as the universe and as increas- ing as the flow of time. He provideth the raven his food and satisfleth the desire of every living thing. The bible teaches that every occurence is perfectly controlled by him. The history of each man, the rise and fall of nations ; and the progress of the Churah of Christ. The essayist then proceeded to deal with special provid- ence, and showed how consoling it was to the Christian day by day. He referred to the numerous cases in Scriptures, illustrative of a particular providence. God caused the Philistines to enter the land of Isrea I just when Saul was about to sieze David, A blasting providence and bad news from Assyria caused Rabshakeh to turn away from Jerusalem immediately before he and hie army might have taken the city. We have Esther and Mordiem, and Joseph, and other caries almost without number. He then gave instances in which mens' lives had been preserved in a miraculous manner, and con- cluded by asking his hearers to ever have as their motto, "Trust in God and do the right." A discussion ensued in which the following took part : Messrs. Samuel M. Timperley, J. Jones, George E. Sutton, Thomas Clarke, Samuel Henderson, and Thomas Cooke. A vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Bisbrovvn for his paper. WIDNES PETTY SESSIONS. . .</p>
        <p>THURSDAY.—Before Mr. J. BrOCl;nd"..ir. J. Lister.</p>
        <p>- MISCELLANEOCIS.—Geo. Turner, Wm. Platt and Fred. Truseott, were each fined for drunkenness.—For commit- ting breaches of the peace Catherine Ecob, Eleanor Johnson, Catherine Wickham, Jas. Mullen, Peter Fox and Wm. Butler were bound over in the usual sureties. Chas. Butterfield, formerly of Beamont-st., now at Grimsby, wag charged with deserting his wife and family, but his friends had made an arrangement with the Guardians and the case was withdrawn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>BRUTAL ASSAULT ON Tan Blur CANAL.—On Wednesday' at Frodsham, John Burgoine and Henry Raine were charged with'a brutal assault on George Davies at mid- night on the 21st ult., on the Ship Canal Works, Trod- sham Marsh. The prosecutor spoke to being attacked by the prisoners and three other men. He received serious wounds on the head and face. The melee lasted a quarter of an hour. The bench decided to deal with , the case summarily, and fined £3 and costs, and Burgoine £1 and costs. SHOCKING INGIZATITUDH.—At Frodsliam Police Court, on Wednesday, before Mr. A. Thomas and Dr. Steele, Jane Hempel was charged with burglariouslv breaking Out of the dwelling house of Sarah Roberts, Main-street, Frodsham, and stealing 10s. in money and a number of articles, value £l, between 10 p.m. on November 20th and 5 a.m. on the 21st. On the afternoon of November 20th the prisoner came to Mrs. Roberts' house with two children. She supplied them with a bottle of ginger beer, and afterwards provided dinner for them. They asked to stay all night, and were permitted to do so. Next morning, the goods mentioned had been taken. The prisoner was committed to prison for a month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>LM M.</head>
        <p>LM M.</p>
        <p>POPULAR SERVICES FOR TITS PEOPLE.—The last of series of popular services promoted by the Wesleyan body, was held on Sunday night, when an evangelistic service was held, conducted by Mr. 11.0. Brigg, who was assisted by Mr. Morgan, Connexional evangelist. All the services have been well attended, and greatly appre- ciated. Solos were sung by Miss D. Warburton and Mrs. Hallam in an excellent manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT</head>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT</p>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>BY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>To many people in Widnes the announcement that the Rev. G. H. Garrett had been appointed to St. Paul's, Liverpool, came as a surprise, but it was generally known amongst Churchmen that he was in the "running" for the more richly endowed liv- ing. On the whole the rev. gentleman has had a successful four years in chemical town, and he has had the gratification of seeing the Parochial Build- ings for Simms Cross district an accomplished fact.</p>
        <p>There is nothing in law to prevent Mr. Garrett retaining the seat on the School Board which he obtained in October last,but he will no doubt follow the usual course, and when he severs his connec- tion with the town, resign his seat on the Board. The appointment of a successor would then rest with the Board, who according to custom, would appoint Major Wareing, one ot the same party, who was an unsuccessful candidate, to fill the vacancy. Major Wareing's return would be hailed with satis- faction.</p>
        <p>The unqualified success of the lecture on the Ship Canal on Tuesday evening, will give Runcorn Free Library Committee not only encouragement to con- tinue in the course they had mapped out, but would provide them with some of that most useful com- modity, money, with which to carry out their ideas. The previous lectures had not been largely patron- ised either by those for whom they were intended as a means of instruction in an interesting way, or by those in other walks of life, but on Tuesday evening, the room was crowded to excess by an ap- preciative audience. The local interest of the sub- ject was no doubt a great cause in this improved attendance. Those who are inclined to take a pes- simistic view of the effect of the Ship Canal on the trade of Runcorn, and of the future of the ancient town itself, would find nothing either in the lecture or the subsequent interesting speeches to confirm them in their gloomy forebodings. By the aid of the Skip Canal and the vehicular bridge (when that latter most devoutly wished for convenience is pro- vided) Runcorn may yet hope to crown its long and eventful history by becoming a city.</p>
        <p>No organisations have done more for the spread of musical knowledge in Runcorn than those organised and carrisd on by our talented tonic-sol- fa teacher. Mr. J. HoHord, and it was a source of re- gret that his efforts to cater for the public were so poorly rewarded as they were at the concert on Monday evening. The quality of the concert was worthy of a bigger" house." The performences of his choir are ever a source of keen enjoyment to those who take advantage of the opportunity to hear them. Several circumstances, no doubt, mili- tated against a large audience on that evening, and, on the whole, Mr. Holford and his choir have had a successful season, especially from a musical point of view, so that they may look forward to their ninth season with hope and confidence of better things. The National Footpath Preservation Society have just issued their eighth annual report. We in this neighbourhood have not always been free from the obstruction of footpaths and the filching of common land, and this report shows that there is a very extensive "tratle"of this kind carried on in the country. No fewer than 158 cases of interference with public footpaths and encroachments on road- side wastes have come to the knowledge of the society during the past year, making a total of 880 since the formation of the society eight years ago. The society ham not acted in all these cases, but as the report points out, the list shows the need of watchfulness and the great necessity for putting such matters under the care of some department of State. _</p>
        <p>The cases cited show that all manner of devices are resorted to by some land owners to obtain pos- session of common land or to block up footpaths. It is stated to be a common practice in Wales to place a bull in a field through which the publicl footpath runs, but we are not free from the same, nuisance and danger in this district. 1 have had to make a long detour in Preston Brook, in conse- quence of the same practice, when accompanied by ladies who were too nervous to pass a treacherous beast of that description. I agree with the report that any new bill relating to footpaths should have a clause making it illegal for such dangerous animals to roam at large in fields having a public footpath. One gentleman who claimed a common in Somerset hit upon the villainous method of stop- ping trespassers by laying down iron or steel fish hooks of large size in a plantation which formed part of the common. These fish hooks were in pairs and fixed to iron wire, only four inches asund- der. The inhabitants sustained their right to the common, and the judge severely condemned the use of such a cruel instiument in any place where men children or even dogs might stray. In yorkshire a mill owner has twice put up formidable obstruction with the object of stopping a right of way through his mill yard, which the people have as promptly demolished, and he then hit upon the novel method of squirting scalding water and steam on those who used the way, which fortunately for himself he did not continue long. -- I The Society is doing a good work in causing a watchful eye to be kept upon public rights in various parts ot the country, and the members in- clude the Duke of Westminster as patron, the Rt. , I Hon. Lord Ribblesdale, as president, and all classes and conditions of men with Local Boards, Corpora-1 Itions,Ratepayers' Associations,Naturaliste Societys,, Field Clubs, &amp;c. The total number of members is 914, of which 126 are public bodies. The Society is ever on the watch to assist those who defend pub- 1 lie rights. For years our Commissioners have been profess- ing that they were disgusted with the antiquated I system by which they were elected, and that they would hail the introduction of the ballot with grat- itude, but after their proceedings on Thursday there , is only one conclusion to be arrived at, and that is that their professions are as hollow as a drum. A 'short clause in the Water Bill would have been sufficient, but that was neglected, and on Thursday Mr. Humphreys gave them a chance to remedy the defect by petitioning to have the Local Authorities Bill amended to include Boards of Commissioners. This bill as the report in another column confers he ballot and , other privileges. The motion was deliberately shelved for a month. The Commissioners are nut always so punctilious of observing statutes and standing orders as they were on this occasion, but the ruling of the Law Clerk served the turn of a number of ratepayers representatives (?) and was gladly accepted. The fact is that the gentlemen knew that when the ballot is swept away the system of cumulative voting,the ballot means" one man one vote" in local affairs, and they would appear to think that such a reform might not result as they desire at the polling booth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>RUNCORN TONIC-SOL-FA CHOIR CON –</head>
        <p>RUNCORN TONIC-SOL-FA CHOIR CON -</p>
        <p>CERT.</p>
        <p>The third concert of the season was given in Runcorn Public Hall on Monday evening, and was on the whole of a very pleasing character. It was a regrettable circumstance that the aUdienee Wad not very numerous, and that the financial results of the concert were not commensurate with the enter- prise of the choir. The programme submitted was of a miscellaneous character, and the artistes engaged were Madame Madge Morgans (soprano), Miss Maude Yates , (contralto), Mr. Seymour Jackson (tenor), and Mr. V. L. Needham (solo flute). The choir, who were led as usual by their hOn Conductor Mr. J. Belford, L.T.S.C., fully maintained their reputation. Their rendering of several part songs and madrigal " Who shall win" were a never failing source of enjoyment, and the com- pany was not slow to recognise the ability of the mem- bers by generous applause. Their voices blended beau- tifully and time and tune were very exact especially considering that in none of the items had the choir the services of an accompanist. The part songs given were " There is music by the River," (Pinsuti) • " Come Madoline," (Kuckeer) ; " Break, break, break, on thy cold grey stories," (Macfarren) ; and" Good-night thou glorious sun," (Smart). Madame Margans contributed "Children's dreams ' (Cowen), and "The flight of ages" (Bevan), and she sang with much taste and expression. In the latter song she treated the audience to a delightful piece of vocalisation, and in response to an unanimous encore she gave " The Better land." Miss Yates gave " Carmena " (Lane Wilson), " The Moorish Maid" (H. Parker), and two quaint old Irish songs ",Live in my heart " and " 'Tis time I should talk to your mother. In the latter songs Miss Yates' pleasing voice was heard to great advantage and she was deserv- edly recalled. Mr. Jackson proved highly successful in the item, set to his name, "The Sailors grave" (Sulli- van), and "lii Old Madrid" (Trotere)—and he was loudly applauded for each rendering. The programme also included the duet" Maying," by Madame Morgans and Mr. Jackson : the duet "I heard a voice" by Madame Morgans and Miss Yates, the duet "Evening' by Miss Yates and Mr. Jackson, and the trio" 0 Memory" in which the three artistes took part. One of the treats of the evening were the performances of Mr. Needham on the flute, and he was vociferously encored £r" Carnival de Venice." his own composition, and he responded to their call. Mrs. W.B. Kay was the accorn- . pail*</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF IMPROVEMENT COMMISSIONERS.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting was held at the Town Hall, on Thursday morning. Mr. F. Wiswall presided, and there were present Messrs. W. Fisher, H. J. Grice, S. Taylor, D. Bisbrown, T. Lea. E. W. Humph- reys, W. Hankinson, H. Coward, R. Abel, J.W.Light- burn, J. Pritchard, P. Speakman, C.C., F. A. Lake (law clerk), E. Marshall (secretary), Dr. McDougall (medical officer), and M. Barker (surveyor).</p>
        <p>MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>The Medical Officer reported that during the past month the deaths amounted to 31. Of these deaths 2 were inquest cases, and of the remaining 29 deaths 3 were due to smallpox, 3 to pneumonia, 7 to bronchitis, 3 to natural decay, 2 to mesenteric disease. 2 to debility, and one each to convulsions, phthisis, scarlatina,kidney disease, imperfect respiration, abscess, heart disease, apoplexy, and inflammation of the lining membrane of the brain. The 31 deaths show an average death rate of 1811 of the estimated population and exclusive of the inquest cases of 1704 for the twelve mouths.—The Sur- veyor said in the corresponding period last year there were 51 deaths.—The Chairman said it appeared to be a very satisfactory report.</p>
        <p>BUILDING OPERATIONS. The Highway Committee recommended that the plans of Mr.Thornas Holmes for the erection of a house in Green- way-road, and Mr. T. Parry for the erection of a house, shop, and stable in Baliour-st., and of Mr. Henry White for the erection of seven houses in Queen's-road be ap- proved.—'The minutes were passed on the motion of Mr. Paylor.</p>
        <p>DISTRUCTION OF CLOTHING- The Health Committee recommended—(a)—That the following sums be paid as compensation for the destruc- tion of infected bedding, wearing apparel,&amp;c.—£B 9s 6d. to Samuel Hughes, 58, Sewell-street ; £1 15s , to John Hughes, 49, Sewell-street ; £1 65., to Henry Whitehouse, 80, Canal-street ; £1 65., to Richard Evans, 25, Grove- street ; £1 6s. to Themes Peach, 109, Church-street, In moving the adoption of the minute, Mr. Lightburn said that in a short time instead of destroying property of this kind, they would probably send it to Dutton where there was a disinfecting machine, which had been pur- chased by the rural authority at a cost of several hun- dred pounds. It would then be unnecessory to destroy clothing.—The minute was passed.</p>
        <p>MN VEHICULAR ERIDOE, The Mersey Fridge Committee recommended that the Board in conjunction with the Widnes Corporation pay the preliminary expenses of Sir Douglas Fox's in res- pect of the proposed Vehicular Bridge across the Mer- sey. Terms—fifty guineas and travelling expenses.— The Chairman moved the adoption of the minute, and said it was necessary to pass such a resolution because he had received a letter from Mr. Campbell H. Sadler of the County Offices, Preston, in which he said "The Lancashire sub-committee will require in the meantime to be furnished with the detailed estimate of the total cost of the Bridge,the construction of the approaches to the Bridge and of the land therefore will you be good enough to obtain and send the estimate to ma as soon as possible, as the sub-committee feel that until they are in possession of the full cost of the scheme they are un- able to deal further with the application." The Com- mittee had. therefore unanimously decided to pay one half of the expense of Sir Douglas Fox making a full estimate for the construction of the Bridge and other matters incidental to it.</p>
        <p>Mr. Fred C. Hußon, clerk of Lancashire County Coun- cil had also written informing the Commissioners that a conference between the sub-Committee of the counties of Lancashire nd Cheshire for the purpose of further considering the proposed scheme for erecting a vehicular bridge over the river Mersey, between Widnes and Runcorn, would be held at the County Session House, Islington Flags, Liverpool, on Tuesday, the 7th inst., at 12 o'clock noon. The attendance of representatives of the Commissioners was requested. They had so far been able to proceed without incurring any expense but it was now necessary to spend a little money in order to get the matter properly dealt with. The Committee felt that the next meeting of the County Councils would decide the question whether they would or would not get the Vehicular Bridge, so that the matter was of the utmost importance.—Mr. Grine seconded the motion and expressed pleasure that the matter was having so serious consideration from various local authorities. It was of vital importance to Runcorn and Widnes locally, but it was also of great importance to the two counties and he had also a strong impression that it was a subject of national importance. He hoped the consideration by the County Council would he favourable to the projeet. —Mr. Bisbrown said such a bridge would be an inestim- able boon not only to the trading people but to the com- munity at large. He took it that after the initial stages were passed that a meeting of ratepayers would be called to consider the project fully, and determine what further steps should be taken.—The minutes were passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>LOCAL AUTHORITIES BILL BLOCKING TEE BALLOT.</head>
        <p>THE LOCAL AUTHORITIES BILL BLOCKING TEE BALLOT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wynne Humphreys moved " That the seal of the Board he affixed to a petition to the House of Commons praying that the Local Authorities (Voting and Quali- fication) Bill—being a Bill to amend the law relating to voting for and to the qualification of members and electors of Local Authorities—be so extended as to apply Ito and include Boards of Improvement Commissioners, and that the same, so amended and extended, may be passed into law during the present session of Parlia- ment."—" That copies of the foregoing resolution he sent to ths Prime Minister, the Home Secretary, and the President of the Local Government Board."—The Bill which at present applies only to Boards of Guardians and Local Boards of Health provides for (inter alia), 1, voting by ballot; 2, abolition of plural voting ; 3, nomination of candidates; 4, medification of rating qualification of membership of Local Authorities ; 5, a register of duly qualified electors ; 6, women to be qualified for membership of Local Authorities ; 7, the application of the provisions of the Municipal Elections (Corrupt and Illegal Practices) Act, 1884 to elections of Local Authorities.—Mr. T. Lea seconded the motion and pointed out that Mr. Brunner, M.P., had given notice of an amendment which Would give effect to the proposed petition. If they passed it, it would strengthen his hands.—Mr. Lightburn said there were many things in the Bill he was in per- fect harmony with, but he thought they ought to have had longer notice.—The Chain:Lein : I think it would have been better if we had had longer notice.—Mr. Grice said this was extraordinary business, and the Board ought to have had a full month's notice.—Mr.Tay- lor : Either it is in order or is it not. If it is in order let us give a vote ; if it is otherwise we have no right to pass it.—The Law Clerk : This being extraordinary busi- ness you are entitled to a month's notice in writing.__ Mr. Humphreys moved that the standing orders be sus- pended. Be did not think it was fair for any member to say he had not had proper notice. es a matter of practice it was ordinarily held that an: notice which gave five clear days before the Board meeting, and ap- peared on the agenda paper, was sufficient. This was an urgent matter.—The Chairman : I am informed that the Bill is not likely to pass as the Government are bringing in the District Council's Bill which will deal wittrit .—Mr. Humphreys: It will rot. He added that the Government had adopted th:s Bill and it had passed a second reading.—Mr. BisbrOwn seconded the suspen- sion of the standing orders. He said there was every possibility that this measure would become law, and it was of urgent importance that the Commissioners should let it be understood that they were anxious to be in- cluded in the Bill. They could not speak of the present mode of voting for Commissioners in language too scathing ;it was an insult to the ratepayers ; and if they could by any means get the ballot eeopted, they were justified in doing so.—The Chr..rtnac : I am advised that the resolution to suspend the standing orders is of no use.—The Law Clerk: You will have to wait a month. Unfortunately you are tied down by statute and standing orders that there must be a month's notice given.—Mr. Humphreys : But the Cernmissioners can pass the resolution for what it is worth ? --The Law Clerk : Yes.—Mr. Humphreys : Then I ;brow upon the gentlemen who object to pass it the responsibility of ' it. He gave notice that he wwuld move the resolution at the next monthly meeting. ItIYNCOIIN WiIER BILL. The Chair Man moved in pursuance of an undertaking given at a meeting of owners and ra'epayers held in the Bethesda Lecture Hall on the 16. h day of February, 1893. " That it be an instruction to the Finance and Parliamentary Committees that before settling terms with the Runcorn, Weston, and Halton Waterworks Company for the purchase of their undertaking, the proposed terms be submitted to a meeting of owners and ratepayers to be convened hereafter by the Chairman of the Beard.—Mr. Humphreys : Does this require a montlfe notice.—The Law Clerk ; I think not,—Mr. Humphreys said he asked for the reason that he did not wish to be sat upon again by Mr. Once. Ile seconded the motion.—Mr. Lightburn said he considered it rather hard that they should have to be placed in the unfor- tunate position of submitting tea vote which could only be answered at on a very cursory and limited amount of information, which had cost the menthols scores of hours to investigate. It seemed to imply a want of confidence which he did not appreciate.—Mr. Bisbrown said he t hought the ratepayers ought to have the opportunity of expressing their opinion upon this question or any other important question.—The motion was carried. THE PROPOSED ELECTRIC LIGHTING. The Electric Lighting Commit tee recommended—(a) That Messrs. Hall and Co., be authorised to put down an experimental installation of electric light at a sum not to exceed twenty-five guineas, such SUM to be merged in the contract price in the event of the af ore- Said firm being entrusted with the installation of the Electric Light for the whole of the public streets.—Mr. Humphreys moved the adoption of the minutes and asked the Board to approach the matter without any preconceived ideas, as to whether it was feasible, and without prejudice from the quarter whence it emanated. . The Committee made the suggestion, for the purpose of giving the Board and the ratepayers an opportunity of contrasting the light given by gas and electricity,and if, after the preliminary installation was tried and report , proved satisfactory,it would probably be the duty of the . committee to recommend that a full and detailed estim- ate be obtained. With regard to the information they •</p>
        <p>had been able to obtain, he explained that Mr. Mar- shall had communicated with several corporations. The replies from Brighton, Harrogate, Bury, and Bourne- mouth, were to the effect that they were not utilizing electricity for public lighting. Blackpool, Hull, and Dundee were, or had laid it down for public lighting,but their experience did not warrant them in giving any de- tailed information between the merits of gas and electricity. There was one distinct reply unfavoui able, namely, from Leamington, which said that they had discontinued electric lighting, and returned the gas, the cost having proved about double, and the incandescent lamp not sufficiently strong. With regard to those who presented a favourable report there was Hastings, who said they were putting down further machinery, and they hoped the light in future would be more satisfac- tory. They had also received two communication. from Dolgelly,where the town had been lighted under the principle he was ask*. the Board to adopt, namely, that of Messrs. A. Hall and Co. These spoke of an advantage with regard to both price and illuminating power. The installation was an un- qualified success in both respects, and the opinion was expressed that if local authorities would take the light- ing of their districts in their own hands, they would be able to supply better light and at a cheaper rate by electricity than by gas. If the proposal he had sug- gested were adopted, they would in the first place obtain a full and detailed estimate, showing what in the opin- ion of the firm was the necessary plant required for the lighting of the town,accompanied by which there would be a specific contract to put the plant down at a fixed sum. He thought there would be an actual saving of from £lOO to £l5O per annum in the lighting bill of the town if electricity were adopted, and the illumination would be increased. If so the ratepayers had a right to demand that the Board should probe the matter to the fullest extent.</p>
        <p>Mr. flankinson seconded the motion in order that it might be discussed. Mr. Lightburn did not exactly understand what Mr. Humphreys meant when he said he hoped the subject would be carefully considered irrespective of the direc- tion from whence it came. So far as he knew, they did not act in that room in the interest of any party, but for the welfare of Runcorn.</p>
        <p>Mr. Taylor said the subject was new to the members, rho had had no experienoe as to the utility or the cost of electric lighting. If electricity would be an advant- age to the town financially, he was prepared to go in for it as strongly as any member on that Board ; but he must say that he could not fall in with the recommenda- tion that they spend 25 guineas until they had more in- formation. He coula not hear of any town that had adopted electricity with success, with the exception of Dolgelly, where they had only 50 lamps, and he believed the length of streets would not be more than half a mile. He characterised the proposal as the thin end of the wedge, which might lead them into serious expenditure. The Chairman said there was a proposed installation of electric lighting by the Manchester Corporation, practically in the centre of the city, for premises only, and the cost was made out to be 4s. as against 3s. for gas, for the same volume of light. The Corporation would make no profit from it, and it was simply being done for the public good.—Mr. Lightbura said great credit was due to Mr. Humphreys for bringing the mat- ter forward. It was patent to all that at present they had not full information, and he trusted they would watch the results of electric lighting elsewhere.—Mr. Bisbrown failed to see that a case had been made out to take the suggested step.—Mr. Grice did not see that the information brought before them was sufficient to en- able them to proceed with definiteness thst morning. —The Chairman agreed with this view. At the same, time, he said it must not be understood that they were going to allow the matter to drop. They would prose- cute the work until they could get something like a fair estimate of what is meant.—Mr. Humphreys, replying to the discussion stated that be had previously breaght before the Board a provisional estimate showing that by adopting electric lighting they would experience a sav- ing of something like £250 per annum. He, however, allowed for an increased expenditure, and even then the saving amounted to id. in the £on the rates. He did not believe that the ratepayers would begrudge the ex- penditure of £25 or £35 in order to further a bona-fide and legitimate attempt to reduce the rate. With regard to Mr. Lig,htburn's remark, he used the expression be- cause he thought there was a likelihood that any scheme he brought forward would be opposed, and on that account he was anxious not to be the chairman of the committee. He, however, was prepared to leave the matter to the judgment of the ratepaye:s, and he might add that he did not intend to let the matter drop. Mr. Lightburn was pleased to hear the explanation, but so fir as he knew, that Board was willing to give fair play to any member, and lie personally was pre- pared to prevent anything in the shape of boycotting. The resolution was lost by eight votes to four.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>MAYORAL "AT HOME" AT WIDNES..</head>
        <p>MAYORAL "AT HOME" AT WIDNES..</p>
        <p>The Mayor and Mayoress of Widnes (Ahlsrman and Mrs. F. H. Gosisage) held their first "at home" on Thursday night. As the Town Hall exists only in name, the buildings designated by that name comprising little beyond the public offices, the Mayor found it necessary to obtain another build- ing for the purpose of holding the first public func- tion of the newly created borough. Lacey-street Wesleyan Schoolroom was secured and on the whole answered ,its purpose admirably. In the hands of messrs. Wilkins and Co., of the Lan- cashire and Yorkshire Furnishing Stores, Sea- forth, and of Mr. R. P. Ker and Sons of Aigburth Nursery, the interior of the building had under- gone a transforinacion. The platform where the band was placed, presented the appearance of a tropical garden huge palms, dracenas, crotons and other foliage plants being artistically arranged there. At the base were placed in charming pro- fusion flowering plants in greatvariety, hyacinths and cinaraia, being prominent. Flowers and plants were also skilfully placed in other portions of the room. The walls were partly draped, and a con- siderable portion was covered with &amp;Adds and trophies of flags, and rainbow-lined fans were used with much effect. White curtains draped the windows,and the doors of the nuinevous clam-rooms were hidden beneath tapestry curtains. The floor was covered with richly coloured crim- son cloth. At the lower end, near the entrance a corner of the room had been cunningly converted into the representation , of a drawing room, and it was here that the Mayor land Mayoress received their numerous guests. A ' Brussels carpet covered the floor, and furniture of a handsome design was provided. The walls of this portion were altogether hidden by Chinese um- brellas, fans, and a large mirror reflected the brillant light which had been fixed by Mr. Carr for ' the occasion. The room altogether presented a scene of oriental magnificence, and when the guests had arrived, the idea was given of a moving panorama of gaiety. Every arr eigement had been made for the comfort of the guests, and the large committee room was used as a refreshment room, the catering being en- trusted to Mr. G. A. Foster, of the Central Hotel. The entire arrangements had been in the hands of Mr. H. S. Oppenheim, the Town Clerk, and his as- sistant' Mr. T. Craik, to whom every credit is due. There had been 550 in vitations issued, of which nearly 400 were accepted, and the guts as they entered the room, were announced by Mr. Whittle, and received by the Mayor and Mayoress, who gra- ciously shook hands with their visitors. The plea- sure of the evening was enhanced by the perform- ances of Evton's Liverpool Badd. The proceedings continued until half-past ten, when the Mayor and party returned home by special train from the Central Station, which .called at all stations to Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. ISAAC SPEAKMAN, OF RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. ISAAC SPEAKMAN, OF RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>It is with deep regret that we have to record the death of Mr. Isaac Speakman, chemist and druggist, of Bridge-at., Runcorn, which took place at two o'clock yesterday morning. A week ago Mr. Speak- man caught a chill, which subsequently developed into pleurisy, and he appeared to be making satis- factory progress towards recovery until Wednesday when pneumonia supervened, and his condition became exceedingly critical. Dr. Robinson was . called into consultation with tar. Mangan's - assist- ant, and Dr. Glyn was sent for, and that eminent physician pronounced the case hopeless oa Thursday evening, and death ensued at the time stated. It was not generally known that Mr. Speakman was seriously ill and when the news of his death became circulated yesterday, universal regret was expressed, that one so highly esteemed and respected should have been cut off in the prime of inanheod. He had attained the age of only 44 years. Mr. Speakman served his apprenticeship with our respected townsman Mr. Wm. Whittaker, who has long been retired from business, and in 1858 the deceased gentleman commenced an establishment of his own. Just about this time the Pharmacy Act came into operation and Mr. Speakman passed the.. necessary examinations with great credit. Lie also successful in passing the tests of the Apothe- caries Hall and became a qualified dispenses of medicines. He had built up a flourishing business, and although he took no part in public affairs.i he easi7oans deservedly esteemed for his kindliness of disposition thestte Mr. and upright character. He was the n Thomas Speakman, for many years colliery manager for Messrs. John and Thomas Johnson, and he is the brother of Mr. P. Speakman C.C., and Mr. _J. J. Speak- man of Stanley Villas. The deceased gentleman leaves one son, aged twelve years who was away at school at Lytham, when the sad event occurred. The funeral. will take place on Monday, and he will be committed to the grave wherein lies buried his wife and one son, at, ernithdown Cemetery, Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 4, 1893.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 4, 1893.</p>
        <p>A DESERTER.—Joseph Howard, was charged with be- ing a leserter from the Bth King's Regiment stationed at Salford, on March Ist, P.C. Turner said that he ar- rested prisoner because he , wore military boots. When brought to the Police Station prisoner admitted being a deserter from the Bth Mine.. Prisoner was remanded to await an escort, and P.C. Turner was granted the usual reward.</p>
        <p>Ammeas RENT.—John Griffin, 4, Haydock-st., was charged that on the 17th Feb., he left certain premises held by him, unlawfully carrying off goods and chattels, to prevent the landlord, Mr. J. C. Sutton, distrainiag them for arr Pars in rent. Mr. Holbrook said that defen- dant owed 17s. rent, and had taken about 50s. in goads. The case was adjourned for seven dlys, to give defen- dant time to pay the rent and cost of the summons.</p>
        <p>COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS. WRDNESDAT.—Befure Mr. John White (in the chair),and Mr. S. D. Armitage. PROSECUTIONS TINDER THE WEIGHTS AND MEASURES Act —John Warburton, Great Sankey, for not having a quantity of measures stamped on Feb. 13th, was fined 10s. and costs.—John Johnsen, Fiddlers Ferry, was fined 15s. and costs, for having a quantity of measures un- stamped, and a number of measures abort measure, on on Jan. 13th.</p>
        <p>CROSS SUMMONS FOR Assaufm—Bridget Feeny and William Feeny were each charged with assaulting and beating Elizabeth Kinsella at Burt-mwood on Jan. 4th. There was also a cross-summons against Elizabeth Kinsella, on the charge of assaulting Bridget Feeny on the same date, Mr. Arthur Browne appeared for Mrs. Kinsella, and Mr. Percy Davies for the two Feenys. Mr. Browne said Feeny and his wife worked at Mr. War- burton's Farm, Hallam. Mrs. Kinsella lived onthe farm, and went night and morning to the farm to milk. On the day of the alleged assault, Mrs. Kinsella went into the yard for the purpose of milking. She was taking some provender to the cows when Mrs. Feeny came up and said" I am Mrs. here, you shall not go through the door first." She then poured a bucket of provender over Mrs. Kinsella's head, and then hit her head with the bucket, causing it to bleed. Mrs. Kinsella then had her head washed in Mr. Warburton's house. When she came out again, Bridget Feeny bit her again with a can. The husband then came up and told his wife to give it her. Mrs. Feeny then took hold of Mrs. Kinsella's hair, and gave her two blows in the face. Mrs. Kinsella in return took hold of Mrs. Feeny's hair, when the husband came up, and took Mrs. Kinsella by the neck bending it, and caused it to crack. He then knocked her head against the wall. A man named Daly then came and separated them. Mrs. Kinsella then went away, but had to stop at a house, where she became unconscious, and the next she remembered was when she was in the Infirmary. She was in the Infirmary three weeks.— Mrs. Kinsella corroborated her solicitor's statement.—Dr. Boaden said that Mrs. ' Kinselle was admitted into the Infirmary on January sin a semi-conscious state. She had bruises on the head, and there was concussion of the neck.—Maud Gibson and Eliza Collins, and P.C. Groark each gave evidence in support of Mrs. Kinsella. —Mr. Percy Davies said that Mrs. Kinselle had brought the whole thing on to herself. He said that she was jealous of Mrs. Feeny, because Mr. Kinsella is not employed at Mr. Warburton's farm. On the day of the alleged offence, Mrs. Kinsella commenced a quarrel with Mrs. Feeny over the provender. They struggled on the flcor for the bucket, and at length Mrs. Feeny got possession of it. Mrs. Feeny denied giving Mrs. Kinsella a blow on the head with the bucket. They afterwards had each other by the hair, and the husband separated them, and Mrs. Kinsella might have caught her head against the wall.—Jonathan Worral denied that Feeny knocked Mrs. Kinsella's head against the wall.—The magistrates were satisfied that an assault had been com- mitted by the Feeny's and they were fined 30s. including costs. The case against Mrs. Kinsella was dismissed.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS. MONDAY.—Before Mr. J. Brundret and Mr. J. Handley. DOG AT LARGE.—Walter Cartwright, of Norman-road, was summoned for unlawfully allowing a dog to be at large unmuzzled.—Defendant pleaded that the 'dog was not his. It was his son's dog. Mr. Cartwright said he would appear for his son, and a fine of is. and costa was imposed. Scaooz, ArremaNCE.—Eighteen persons were sum- moned for not sending their children regularly to school and the usual fines of ss. and costs was imposed in each case.</p>
        <p>ADM/it/CT/VS VAGRANT.—WiIIiam Jones was charged with destroying clothing belonging to the Runcorn Union, at the Workhouse, Dutton, on the previous day. Mr. Cunnah proved the case.—Committed to gaol for seven days. USING 0/39CENH LANGH7AGE.—Mary Hulse was charged under a warrant with using obscene language OR the 23rd December.—She was ordered to pay is. and 14s. costs.</p>
        <p>WIDNES POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>MONDAY. —Before Messrs. J. Bruck and H. Wade Deacon.</p>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS—David Campbell, Chester-street; Catherine Ramsdale, Runcorn, and Edward Rooney, Wavering-street, were fined for this offence. Sureprxa IN CHEMICAL WORKS.—Michael Manmon and John Smith, no fixed residence, were charged with sleep- ing in Hutchinson's chemical works, on Saturday night. —On promising not to repeat the offence, defendants were discharged. Boy's FIRST Them—Thomas 'Hooligan, aged 11 Margaret-street, was charged with stealing 410. from the person of Murial Johnson, who stated, that she lived with her parents at 12, Albion-street. On Saturday night she was sent to the Waterloo Vaults, for a gill of beer, for which her father gave her sixpence. She oh- tained the beer and 40. change. Prisoner was in the vaults at the time, and he followed her oat, snatched the money from her hand and ran away.—Prisoner in answer to the charge, said "1 took the money, but I don't know wby."—Defendant was ordered to receive six strokes. THE DRINK Din IT.—A seaman named Thomas Coornbes, James-street, Widnes, was placed in the dock charged with having unlawfully assaulted and beaten his wife on the 24th inst.—Prisoner pleaded guilty. He was bound over to be of good behaviour for six months.</p>
        <p>DAHESBTJRY PETTY SESSIONS. TITESDAT.—Before Mr. W. E. Jones (chairman) Messrs. William Long, Hy. Nield, and H. Talbot. .</p>
        <p>ALLOWING Dons TO ea UNMIIZZLED.—Georgina Ry- lands, Thelwall, pleaded guilty to allowing eight dogs to be at large unmuzzled. Fined Is. for each dog.— Charles Moran, 27, Silver-st., Warrington, was charged, with allowing a dog to be at large unmuzzled at Stock- ton Heath. The case was dismissed.</p>
        <p>NEGLECTING TO SEND CHILDREN TO ScHoor...—William Henry Owen, Walton Inferitw, for neglecting to send three of his children regularly to school was tined 3s. 6d. for each child.</p>
        <p>TIM RAMS HEAD, GRAPPHNHALL.—ME. W.OWSTI, arch- itect, submitted plans on behalf of Messrs. Greenell, Whitley and Co., Limited, for the proposed new Rams Head Inn, Grappenhall, in the place of the present pre- mises. The Bench sanctioned the alteration.</p>
        <p>NEWTON PETTY SESIONS.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.—Before Messrs.</p>
        <p>T. D. Grirnko Drayton and</p>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS.—For two offences committed on the 4th and sth Feb., Frederick Parkin was fined 10s. and Costs—Win. Rooney, who was charged with drunken- ness on the 15th Feb. was fined 20s. and costs. The defendant who made his 19th appearance said that he was not drunk, but he considered that so long as the police persisted in tollowing him about and promis- cuously charging him with drunkenness,he should never be able to keep out of the Court. BREACHES OF THE PEACE.—Wm. Tracey, and Andrew</p>
        <p>Athertm were summoned for fighting on the licensed remis-)spf Thomas Foster, of the Rsais Head, -on the lath Feb. A constable stated that he was called into the deftndants who were fighting on the floor of the taproon. Defendants were bound over to keep the peace for six months in the sum of £5 each. For a effence, on the 13th, Thomas Brennan and James Raglan were also bound over in £5 each to keep 'the .peaw.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>SCENES AT NEWTON SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>SCENES AT NEWTON SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>CALLED OVER THE COALS.</p>
        <p>LANDLORD EDWARDSON'S PREDICAMENT.</p>
        <p>When Mrs. Elizabeth RobinsOn, a married woman of Buttonwood, stooped into the complainant's box at the Newton Sessions on Saturday, she had two black eyes, which she did not appreciate for " their loveliness," as did the lady in the song. She said that one of her neighbours, Mrs. Martha Jones, had ungenerously bestowed the facial decoration upon her,and she wanted the Bench to rensard Mrs. Jones for her gilt. , "But how did she do it?" asked the Magistrates' Clerk.</p>
        <p>So Mrs. Robinson commenced to tell her story. On the 15th she went to the back door to pay a carter who ' had " tipped " a load of coal at her back gate. In the ' midst of her business transaction she was surprised to hear a voice—not "a still small" one—call "I want you ; I want to give you something for calling me names." She looked round and observed that the owner of the voice was Mrs. JoneS. The latter individual then remarked, with a few feminine expletives, that she would " bounce" Mrs. Robinson, whereupon that lady tried to climb over the coals. This might have been a reasonable action considering that she was being "called over the coals" ; but she didn't succeed, be- cause Mrs. Robinson laid hold of her back hair, and they wrestled on the top of the fuel until Mrs. Robinson's daughter came to the rescue. By this time, one of the two parties had lifted up a cob of coal—each said it was the other, and the daughter could not throw any more light upon it, because she was only in time to see it fall upon her mother's nose. Anyhow, when Mrs. Robinson got her back hair loose she ran into her yard and found she had received two black eyes, which she emphatically attributed to the manner in which Mrslones had struck her. She said Mrs. Jones often came for a quarrel, but as Mrs. Robinson wasn't a fighting woman, she always "got left." Mrs. Jones, who spoke the. Lancashire dialect rather strongly, said the complainant was in the habit of call- ing her names. When she asked her what she did it for Mrs. Robinson called her some more, and to use her own parlance "pooed my yead." Now, Mrs. Jones said, she could put up with insults,but not with added injury, therefore, she climbed on the coals after her neighbour and "hit her back—and that was all I done."</p>
        <p>Several neighbours gave evidence that "one was as bad as the other," and after the parties had argued irre- levant questions tilt the Bench WU tired, the defend- ant was fined 2s. Bd. and costs.</p>
        <p>In the next case Adam gdwardson, landlord of the Millstone Inn, fiolborne, was charged with being drunk on his licensed premises on the ath inst. He was repre- sented by Mr. J. 0. Swift, of St. _Helens. . .</p>
        <p>Police-constable Simpson said he was passing the i house about 8.50 on the night .in question, when he heard a disturbance going on in the back yard, and someone calling out "Murder." On going through thel premises, he saw defendant quarrelling with his wife. Witness went for another constable, and returned about 8.30, when defendant was drunk in the taproom. He was staggering about in the room. Witness told him he was not in a fit state to conduct the house, and would be reported.—Police-constable M'Kay said he accom- panied the last witness to the house at 8.30. Defendent was staggering in the taproom, and was swearing loudly.—Jeseph Clayton said he was in the Millstone at 8.30 on the night named. The laddlord and his wife were quarrelling because defendant wanted to give the customers half a gallon of beer. The wife forbade it, but defendant went and filled the beer himself and gave it to the customers. Subsequently they quarrelled in the backyard, and witness heard a woman crying " Murder" several times. Fearing there would be some more bother, witness and a companion left the house.— Thomas Toft corroborated the last witness. They sat in the parlour, and were served by the barmaid. They saw the landlord by looking through the parlour window into the taproom. Mr. Swift addressing the bench for the defence, said undoubtedly defendant had several glasses of beer, but he was prepared to prove that the man was sober, and was capable not only of walking straight, but of attend- ing to the custotners. There was a very considerable row betwoen defendant and his wife, the latter consider- ing that her husband's motives were too generous ; he wanted to give his customers half a gallon of beer, and his wife objected. Thinking it would frighten him, the wife ran into the backyard and cried" Murder," but he (Mr. Swift) contended that what the police called drunkenness was simply excitement. Defendant, qaestioned by Mr. Swift, admitted that he quarrelled with his wife, but denied that he was drunk. He was excited, but was able to attend to the taproom customers until eleven o'clock that night.—Mr. Superin- tendent Brassington : Why did you want to give the customers half a gallon of beer ?—Well, we must treat them when they have no money, musn't we. (Laugh- ter.)—Mr. Brassington : How mnch beer did you have? —Defendant: Seven or eight glasses—something like that.—Mr. Brassington : Would seven or eight glas sea make you drunk ?—Defendant (with an astonished air) : —No sir.</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift : I have an expert here, who says that twenty pints would not make him drunk (loud laugh-- ter.) (To defendant) : Could you have toed a chalk line ?—Defendant : I could have done, but I didn't. (More laughter.) Martha Edwardson (defendant's wife), said it was true she and her husband quarrelled about giving the half gallon of beer, but defendant was not drunk, and was able to attend to the taproom customers from 6.30 to closing time. He went to bed between eleven and twelve o'clock, and was up about seven next morning, I and went to his work.—Mr. Brassington : Is he liberal with his customers ? Witness : Yes.—Mr. Brassington : He is :noro liberal when he is drunk than when he is sober P—Witness : Yes, a great deal. (Laughter.)—Mr. Swift : But he is liberal when he is sober P—Witness : Not very often. (More laughter) Richard Gittens (collier), said he was in defendant's '1 house until nearly ten o'clock on the night named. Defendant and his wife had a row. Defendant had had drink, but he was sober, and was capable of attending to the customers.—By Mr. Bassington : Witness was one of the men who shared the half gallon of free bear. Defendant had given many a score of half-gallons to his customers, generally on Sundays at dinner time. Further evidence On behalf of the defendant was given by William Howard, Thomas Ince, and Jessie Cornwall, and after a brief consultation, the Bench dismissed the case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>DEATH OF SIR A. B. WALKER.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF SIR A. B. WALKER.</p>
        <p>TRIBUTES FROM WARRINGTON. THE DECEASED'S CAREER.</p>
        <p>The news of the death of Sir Andrew Barclay Walker, Bart., was received in Warrington, on Mon- day. It occurred at his residence, Gateacre, after an illness which began about the middle of last year at his Derbyshire mansion of Osmaston Manor, and grew worse after his removal to Gateacre. The news was not unexpected, andfit was signalled by those melancholy signs, the halfmast flags, at the Town Hall, the Conservative Club, and Walker's Brewery. In its material aspects Sir Andrew Barclay Walker's career was one of unbroken prosperity and advance- ment. Himself a brewer and the son of a brewer, and deriving his wealth from a traffic which in the eyes of many is accursed, he laid out a great part of his revenue in philanthropy and public service of a substantial kind. A life offering broadly marked contrasts of this kind naturally etiougli presented matter for comment, and if this was offered from time to time and in public with some vehemence, it must be remembered that the topic was one of provocative interest. Essentially a diffident man, he found the necessity of public appearances, and especially of public speaking, exceedingly irksome ; yet a supreme quality of tact which he possessed enabled him to discharge his public duties at all times with grace and efficiency. In private life his qualities were always heartily recognised,, and to his private virtues be added much business shrewdness and foresight. It is clear, in fact, that his career could not have been so eminently successful in a material sense without the possession of these last attributes. Sir Andrew was born . in" the trade." in - 1824, when he first saw the light, being the second son in the family of three, his father, Peter Walker—a Scotchman—was a brewer in a fairly successful way of business. Young Andrew was edu- cated at the Liverpool Institute, and in due time joined his father and brothers in the firm of Peter Walker and . Son. The brewery was at that time situated in Liver- pool ; but sabsequently the Warrington brewery was started, which made the fortune of its possessors. Before this climax was reached, however,young Andrew 1 Walker's brothers quitted the firm and established as a separate business a brewery at Wrexham, leaving "old Peter Walker," as he then came to be called, to conduct with Andrew the Liverpool and Warrington businesses on their own account. The subsequent pro- gress to wealth was unchequered, and in April, 1890, Peter Walker and Co. were registered as a limited liability company, which to-day takes rank amongst the most successful existing ventures of the kind. For a number of years Mr. A. B. Walker took his share hi the municipal life of Liverpool. He was elected to' the Town Council in 1867, being afterwards promoted to the ' aldermanic bench. He was appointed mayor for the first time in 1873. It was about this time that his long series of public benefactions commenced. In January, 1874, on the occasion of the marriage of the Duke of Edinburgh, he entertained three thousand of the poor of Liverpool to a dinner at St. George's Hall, two days being necessary for the purpose. In the same year he announced his desire to build and present to the town the Art Gallery which afterwards received his name. The foundation-stone was laid in September of that year and the building was opened by Lord Derby in 1877, in which year Mr. Walker was again occupying the civic chair. Besides providing the original building, Mr. Walker, finding its accommodation insufficient, after- wards undertook at his own charge a considerable exten- sion of it, and supplemented this piece of liberality by the gift of the two figures in marble of Michael Angelo and Raphael which embellish the entrance, the work of Mr: Warrington Wood, a sculptor of some promise, whose early death was much regretted. The cost of the Art Gallery and its extension exceeded £50,000. Sir Andrew's next important gift to Liverpool was announced in the Jubilee year of Her Majesty, and it was nothing less than a complete system of engineering laboratories to be erected in connection with the Univer- sity College. These were completed in 1889,at a total of £20,000 cost and opened by Lady Walker. To relate all Sir Andrew's benefactions is unnecessary. Not only did they relate to Liverpool itself, but to communities else- where. To the pretty village of Gateacre, near Liver- I pool, where he lived for a long time, he presented as a 1 Jubilee gift a village green with a marble bust by Count Gleichen of Her Majesty, and at the same time a corn- 1 modious and prettily designed library, instittate, and reading-room. The honour of knighthood, which was conferred upon him in recognition of his liberal gift of the Art Gallery, was felt by all to be well deserved, and to this was added in 1886 the dignity of a baronetcy. In the same year he filled the office of High Sheriff of Lancashire. By way of recognition of his public set-. .vices, Sir Andrew Walker was presented in January, 1890, with the honorary freedom of the city, this being the first occasion on which the new powers of the Council in this direction bad been exercised; and in December of the same year his portrait, finely painted by Mr. Orchardson, was subscribed for and presented to him by a number of friends. Sir. A. B. Walker was twice married. His first wife (by whom he had six sons and two daughters) was a daughter of Mr. John Reid, of Limekilns, in the county of Fife. She died in 1882, and on the 11th of October, 1887, Sir Andrew married the Hon. Maud Okeover, daughter of Mr. Charles, Okeover, of Okeover Hall, Staffordshire. Sir Andrew Walker enjoyed most of the tastes of an English country gentleman. He was fond of sport, particularly shooting and yachting, and his steamer, the Cishona, is one of the finest craft of the day. In politics Sir Andrew Walker I was a thoroughgoing Conservative, and for party pur- poses had always an open purse. The title falls to his eldest son, Mr. Peter Curlaw Walker. A VOTE OF CONDOLENCE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Conservatives of Fairfield Ward on Monday evening,a vote of condolence was passed to the bereaved family. Alderman Fran— comb, J.P., the Chairman, referred to the death of Sir A. B. Walker, whose name, he said, was well known to all of them, and many of them had often had the pleasure of seeing him in their town. He took a deep interest in everything connected with Warrington, and was at all times willing to subscribe to any good object. Sir Andrew's liberality knew no bounds ; especially to elm people of Liverpool.: The Walker Art Gallery and the Technical Schools which he presented to Liverpool would be a lasting memorial of his generosity. Mr. Francomb said Sir Andrew was a shareholder in their club, and always took a lively inte- rest in its success. They all deeply deplored his death, and he was sure they would all join with him in expres- sing their sympathy with Lady Walker and the members of Sir Andrew's family in their sad bereavement. A formal resolution of condolence was then passed.—Many wreaths and messages of condolence were sent by War- rington people to Gateacre Lodge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>WARRINGTON RAILWAY MEN.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RAILWAY MEN.</p>
        <p>R. Stone.</p>
        <p>RICH, PURE, MID FRAGRANT • • &amp; Is 4d PER LE.</p>
        <p>N)</p>
        <p>• ••.-</p>
        <p>The following address, signed by Mr. W. H. Rigbey, station master, on behalf of the staff at the Central Sta- tion, Warrington, was forwarded to Lady Walker on Wednesday night:—" To Lady Walker and family of the late Sir Andrew Barclay Walker, Bart., deceased. The staff of the Warrington station of the Cheshire Lines Committee respectfully desire to express their feeling of condolence with you on the death of the late Sir Andrew Barclay Walker, and to place on record in this short address their high and grateful appreciation of the courtesy and many acts of kindness and generosity which they have on. many occasions during past years experienced from him and his sons, and to tender to you</p>
        <p>PER LB. WORLD CAN PRODUCE. I.s. 7d. No Higher Price.</p>
      </div>
