      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>BEFO R E THE MIGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFO R E THE MIGISTRATES</p>
        <p>FOWL ROBBERIES.</p>
        <p>At Warrington Police Court on Thursday, Walter Humphries and Peter Comerford were each charged with stealinl a hen from Rebecca Locker, Mill-st., on February 3rd. Walter Humphries was also charged with stealing five hens and a cock on February 15th. The Chief Constable said that there had been a good number of fowl robberies lately, and several officers bad been engaged making inquiries. Sergeant Cheeney found out that the prisoneEs had sold a hen to a boy named Fred Hampton, on'the same day that one bad been stolen from Mrs. Locker. Some fowls had also been sold to another young man named Fred Bennett, and also to Henry Siddall. When charged with the *Hence the prisoners admitted it. Mr. Hunt said that be thought that the police had erred on the side of Leniency in not placing more persons in the dock, as the fowls had been sold to the three persons, Bennett especi- ally, very cheap, and they ought to have known that the fowls had not been obtained honestly. Mrs. Locker valued the fowls at 12s. The Bench ordered Hninohrios to be sent to gaol for one month, and Comerford for 14 days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>ROBBERY AT WARRINGTON:</head>
        <p>ROBBERY AT WARRINGTON:</p>
        <p>Thomas Morley was charged on Saturday at the War- rington Borough Court. with stealing a purse containing 205., the property of John Pinder lodging at a ladgine- house at Friar's Green. The Chief Constable said the defend int and complainant were both in the Shakespeare Inn, on Friar's Green on the previous day. They had beent drinking together all the afternoon in several pub- lic-houses. While they were in the Shakespeare Inn, Morley knocked Pinder to the ground, taking his purse from him, and tearing his clothes in toe struggle. Mor- ley afterwards gave up the purse, but the money was missing. When arrested by D.S. Monaghan Morley said" He was flashing the money about, and I took it eff him to take care of it." Complainant and a man named Boyle bore out the Chief Constable's statement. Mrs. Young, the landlady, said that she saw a scuffling in her house. She had known prisoner for four years. Detective Monaghan said that on Friday night, defen- dant and complainant came to him at Market Gate. They charged each other with the robbery. They were brought to the police station and it was then found that prisoner was the offender. Prisoner was fined 101. and costs or 14 days. CHANGE OF STIBATANG MONEY FROM • PUBLIC•HOUSE.</p>
        <p>At Daresbury Petty Sessions on Tuesday, Samuel Shukar, of Lostock Gralam,was charged with feloniously stealing 19s. 9d., the moneys of John Kirkham, landlord ef the Hatton Arms, Hattori, on the 17th January. Mr. A. Browne. Warrington, appeared for the prosecution, and Mr. Bancraft,Northwich,represented the defendant. —Mr. Browne stated that the defendant, along with another man named Steele, entered the Hatton Arms, after a sale that had been held in Hatton. The defendant after ordering drink in the lobby went into the kitchen, where Mr. Billington, farmer, Dutton, was present, who was also taking drink. While the defendant was there, Mr. Billington gave the barmaid a soVereign, and she went to get change. The girl when returning with the change was met by the defendant in the lobby and he said " Where's my change." She asked him whether it was his. and he replied it was. She thereupon gave him the 19s. 91.,and he departed.—Mr.J.Billington, farmer, Dut- ton, Mary Jane Lambert and Elizabeth Green, tore out Mr. Browne's statement. For the defence it was argued that the ehange given the defendant was his oven,and that sinte the time of the alleged offence he had had an opportunity of, paying back the money which would leave prevented him from appearing to defend his good character of 25 years standing.—Thomas Steele, John Kinsey, farmer, Lostock Gralam, Mr. Yarwood, farmer, Wincharn, also gave evidence on the defendant's behalf. The bench retired to consider their verdict, and fined the defendant 40s.and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT. Mcsmar.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), and S. Wright. DRUNK AND DISORDERLT.—RaIph Taylor, last from St. Helens, was charged with not appearing to his summons for being drunk and disorderly in Winwick-st., on August 22nd, 1891. P.C. Cooper said the prisoner was fighting another man on the above date. The other man was locked up, and prisoner was summoned but failed to appear. He was arrested on Saturday night. Pris- oner was fined in costs 13s. 6d., or in default seven days. DRUNK AND REFUSING TO QUlT.—Michael Iligharn, 4, Dutton-st., was charged with being drunk and disorderly and with refusing to quit the licensed premises of the White Bear Inn, and also with doing nialievms damage to a window in the same on Saturday night. P.C. Hampson said that on Saturday night he was called into the White Bear Inn in consequence of a disturbancc.</p>
        <p>He saw Mr. Marshall, the landlord, trying to eject the prisoGer, who was drunk and refused to go away. Wit- ness was obliged to lock him up. Mr. Marshall said that prisoner came into his house the worse for drink, and witness ref usedto serve him. Prisoner and another man then commenced to fight and in the scuffle, a window was broken. Prisoner was fined ss. and costs or seven days. MEETING A DOG WITHOUT A LICENSE.—Joseph Lawrin- son,Old-road,Latchford.was charged with keeping a dog without haring a license. P.C.Hampson proved the case, and the defendant was discharged on payment of costs, as he had got a license since he had the summons. FIGHT BETWEEN NEIGHBOURS.—JOSeph Simcock, 16. Hall-street, and Wm. Wood, 3, Hall-street, were charged with fighting with each other In Hall-street, on Feb. 18th. P.C. Barnett said that on the above date, he proceeded to Hall-street, and found the two defendants fighting on the floor. Witness separated them, and sent them home, but they wanted to fight again, and witness had to report them. The Chief Constable said there was some ill-feeling between the two defendants, and if nothing was done, he feared that they would break out again, as they had used threats against each other. Defendants were each bound over to keep the peace for six months, and were ordered to pay 7s. costs. OnsruucTros ON THE FOOTPATH.—Wm. Bliley, Golden Grove-st., was charged with causing an obstruction on the footpath in Market-st., on February 18th. P.C. Turner said that on the above date he was on duty in Market-st., and he saw a barrel of fruit belonging to defendant on the footpath. He told defendant to re- move it, but he refused. Half an hour afterwards, the barrel was still there, and witness told defendant that he would repert it. P.S. Cheeney corroborated the last , witness, and defendant was fined 3s. 6d. costs. . _</p>
        <p>, PLAYING PITCH AND Toss.—Three boys, Joseph Tom- linson, aged 13 years, 23 Orchard-st., Edward BirchaU, aged 13 years, 82 George-st., and George Ogden, aged 16 years, 16 Dutton-st., were all charged with ylaying pitch and toss in Back William-st., in February 19.—P.C. Newry proved the case, and defendants were fined 33. 61. costs.</p>
        <p>Cline:EY ON FlRE.—William Smith, 3 Clothall Yard, and George London, 1 Cheshyres-lane, were each charged with having their dwelling-house chimney on fire, the former on the 17th inst., ad the latter on the 18th inst. They were each fined is. ASSAULT ON A WOMAN.—Patrick McDonald and his wife Kate McDonald, 17 Refuge Square. were charged with assaulting Jane Roberts on the 18th inst.—P.C. Passmore said that on the 18th lit., he was on duty in Bewsey, when he saw defendants assaulting complain- ant. He separated them, and got their names and re- ported them.—Mrs. Roberts said that there had been a row with the McDonalds, and she went to see, what it was about, when the female defendant came up to her and asked her what she was laughing at, giving her a blow in the face. Witness was afterwards knocked down, and the male defendant then struck her.—Patrick McDonald was dismissed, but his wife Kate was fined ss. and costs or seven days. TUESDAY.—Before Mr._,E. J. Guest (in the chair), and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>Dr. S. Smith.</head>
        <p>Dr. S. Smith.</p>
        <p>APPLICATION.—Mr. Frank Nield applied for tem- porary authority to sell in the Ring o' Bells, Church-st. The application was granted. SUMMONS WITHDRAWN.—The case against Joseph Lowe for arrears in the maintenance of his wife, which was adjourned for a month was withdrawn as the par- ties are living together. WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. J. Fairclough (in the. chair), Councillor Fleming and Mr. J. Geddes. THEFT AT THE BARRACKS.—Henry Green was charged with stealing a razor and other shaving utensils from the military authorities. The Chief Constable said the prisoner was in the 3rd Battalion of the South Lanca- shire Militia. Be had been training during the past seven weeks, and his time expired on Tuesday. As he was leaving the barracks on Tuesday, Sergeant Riley asked him if he had anything which did not belong to him, as there had been a number of brushes and razors missing lately. Receiving a reply in the negative, Ser- geant Riley searched prisoner's pockets, and found a razor and other shaving utensile, which belonged to other members of the barracks. Prisoner was sentenced to 14 days hard labour. ASSAULTING THE POLICE.—WiIIiaDI Cousins, Robert Roberts, and James North were charged with assaulting P.C. Delahay while in the execution of his duty on the previous night. P.C. Delahay said that he was going along Knutsford-road, and met prisoners singing and shouting. He requested them! to desist, but Cousins wanted to fight. Witness pushed him away, but he ' came again and gave witness a blow in the chest. Wit- ness tried to arrest him, but the other prisoners pre- vented him, and they then went off towards Thelwall- lane. Witness followed, and got the assistance of P.C.'s Leithwood and Barlow,and prisoners were arrested. The magistrates fined Cousins 10s. and costs or 14 days, and dismissed Roberts and North. Mr. Hunt said that extra police had to be put in Latchford in consequence of these gangs of men at night. The magistrates said that if such cases were brought before them again, they would be severely dealt with. THURSDAY.—Before Alderman Chandley (in the chair), Alderman Sutton and Councillor J. Fairclough. I</p>
        <p>ASSAULT ON A Sisraa.—John Gibbons was charged on a warrant with assaulting his sister Bridget Atherton, on February 28th. Bridget Atherton said that her bro- ther boarded with her. On the above date they -were in Lockhart's beerhluse, when her brother, from som., dis- pute about the board, gave her a blow in the eye, swel- ling it very much, and making it black. Prisoner was sentenced to 3 days' hard labour. ."</p>
        <p>FAILURE TO APPEAR TO A SCTISMONS.—AIice Thomas was charged with not appearing to her summons for fighting in Mersepst., on October 27. P.C. Corcoran said that prisoner had been fighting with some soldiers last October. She was summoned but failed to appear. Prisoner was bound over for six months,or 14 days' hard labour. _</p>
        <p>School. CASES.—The following school cases were dealt with :—Thomas Newall, gas stoker, Hall-st., fined 53. ; Thomas Whalley, forgeman, Hale-st., 2s. 6d. ; James Turner, Ship Canal foreman, Latchford, 2s. 6d. ; Ben Matthewman, brush maker, Manchester-lane, 53. ; Win. Partridge, joiner, Manchester-lane, ss. ; Thos. Douglas, forgeman, Gee-st., 2s. 6d. •, James Hennigan, forgeman, Buckley-st., ss. ; Richard Booth, forgeman, Warburton- st., 2.5. 6d. ABSENTEE F2OII TEE MILITIA.—Thomas Connor was charged with being an absentee from the 3rd South Lancashire Regiment in 1892. Detective Monaghan proved the case, and prisoner was fined 40s. and cost,or one month. .</p>
        <p>FRIDAY. —Before Alderman Wright (in the chair), - - •</p>
        <p>Councillors Roberts and Fleming. SLEEPING IN ENCLOSED PREMISES. Annie Ryan, Winifred Jennings,Peter Higgins,and Thomas Knockton, were each charged with being persons of bad character, and known thieves, and with being found on Thursday night, on the enclosed premises of Messrs. Kenyon, spelter manufacturers, Bank Quay. P.S. Taylor said that on Thursday night he visited Messrs. Kenyon's Spelter Works in company with P.C. Clough, and found the prisoners just leaving the premise. They ran across Arpley Fields, and witness and Clough gave chase. The two female prisoners were caught, and taken to the police station. Witness afterwards saw the male prisoners in Friars' Green, and gave chase, and they ran into P.C. Corbett's arms. Mr. Kenyon said that he often had complaints concerning persons sleep- ing in the works, and there were several things lying about which might be stolen. The Chief Constable said that this class of persons gave a lot of trouble to the police. The haystacks in Arpley fields had been interfered with, and several places had been broken into during the past few weeks. Prisoners were all sent to gaol for 14 days' hard labour.</p>
      </div>
