      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <p>their most sincere sympathy aid sorrow in the great loss which you and a wide circle of the commuaity have sustained by his death. Having been a frequent travel ler on the line, he was well known to most of the staff, and by all of them his memory will always be held in high esteem and tender regard."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>On Thursday, a very large number of persons pro- ceeded from Liverpool, Warrington, St. Helens. Mate cheater, Buxton, and other places to Gateacre and Childwall to take part in the obsecoies of the late Sir AB. Walker, and to pay a last tribute of respect to his memory, The day was fine and numbers proceeded by road as well as rail to the last resting place of the de- ceased baronet. The interment took place at Childwall Church, amid many manifest feelings of regret. The scene at the Grange, Gateacre, the late resi- dence of the deceased, was a very impressive one. The usually quiet little village was thrnwn into a state of subdued excitement, and on every hand there were signs of mourning. At Most of the places of business shutters were up, while almost every house in the village had its blinds drawn. A very large number of local gentlemen sent their carriages,, which drew up in line in the roadway just outside the grounds attached to the Grange. At the entrance gates the villagers congregated in considerable force to witness the depar- ture of the cortege for Childwall churchyard. In addition. to the chief mourners, many friends of the deceased baronet attended the funeral, some of whom also sent wreaths. A vast number of exquisite floral 'tributes were contributed by many sympathisers who were un- able to attend, and associations and societies in which Sir Andrew during his lifetime was more or less actively connected.</p>
        <p>The cortege left the Grange at a quarter to twoi the hearse being drawn by four beautiful blac.lz horses. The coffin was almost hidden from view by wreaths and crosses. The chief mourners were as under :—First -carriage, Mr. Peter Walker, Mr. John Walker, and Mr. Wm. Walker (sons).;, second carriage, Mr. Arthur Walker, Mr. Monro Walker, Mr. Peter Walker, and Mr. Barclay Walker ; third carriage, Mr.. H. C. Oakover and Mr. W. Roylance Court; fourth carriage, Mr. William Ramsay, Mr. Archibald Hood, and Mr. James Monroe;. 'fifth carriage, Mr. Mathew Bullock, Mr. Geo. H. Robert- son, and Dr. H. Gorst ;.sixth carriage, Illr.Howard Cart- land and Mr. George Gartland ; seventh carriage, Mr.H. Branston, Mr. G. J. Branston, and Mr. G. H. Branston. I The Mizyor's carriage came nest, and contained his Wor- ship the Mayor (Mr.R. D.Elolt), Alderman W.B.Bowring, and the Town Clerk (Mr. G.J. Atkinson). Among those also present were Messrs. C. A. Cain, W. E. Cain, Alfred D. Cain, Herbert Cain,-A. Mackenzie Smith (chairman of the Widnes Division Conservative Association),Robert Madge (secretary Widnes Division Conservative Associa- tion), James Thompson and S. Starbuck (Liverpool Constitutional Association), S. S.Bacon,.J.G.Livingston, John E. Pearson, and R. Willis (Halsenhead Park), Dr. Sinclair, Messrs. James M‘lileiliedge,W. C. Atkinson, John Beers, Ed.G. Slatter, J. Hughes, Rutherford, J. Price, H. Greenall, and Wm. Pilkington. The members of the Gatacre Institute, in which the deceased baronet took a very lively interest, followed the procession in a couple I of brakes, about fifty being present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>SIR A. B. WALKER'S FATHER.</head>
        <p>SIR A. B. WALKER'S FATHER.</p>
        <p>A sketch of Sir Andrew's life • would be incomplete without a reference to his father, Mr. Peter Walker who died on Saturday, November 15,. 1879, at his residence, St. Lawrence House, Ayr, at the age of 84. The father of Mr, Peter Walker was Mr. Andrew Walker, of Ban- viii, Maryhill, near Glasgow, who in his day was one of the largest colliery owners in Scotland. Mr: Peter Walker was originally a brewer -in Ayr, but early in life he removed to Liverpool,where he resumed the business of a brewer. He subsequently came to Warrington from Liverpool about 1838, and commenced business at the old brewery in King-st. Under his able management the business grew very rapidly,. and the old building was by degrees extended and improved to its utmoist capacity, and when incapable of further growth was Ire-enforced by the present extensive building in Hallam- ' lane, which, in its turn, had to be enlarged from time to time. Messrs. Walker and Son for some titae carried on ,the brewery in Tanner's-lane, formerly belong- ing to Mr. Edward Pennington, and they also worked the one known as the " Pioneer Brewery." The late Mr. Peter Walker was for a short time a member of the Warrington Town Council, and he also in- terested himself in political matters. He was a liberal supporter of local institutions, and on one occasion made a gift of £l,OOO to the borough. His last residence in this neighbourhood was the New Bewsey Hall. On his retirement from business he returned to his native town I of Ayr, and resided at Balrazi/e House there. A refer- ence to his inventions will do doubt be interesting ;in fact, his success as a brewer was largely owing to his early inventions in connection with his Warrington brewery. At a very early date he was able to save an enormous amount of labour by the application of mac- hinery to brewing to an extent previously unknown. The necessary ingredients put into a huge reservoir at the top of a lofty building came out in due course at the• basement storey as foaming treble X without the inter- vention of human hands. Some brewers behaved honourably, and purchased Mr. Walker's inventions, but I he had to sit silently by and hear of their stolen applica- tion at leading breweries in other parts. One invention by Mr. Walker bade fair to give hint a lasting name,as well as a world-wide fame, but, coming from an out- sider, it was coldly received. It was a means of dis- covering explosive gas in coal pits by exploding it while in small quantities, and he exhibited his electrical apparatus by which the explosion was secured at King- street works, and also an engine of a peculiarly original make.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>DEPUTATIONS FROM WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>DEPUTATIONS FROM WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The fallowing deputation from the Conservative Club, Sankey-street, left Warrington, on Thursday morning to attend the funeral of the late Sir A.B.Walker : The Mayor (Alderman Greenall),Aldermen Francomb,Wright,Sutton Councillors Dr. Celinell and Smethurst; Messrs.J.Duan, E.M.Cresfield,Henry Greenall, W. C. Jones,H.Tlnsley,F. Massey,Higham and other gentlemen. Mr.lbgham took a wreath, sent by the members of the Conservative Club. A large number ef employes of the late baronet also left Warrington by the same train. They also took a large wreath, subscribed ior by the employes. The Warring- ton Licensed Victuallers' Association was represented by Mr. John Evans (president), and Peers Brimelow (secretary). THE EMPLOYES.</p>
        <p>Amongst the employes of Messrs. Peter Walker and Son. Limited, present were :</p>
        <p>Messrs. Peter Wright, Liverpool general manager ; Edward G. Slatter, private secretary to the late baronet Ernest Ellis, secretary to the company ; Charles Price, head bookkeeper ; Frederick Jenkins, thief clerk of estate department ; Samuel J. Rowe, William R. Arthur, George Cannon, W. R. Armstrong, George Bilsbarrow, James Parish, Arthur Bennett, George Armstrong, Edward Lockett, Thomas Potter, Walter W. Dolby, F. R. Ledsham, William Watkin, George Wilkinson, C. E. Jones, Alfred Craddock, Thomas H. Cox, H. Findley, William Firth, T. J. Morgan, John Ellis, and Robert Milner, members of the general staff at 105 Duke-st . ; William James, head outdoor manager; Frank L. Calder Peter Edge, Richard Steadman, John Edwards, Jahn Adams, Peter Leigh, William Davenport, Charles Jen- kinson, and John Tomlinson, outdoor managers ;J. Fulton Smith, Warrington general manager ; J. R. Greaves, Warrington head brewer ; Arthur Potter, War- rington chief cashier ; James Dunn, John Baird, James Baird, Samuel Parsonage, Frederick Parsonage, T. Brooks, Samuel Ross, and Peter Wells, from Warrington staff ; A. E. Benson, Burton general manager; C. E. Hill, and W. B. Ledsham, Chester staff ; and J. C. Nel- son, Birkenhead staff. LETTER FROM THE QUEEN. Tier Majesty toe Queen has written a letter of condo-. lence to Lady Walker.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
