      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <p>THE COAL CRISIS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Derbyshire Miners' Council on Saturday it was decided to support a proposal to restrict the output of coal by " playing " two or more days per week, and to fight against any reduction of wages. At a meeting of the Mid and East Lothian miners held at Dalkeith on Saturday the men decided not to submit to the proposed 10 per cent, reduction in wages. ,Opinion in favour of a national miners holiday was expressed. DECISION OF ST. HELENS MINERS. A meeting of StyHelins and district minerawas held in the Town Hall, St. Helens, on Saturday, for the pur- pose of considering the present condition ;of the coal trade throughout England, Scotland, and Wales, and to decide upon a course of action respecting the proposal of the Executive Committee of the Miners' Federation of Great Britain that the whole of the miners in the Federa- tioe shoul take a general holiday.—Mr. John Tranter, a miner for fifty years, was voted to the chair. , Mr. T. Glover J.P.,' miners' agent, explained the. position as it had been placed before the Federation, and which led'them to their decision. He said they wake face to face with a great crisis, which had been brought , about not so much by the employers of the Federation as by the employers outside the Federation. He (Mr. Glover) knew very well that, so far as St. Helens and Lancashire and the Federation were'concerned, they had during the past five years obtained 40 per cent, increase. on wages, but so far as St. • Helens was concerned they were receiving to-day not more than 20 per cent, as the- other 20 per cent, was taken off by private reductions. Dealing with the reductions in Northumberland, Dur- ham, South Wales, and Scotland, and other efforts to further reduce, Mr. Glover said they must know what , effect those reductions had upon the markets. That was all very well if they could do it in those districts and not injure other districts. But the fact was that those manufacturers came into the markets, and Undersold the employers in Federation districts. South Wales was in the Liverpool markets underselling the Lancashire • employers. If something was not done to prevent this underselling and overstocking the markets the Lanca- shire miners must have a reduction. Wrist were they to do? Was the holida last year a good or evil Le them? (A voice : " Evil"—bear, hear and applause). -Ur. Glover...continued :—Well, if that was sof don't , I think we should commence another evil, and thereford, It is for this meeting to decide. He stated that the- effects of the last stoppage were neutralised by the men , workingsvertime the few weeks before the stoppage took plah, and again after it was decided in July to work , five nays some districts actually worked six days a week. Thus the markets were overgkitted. What he asked, was the I:pest policy ?(A voice " Four:days a IMr. Glover said in his opinion the best policy was to , have a uniform working week-Amy four days, if that was thought necessary, throughout the federated dis- tricts. (Applause.) Mr. Glover said he had addressed three or four meetings during the weekr which account- ed perhaps for the smallness of that gathering. (About • 300 were present). He then submitted the two resolutions. Only six voted for a stoppage, an the first resolution was negatived, whereas the second Rso- lution, for working four, days, was carried unanimously, ' and with applause. The resolutions were as follow ;First Resolution That this meeting of St. Helens I miners do hereby agree. ta take a general holiday of one or two weeks, along with all the miners of England. Scotland, and Wales, on a date to be fixed by the National Conference in Birmingham on Tuesday nett." Second Resolution e---“ That this public meeting of St. Helens miners believe that the best policy for keep- ing prices and wages up. will be best maintained by only working four days .a week, and we instruct our repro" ; sentatives to go in favour of the same at the federation; I meeting,to be.. held on Monday, the 27th inst.; Wigan. , NO STOPPAGE. The National Conference of Miners at Birmingham ote Wednesday discussed the recommendation of the Execu- tive Committee of the Miners' Federation that there' should be a general stoppage of work in, coalmines with a view to prevent a further reduction of wages. Au, amendment that the recommendation be not entertaineu was proposed, and eventually carried by a majority oi 26,000 of the men lepresented at the Conference. A second resolution, to the effect that, with a view te regulate the present position of the coal trade, work the mines should be limited to four days per week, also defeated, and the Conference unanimously resolveu that the question of the advisableness of action with re- geed to the wages question be relegated to the Execu- tive Committee of the Federation.</p>
