      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>PANAMA "THUNDERCLAP."</head>
        <p>A PANAMA "THUNDERCLAP."</p>
        <p>A new interest is given to the Panama scandals by a statement made at a meeting in Paris on Friday night by M. Andrieux, ex-Prefect of Police. He said he had not yet divulged the name of the mysterious X. because he had promised on his honour to preserve silenee. When the time came, however, for the electoral body to give its opinion he would announce the name of that individ- ual, as well as those of several other important person- sonages. He was now collecting evidence which would crnclusively prove the charges he had made, and when the proper time arrived the thunderclap would burst.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>FEROCIOUS MURDER BY A GIRL OF SIXTEEN.</head>
        <p>FEROCIOUS MURDER BY A GIRL OF SIXTEEN.</p>
        <p>MEAFORD, ONTARIO, TIIESDAY.—Jennie Wanch, a young girl of 16, murdered a cousin, a farm hand, at their home, situated at Craig Laith, on February 23, by burying an axe in his skull as he stooped to pick up pieces of kindling wood. She then dragged his body to I the woods more than 200 feet distant from the scene of the murder,buried the body in a snowdrift, then effaced the traces of the blood at the scene of the cride, and came here, where she found employment in an hotel as a domestic servant. She was arrested on Monday, and said that she sought employment in the hotel in order to obtain money to pay for the decent burial of the body. The motive of the crime was jealousy, as her cousin after paying court to her, transferred his affections to another.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>MR. GLADSTONE'S VISITOR.</head>
        <p>MR. GLADSTONE'S VISITOR.</p>
        <p>It is significant says a correspondent of the Manches- ter " Guardian," that one of Mr. Gladstone's first visi- tors on Friday morning was the Archbishop of Canter- bury. Did he call to congratulate the Grand Old Orator upon his remarkable effort the previous evening ? On the contrary, it rather looks as if our ecclesiastical digni- taries were bringing to bear for the retention of their property that process of spiritual intimidation which procured it for their predecessors. It is not unknown that when two years ago the rumour went abroad that Mr. Gladstone intended taking part in the debate on Mr. Pritchard Morgan's amendment he was haunted and harassed for days by priestly advisers. It was suspected at the tune that their coaching had something to do with the glowing phrase in which Mr. Gladstone dilated upon the work done by the Church in Wales at the pre- sent moment.</p>
        <p>THE OWNERSHIP OF THE "SANDS OF THE SEA."</p>
        <p>A singular action for trespass, involving the question of ownership of the sands at Skegness, was heard at Lincolnshire Assizes on Monday. During recent years the sands have been taken possession of by booth pro- prietors, stall holders, and gipsies, who have acknow- ledged the authority of no man in the way of ownership. The Earl of Scarborough, as owner of a great part of the land fronting the sea, at last determined to test the question, and took proceedings against a travelling showman, on wheselbehalf, it appears, the point was raised that the sands, being the result of the gradual recession of the sea, belonged to the Crown. Mr. JusticA Dav bid if- ~e vuutig the sea is entitled to all accretions which may occur through recession, and the jury found a verdict for the plaintiff.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>A. " LOST " PARTY.</head>
        <p>A. " LOST " PARTY.</p>
        <p>NO PROGRAMME AND NO LEADERS.</p>
        <p>I The London Conservative papers were occupied on Monday in censuring the Opposition leaders for " mis- management," "carelessness," and "indifference." The following letter, which is taken from the " St. James's Gazette," will be read with amusement by Liberals :—" Sir,—As Ana of the rank and file of the Conservative party who has spoken incessantly in various parts of this country since 1885, I should like to know what is the policy and the programme of the party to which, somehow or another, I belong. Will any lead- ing Conservative kindly let me know what I am to say to an audience next time they have the misfortune to be addressed by me am, sir, your obedient servant, A. W.F.—P.S. It may be that the Conservative party have gone away leaving no address, as I have heard no- thing of them lately, but perhaps this letter may catch the eye of some of them in their retreats."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>DUKE OF YORK AND PRINCESS</head>
        <p>THE DUKE OF YORK AND PRINCESS</p>
        <p>MAY.</p>
        <p>EMPHATIC DENIAL OF THE RUMOURED INTENDED</p>
        <p>MARRIAGE. A Berlin correspondent telegraphs :—The rumour which has recently been busy with the Duke of York and Princess Victoria of Teck is absolutely baseless. The authority for this statement is a high court official who was lately at Osborne, and who brought away with him a very valid impression of the pain and annoyance which the rumour has produced in Royal circles in Eng- land. The whole thing, he was told, was a pure myth, and the rumour has only enjoyed so long a lease of life from the fact that, owing to the very nature of the case it was impossible to dispose of it outright by a positive denial. Why it ' was impossible to do this will readily suggest itself to those who belLive that supreme tact and consideration for the feelings of others have invariably been the rule of conduct at Marlborough House. What may yet happen it is, of course, impossible to predict, but for the present at any rate there is no serious idea of such a Royal marriage as has been forming the topic of society talk in London for some weeks past.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>REVOLTING CRUELTY IN BOLTON.</head>
        <p>REVOLTING CRUELTY IN BOLTON.</p>
        <p>ROASTING A CHILD.</p>
        <p>A case revealing barbarous cruelty to a child occupied the attention of Belton Borough magistrates on Monday. A collier named Thomas Cooper was charged with wil- fully ill-treating a child named Jane Gaskill, aged six. The child was carried into the court on a stretcher, and the solicitor for the defence said he had not the heart to cross-examine it. The prosecution was undertaken by the Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children. Prisoner had becn cohabiting with a woman named Gaskell, mother of the ill-treated child. The evidence showed that prisoner had put the child in a box doubled up, pushed her head foremost in a dollytub and threatened to drown her, smacked her in the face and given her a black eye, pinned the clothes over its back and scorched the lower portions of the body before the fire, finishing his revolting cruelty by plastering the in- jnred parts with grease and flour, then pricking the blisters and attaching the child to a line over the fire with her head hanging down, When the mother remon- strated, prisoner cursed her, and repeated his inhuman work. She and her children finally got shelter at a neighbours, and prisoner was arrested .—Prisoner, who protested his innocence and said the child's burns were caused through a younger brother pushing her against the fire, was committed for trial at the Quarter Sessions bail being allowed, prisoner in £5O and two sureties of 225.</p>
        <p>RAY'S COMPOUND a demulcent anodyne expectorant for Coughs and Colds, 90., 13,1 d. Of all Chemists.—Advt. In the divorce suit of Drummond v. Drummond (Fox cited) the jury on Saturday assessed the damages to be paid by Mr. Fox at £4,000.</p>
        <p>COAGULINE.—Cement for Broken Articles, 6d. and is. ; postage, 2d. Sold everywhere, home and abroad.—Advt.</p>
        <p>The campaign at Grimsby began on Monday. Mr. Henry Broadhurst was chmen on Saturday night as the Liberal candidate' and his opponent will be Mr. Heneage, who wasdefaated by Mr. Joss° (L) the retir- ing member. THROAT AFFECTIONS AND Hcanscisras.—All suffering from irritation of the throat and hoarseness will be agreeably surprised at the almost immediate relief afforded by the use of "Brown's Bronchial Troches." These famous " lozenges " are now sold by most respectable chemists in the country at is lid per box. People troubled with a "hacking cougli," • slight cold," or bronchial affections, , cannot try them too soon, as similar troubles, if allowed to progress, result is serious Pulmonary and Asthmatic affec- tions. See that the words "Brown's Bronchial Troches" are on the Government stamp around each box. Prepared _,py JOHN I. BROWN &amp; SONS, 805t074, U.S. European depot, SO, Parringdon-road. London. -Advt.</p>
      </div>
