      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>tatty% to tin Orbiter.</head>
        <p>tatty% to tin Orbiter.</p>
        <p>SIR G. GREENALL'S TESTIMONIAL. Sir,—l read with pleasure in your last issue Merlin's remarks upon the purchase of the above testimonial. As a tradesman of the town I protest against the money of tradespeople being taken out of town; it is simply abominable that in all public affairs there is quite a rush to go out of town, with the tnwnspeople's money. Who is responsible for this last disgraceful proceeding ? Can't you let us know through the columns of your paper, tha names bf these gentlemen ? lam sure their action in spending the scowl out of town, will not meet with the approval of the general body of subscri- bers.—Yours truly,</p>
        <p>A TRADESMAN.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l am really glad that you hit the Tories back, over the amount 4f money collected for Sir G. Greenell's testimonial. Why, in proportion to the length of time Sir Gilbert has served Warrin ' gton and the short time Dr. Houston was amongst us, the ".bunkum "Conservative leaders ought to have raised, instead of a paltry £l2O, over £3,000. But have they raised the £l2O ? I think not. I hear that presmre is being brought to bear non certain delinquents, purely for the "Cause's sake' if not for the old gentleman's. The Chairman of this Committee must feel as if he were presiding over a tripe supper or a hot pot "do," after a bowling handicap-1 it is such a shabby affair in comparison with the ser- vices rendered. Ah well, we shall bear soon of presen- tations being hit on the head, and Aldermanships and J,P.'s being substituted. Well, after all, its better to have a handle at the front or behind your name, than having " plate, &amp;c." which may be removed any time by the light fingered fraternity. A good solid "J .P." is there, can always be seen, that is if you bring yourself under the *ye of a pressman. How is it your friend "The Observer "is so self satisfied about this presenta- tion job? One would have thought that they would have stuck up for local tradesmen, and have exposed such a shabby trick. But I suppose there are wheels within wheels.—Yours</p>
        <p>ONE DISGUSTED.</p>
        <p>BUDDHISM AND CHRISTIANITY.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The strains of an old song" Oh where, and 0 where " come floating to memory as I look over" Harry Esmond's "last; missive. I had fairly expected that my first letter would have conveyed clearly enough my own standpoint in this discussion ; I mean the objection which I take to the strained and ill-founded parallels, said to exist between certain incidents in the lives of Buddha and Chrisbna on the one hand, and of Christ on the other; and more especially let me add ,to the false and profane inference, apparently intended to be drawn that our gospel narrative of the incidents in Christ's life is only a copy of the elder legends of Buddha, and that wretched travesty of the Brahmanic god Chrishna. To this issue I confined myself absolutely, and on several grounds, namely, the late appearance in Sanscrit writ- ings of the parallels in question, the modern instances ef discovered frauds recorded by Max Mailer, and lastly on the shadowy and insignificent character of most of the parallels citod, I closed my letter with a not too strongly expressed feeling of tontempt for the insult offered to our gospel history. . _</p>
        <p>Now, how does " Harry Esmond" meet my position? He presents us with a series of quotations from the Encyclopedia Britannica, the whole article of which on Buddhism I had carefully perused before entering upon this discussion. But not a line in his extracts bears on the main point at issue, or gives the least support to the Inference that our gospels owe anything whatever to the I legends of Buddha. It is not until the author of the article comes to what he calls "Later Buddhism" that he gives any prominence to the apparent resemblances .of incidents between Buddha and Christ. He then details certain parallels but first states that "it would be hazardous as yet attempt to trace chronologically (the italics mine), the growth of the Buddhist legends—which are found in one or other of Buddhist books." In a former part of the article whilst arguiug for the sub- stantial identity of the sacred books of the Buddhists at present existing in Ceylon (though not without altera- tions and additions) with a canon dating 250 n.c.,he con- cludes by stating "that much more reliance can be placed upon the dcctrinal than upon the biographical parts" of the work. Now " Harry Esmond" supposes that I shall admit that this article is an authority. Exactly so; its anttnr Dr.lthys David&amp; is an eminent Buddhist scholar; and I learn from statements in other works of his that I have drawn the correct inference from his article in the Encyclopedia above earned, namely, that in the later Buddhistic literature we have no proof whatever that our gospels owe aught to that source. The Work known among scholars as the " Lalita Vistara," se much used by those who would insist on the agreements between the legend of Buddha and the story of Christ, was prob- ably csompose(l, he says, in Nepal, and by some poet who lived between 600 and 1,000 years after the death of Buddha "—that is between the 3rd and 7th century of our Era. And in the same work he expresses himself quite as decisively as, to the "want of evidence of any actual, and direct communication of theseideas common to Buddhism and Christianity from the East to the West" that is from India to Judea). See his Hibbett Lectures, 1881, pp. 151. 197. Much more to the same effect might be quoted from many others among our best authorities. But let this suffice for the present. "Harry Esmond" professes himself amazed that I should quote Max Muller in this connexion. I feel much more amazed at his inability to estimate the value of such an authority as this eminent savant, whether in this connexion or any other, touch- ing Sanscrit literature and history. Had he perused more carefully the article in the Encyclopia Britannic&amp; he would have found that he haa his place duly acknow- ledged among such authorities. It would have looked better to have produced counter statements to his on the points for which I specially quoted him. I am quite aware that there are sentiments in Muller's works with which I should not agree ; but I am too conscious of the strength of my own position in this cage to feel alarmed at" Harry Esmond's "future dealings with Max Muller. Just one other word; I demur to the charge of using coarse and ungentlemanly epithet'; but let me remark that when the holiest and most sacred name known on earth is dragged into any conjunction with the trickiest rogue and the foulest dehauchee of the Hindoo pantheon —the Brahmanic Chrishna—ene's resentment is but faintly expresed by the use of so tame a word as " gullible."—Yours, etc,</p>
        <p>JOHN DIGNUM.</p>
        <p>Penketh, March 2, 1893. PS.—! must correct a slight inaccuracy in my last week's letter, in including Dr. nye David s ss supporting the view of Christian influence on the later legends of Buddha.—He rather favours the view that what resem- blances exist are not copies of one, from the other, but arise independently ; a view which" does not interfere in the least, however, correct it may be, with my main position that our gospels owe nothing to Buddhist legends. J. D.</p>
        <p>BUDDHISM AND CHRISTIANITY.—SOME FOINTS OF RESEMBLANCE. Sir.—So Mr. Dignum is climbing down. He is " most heartily ready to admit that there are truly wonderful points of comparison between the moral teachings and conduct of Buddha and the character and teachings of Christ." Now, this is refreshing, and if he will admit a little more there won't be very much left for us to cavil about. lam glad he has dropped the flippant and self- sufficient style he adopted inhis first letter, in which he said that" the string of ordinary insignificant parallels cited e-,ty your correspondents (I refer here to facts or incidents) should merely excite one's derision or con- tempt. The fleeting features of most of the coincidences noted vanish the moment you come to trace or compare them." This, and more to the like purport, he said which, viewed in the light of his later utterance,is sheer and undiluted nonsense. These ordinary insignificant coincidences are now admitted to be truly wonderful points of resemblance. Good ! In his first letter he said that "Christianity owes no more to the ancient religions of India than it does to the inhabitants of the moon." Now suppose, for arguments' sake, that it did, wOuld it be any the worse for its indebtedness ? Suppose some</p>
        <p>priest or prophet or any other great man, richly endow- ed in a spiritual sense, had been inspired with great truths and spiritual insight in the remote ages of the past, and had founded a great religion on the principles of righteousness and universal love, and the Christian religion had borrowed these great principles from it would Christianity be any the less precious to its follow- ers on that account? Our friend seems to be partial to Max Muller, so let us see what Max Muller says. In his work," Chips from a German workshop," Max Muller seems to be of opinion that Christianity does owe a good deal to the other reli- gions which have preceeded it. He says—" More surpris- ing than the continuity in .the growth of language is the continuity in the growth of religion. Of religion,too,as of language, it may be said that in it everything new is old, and everything old is new, and that there has been no entirely new religion since the beginning of the world. The elements and roots of religion were there, as far back as we can trace the history of man, and the history of religion, like the history of language, shows us throughout a succession of new combinations of the same radical elements. An intuition of God, a sense of human weakness and dependence, a belief in the divine government of the world, a -distinction between good and evil, and a hope of a better life, these- are some of the radical elements of all religions. Though sometimes hidden they rise again and again to the surface. Though frequently distorted, they tend again and again to their perfect form. Unless they had formed part of the original dowry of the human soul, religion itself would have remained an impossibility, and the tongues of angels would have been to human ears but as sounding brass or a tinkling cymbal. If we once understand this clearly the words of St. Augustine. which have seemed startling to many of his admirers, become perfectly clear and intelligible, when he says : " What is now called the christian religion, has existed among the ancients, and was not absent from the beginning of the human race, until Christ came in the flesh ; from which time the true religion which existed already, began to be called christian." Max Muller then goes on to say. "To the missionary more particularly, a study of the religions of mankind would be, I believe, of the greatest assistance. Mission- aries are apt to look upon all other religions as some- thing totally distinct from their own, as formerly they used to describe the languages of barbarous nations as 1 something more like the twitterings of birds than the articulate speech of ann. The science of language has taught us that there is order and wisdom in all 1 languages, and that even the most degraded jar- gons contain the ruins of former greatness and beauty. The Science of Religion, I hope, will pro- duce a similar changein our views of barbarous forms of faith and worship ; and missionaries' instead of look- ing out for points of difference, will look out more anxiously for any common ground,any spark of the true iight that may still be revived, any altar that may still be dedicated afresh to the true God." A native convert of India says :—" I know from personal experience that the Hindu Scriptures have a great deal of truth. If you go to India and examine the commots sayings of the people; you will be surprised to find what a splendid re- ligion the Hindu religion must be. Even the most ignor- ant women have proverbs that are full of the purest re- ligion." "To those, no doubt, who value the tenets of their religion as the miser values his pearls afi pre- ciouti stones, thinking their value lessened. if p s and stones of the same kind are found in other partt f the I world, the Science of Religion will bring many a rude shock ; but to true believers, truth, wherever it appears, is welcome, nor will any doctrine seem the less true or the less precious because it was seen, not only by Moses or Christ, but likewise by Buddha or Lao-tse. Max 1 Muller says a good deal more in the same strain, but I , have quoted quite enough to show the weakness of Mr. 1 Dignum's contention. 1 I wonder if Mr. Dignum has read Sir Edwin Arnold's I "Light of Asia." This is a wonderful book and created I quite a sensation on its first appearance a few years ago. In the preface Sir Edwin says that Buddhism has existed during twenty-four centuries, and at this day i surpasses in the number of its followers and the area of 1 its prevalence, any other form of creed. Four hundred and seventy millions of our race live and die in the tenets of Buddha. "More than a third of mankind, therefore, owe their moral and religious ideas to this illustrious prince , whose personality , though imperfectly revealed in the existing sources of information, cannot but appear the highest, gentlest, holiest, and most beneficent, with one exception, in the history of thought." "In point of age, most other creeds are youthful compared with thia venerable religion, which has in it the eternity of a universal hope, the im- mortality of a boundless love, an indestructible element of faith in final good, and the proudest assertion ever made of human freedom. The extravagances which dis- figure the record and practice of Buddhism are to be referred to that inevitable degradation which priesthoods always inflict upon great ideas committed to their charge." , I will deal with the Brahman God Christina next week, and will also institute a comparison between the morale of Buddhist and Brahman nountries and Christian ones. In my last letter I stated that Max Mutter's books were given in the form of lectures about thirty years ago. This is a mistake. I should have said twenty years ago. HARRY :'SMOND.</p>
      </div>
