      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>EVENTS AT THE Y.M.C.A.</head>
        <p>EVENTS AT THE Y.M.C.A.</p>
        <p>AMBULANCE CLASS.-PRESENTATION TO DR. T. STARKEY SMITHS J.P.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this class was held at the Y.M.C.A., on Monday evening, Mr. S. B. Stringer in the chair. The business wastbe distribution of the certificates to the successful candidates, and to make a presentation to Dr. T. Starkey Smith. Mr. F. S. Bnzza, honorary secretary to the class, read his report, which stated that a class for males, under the auspices of the St. John Ambulance Association, was commenced on Monday evening, Oct. 10th, with Dr. Smith as lecturer. Twenty-five pupils joined the class, consisting of 19 Y.M.C.A. members, and non-members, 19 of whom, !presented themselves for examination before Dr. Macpherson, of Liverpool, the examiner appointed by the Head Department of the St. John Ambulance Association, and all passed successfully. The certificates were kindly distributed by Dr. Smith, to the following gentlemen i—Mesms. W. H. Bayley, A. M. Bayley, D. S. Birrell, F. S. Buzza, H. B. Crowder, J. B. Doncaster, E. Edwardson, F.:Garside, A. G. Gartland,G. H.Gill,R.Hazledine,l.MeUor,S. B. Stringer,Joshu a Taylor, Henry Thornton,J.H.Thornton,W.E.Thornton,E. Walker, G. Yeadon. The Chairman, on behalf of the members of the class afterwards presented Dr. Smith with a hand- some bird cage and stand in recognition of his able and interesting services as lecturer. Dr. Smith suitably ac- knowledged the gift, and stated that the attention paid to his lectures was very gratifying to him. Recitations, songs, and pianoforte solos, were ably rendered by Messrs. Crowder, Woods, and Wright. Hearty votes of thanks to the honorary secretary, and to Mr. Stringer for presiding, terminated a very pleasant evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>P.S.A.</head>
        <p>THE P.S.A.</p>
        <p>On Sunday the P.S.A. meeting room at the Y.M.C.A. rooms, was well filled with men. A steady and con- tinual progress has attended the P.S.A. movement since its commencement, and every meeting has been well at- tended by men of all sections of the Church. Some, however' who have been very seldom seen within the walls of the churches and chapels of the town, have joined the movement, and now attend remilarly. The promoters of the movement have met with very little ' difficulty In securing the services of musical friends, who have come forward most readily and given their ser- vices. On Sunday a short address on " Thoroughness " was delivered. Mr. T. S. Kent, president, occupied the chair. The sinOng of Mr. F. Fieldhouse, and Mr. W. H. Ireland (Great ftnkey) was much appreciated. Mr. F. Hickman executed in a creditable manner the duties of accompanist. The prizes given for attendance will, it is expected, be exJaibited en Sunday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>I'ENNI9 CLUB.</head>
        <p>THE I'ENNI9 CLUB.</p>
        <p>Highly successful, both numerically and financially, may-be said of the social gathering, under the auspices of the Y.M.C.A. Tennis Club, held in the Lecture Hall of the institution on Thursday evening. Mr. T. Longdin occupied the chair, and in the course of a short address said it was only four months since they met in reference to the Lawn Tennis Club. He was zlacl at the response given that night to the invitation of the Club Commit- tee, and be hoped that, as a result, the debt incurred on account of the tennis ground would be cleared off. The programme submitted was an excellent one. includ- ing trios (violin,'cello, and piano) by Messrs. R. H. Pear- son, H. Bond, and W. H. Payton, executed in an admir- able manner. Songs were given by Miss E. Burnett (Salford). Miss Dooley, Mr. F. Brownlow, and Mr. F. R. Roberts, M.A. The songs by Miss E. Burnett were par- ticularly well appreciated, the one with violin accom- paniment more especially: and in response to vociferous applause she sang "Coming through the Rye" which still further pleased the audience. Miss Dooley and Mr. F. R. Roberts were also rewarded with encores. Mr. R. H. Pearson contributed a violin solo with his usual good taste and skill. Mr. F. R. Roberts, M.A., read one of Artemus Ward's selections "The Twins" in an excellent wanner, and Miss A. M. Longdin very creditably exe- cuted a pianoforte solo. Mr. W. H. Payton ably per- formed the duties of accompanist during the evening. A vote of thanks to the Chairman and all who had rendered their services during the evening was passed on the pro- position of Mr. Pattison, seconded by Mr. Broadhurst. THE DEBATING SOCIETY. At the usual weekly meeting et the Debating Society, in connection with the Young Men's Christian Associa- tion, held on Tuesday evening, an interesting paper was given by Mr. S. B. Stringer, on "Characters that we meet." A lively discussion followed the reading of the paper in which many took part.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>TEMPERANCE ZNTERTAINVENTS:,</head>
        <p>TEMPERANCE ZNTERTAINVENTS:,</p>
        <p>Another entertainment was given on Saturday, in the Golbonie-et. Baptist school-room, by mem- bers of the Temperance Society. Mr. Daiken was chairman, and gave* most interesting and instruc- tive address on temperance. Readings were given by Mr. Joha Shaw Green and Mr. P. Pennington so.nga, Mr. IL D.Walker, recitation, Miss A.Buckby, , pianoforte solo, Miss Maud White. Miss Gough was the accompanist for the evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <p>NOTES</p>
        <p>Voarmu.i.</p>
        <p>"Hooray !" Now, another one, hip" Hooray." Why I this singular expression of bubbling happiness you ask ? Well, ask the P.D., 'twits he who did it. He says his heart swelled like a blown bladder with gratitude, when he heard that all the Warrington teams had won, and he has been so elated with the way in which Webster (the tall athletic) scored the first try, that he has had to be tied down to a pica quod with a page chord, to pre- vent him squandering his money on a leather medal.</p>
        <p>It certainly came upon me as a cheering surprise to hear that the first team beat Broughton by 0-44 to nil; that our A team beat Broughton A team by 3-4-5 to nil, and that the good old second team beat the Broughton second team by 2.-2-4 to 1 goal (which was a penalty one). You speak of a real genuine all round " but can you find one to beat that ? Poor old Broughton! Played 13 matches, and won one. Ah, well, if you can't get a currant loaf be satisfied with a bean-feast.</p>
        <p>Saturday was the seventh time that our first team met ' Broughton for the purpose of trying conclusions with them. On each occasion the " Grimes" obstinately refused to be beaten, and hence prolonged the pleasure which Warrington enjoyed in bringing about their defeat on Saturday. lam told that the manner in which Webster scored the first try a few minutes after the start, struck confusion into the hearts of the enemy. A pass which was meant for Craven the Broughton half back, reached Webstet, or rather his lengthy arms reached it, and away he scurried, like a sprinting giant, across the line. It was funny. No one followed him. The Broughton men only opened their mouths, and gazed at him, and said " Oh-o-o."</p>
        <p>, Next came along " Smeck " Bate, and just before the time for the administration of lemons,ohe got over the line also. Boscow played an excellent game again ; but Lawless would insist upon being too "sudden,"and made a dreadful bungle just at a time when from a pass of " Smack" Bate's he might have scored. The two Bates played well, again, and " smack" was more generous in passing.</p>
        <p>I observe that the " Athletic News " drew the atten- tion of their readers this week to this fact: " Warring- ton are rapidly coming to the front, and, judging from their latest performance, there ought to be a rare fight at Salford next Saturday. It is an open question whether there is a club in the north with a quartette oi tall men equal to Webster, Lawless, Fair Turner, and Ashton." I was told last week that there is certainly no prominent team in the North of England, with four such men as the four named. There are several in the south of England, but they are chiefly gentlemen's sons who have had nothing else to do but develop their physiques. Some years ago when several of our retired players used to appear on the field, it was extremely difficult to find a team of equally well made men. It must be remem- bered, too,that of our finest players, Ashton and Fair Turner, are both men who may lay claim to the term of " veteran," for although certainly not very old in years they are both old in experience, and are two of the strongest builders up of the local reputation of the club.</p>
        <p>The results of the competition are as follows For. Agst. Pid. Won. Lost. Drn. G.T. G.T. Pk. Swinton ll 9 . 0 . 2 ...15 25—'1 3 ...20 Salford St. Helens Recreation 11 ... 5 ... 3 ... 3 ... 9 11... 5 6 ...13 Tyldesley Warrington . 9 4 . 3 . 2 ... 3 14... 3 3 ...10 Oldham l2 3 . 6 ... 3 ... 5 13... 7 9 ... 9 Rochdale Hornets Broughton Rangers Wigan 8 2 ... 5 ... 1 ... 2 2 ... 5 6 ... 5 Broughton l3 . 1 ... 10... 2 ... 33 ...13 28... 4</p>
        <p>"Popping up again my love "is the title of a song in pigeon English ; and something made me try to hum it when ',saw that after a few weeks rest the competitors for the Warrington Junior League Championship, aroused themselves from their native indifference on Saturday, and taking their hearty voices and thick shoes, journeyed to Stockton Heath to see the village team and St. Mary's fight for Mr. Davies's cups The result, after a hard fight, was five points for Stockton Heath, to a try. Singular to say the Parish Church, Stockton Heath, and St. Mary's have all won three matches out of five, representing six points each. This is the table of their scoring :</p>
        <p>For Agst. Plvd. Won. Lost, Drn. G.T G.T. Pts. Parish Church 5 ... 3 ... 2 ... 0 ... 3 6 ... 2 3 ... 6 Stockton Reath— 5 ... 3 ... 2 ... 0 ... 3 3 ... 2 3 ... 6 St. Mary's Ranger. Latchford Walkers' Brewery 3 ... 0 ... 3 ... 0 ... 0 0 ... 3 6 ... 0</p>
        <p>1011011110 intAl £llO A.G.CJ Moriland Orz Oges -nrelmv had an easy victory of one try to nil. J. fl. Compton, of Runcorn played a brilliant game as three quarter, and he is selected to play against Scotland to-day. Lock- wood, the Yorkshire Captain sustained an injury and will not be able to play.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 4, 1893. ON GAMES AND AT OUR LOCAL CHU-ROI:MS. ATHLETICS. ROMAN CATHOLICISM. The Rector (the Rev. F. W. Willis) continued his special sermons on Sunday night, the subject being Roman Catholicism. He said that Romanists separ- ated from-the Church because they were in favour of closer discipline. He would go back first to speak of the 'Roman Church, and then of the Roman schism. The .Romanists laid great stress on the authority of St. Peter. This was founded on several passages of scripture which the Rector , quoted, but he contended that these passages gave ' St. Peter no special authority whatever. From the Acts of.the Apostles and the writings of St. Paul, it was certain that St. Peter did not found the Church of Rome: so that he could not give what he never had. Also in the writings Of the early Fathers, such as Clement of Rome, Polycarp, Ireneaus, Ignatius, Clement of Alexandria, and Cyprian, there was nothing to justify Romanist claim for the authority, of St. Peter. Again the Bishops of Rome had no right to supremacy, Although they had the primacy of honour. There were several reasons for this primacy of honour. First of all there was the prestige of heathen Rome. Christian Rome inherited the prestige of pagan Rome. Then 300 years after Christ, the seat of empire was removed from acme to Constantinople, by which the bishops of Rome succeeded as it were to the possessiorls of the empire,ancl lastly Rome was generally on the side( of orthodoxy. From these reasons, Rotnegained the primacy of honour, but it was only in later times that she usurped the supremacy. At the Council of Nice she had no such supremacy, and although there are reports of tortner councils in favour of this supremacy, these reports are proved to be false. Coming to the subject of Rome in this country, it was a mistake to suppose that Rome had aay right of supremacy over this country. It was only natural that. Christianity should come to this country from Rome, by the Roman legions. There was perfect concord between this country and Rome in the first eenturiei. British bishops attended the councils On the continent. They looked upon Rome as the great patriarchate of the West,and by degrees,there grew up an idea that the Pope was wnat he claimed to be, the Vicar of God. The first act of Papal aggression in this coun- trY was in the year 600, when Pope Gregory the Great sent over St. Augustine, no doubt from the most bene- ficent motives. This aggression consisted in Pope Gregory claiming superiority for the bishops he made in this country. There was opposition to this claire in after times, by several bishops, so that the claims of Rome were not accepted unquestioned. Coming to the time of Henry VIII., the Rector admitted that this king was a monster,but he was an instrument in God's hands in breaking the claim of Rouoe's supremacy. In the year 1531, convocation no doubt by pressure, rejected the papal supremacy, and acknowledged the royal supra- macs* with the saving clause "Su far as the law of Christ permits." No doubt soma persons such as Bishop Fisher and Sir Thomas More thought that this reserva- tion was in favour of the Pope, but we have only to look at what convocation said to see that it was in favour of Christ. It was not until' this time that the Romanist schism arose,icknowledging the supremauy of Rome. The Rector then examined the history of the times of Mary and Elizabeth, and read some letters from several church dignitaries, bearing out his contention that it was not the Church of England who separated from the Roman Catholic Church, but that it was the reverse. He finally appealed to the people; not to be carried away with the idea that the Church was a modern institution, but that it was an ancient institution of which they ought to be proud.</p>
        <p>motion of the proposed library, and hoped they would get a good set of books,but not any of the class of Canon Driver's.</p>
        <p>The programme of the evening was a very interest- ing one. Mr. Ward gave an excellent solo, entitled "Romance l'absence," on the oboe. Then followed a bass solo by Mr. James Bennett, who was well received. Mr. C. George came next with a recitation entitled " Amen Corner," which he gave in an admirable man- ner, and Miss Pigot sang " The promise of years." The next name on the programme was Miss Bessie Dutton for a reading, and great regret was expressed at her absence, owing to illness. Miss M. Bennett followed with a solo.</p>
        <p>Mr. FORD was then called upon to give his paper on Circuit Reminiscences. This was a very excellent paper full of wit, humour and anecdote. He said that the Warrington circuit was not of a character to inspire the artist, but it was a circuit of pleasant memories, happy associations and loyal Methodists. All the ministers who had come to Warrington had left it with regret, at having to submit to the inevitable. Mr. Ford then men- tioned some) of the ministers who in past times were stationed at Warrington, among whom were the Revs. Mr. Morris, T. Little, and J. Agar Beet, (now Dr. Beet). Coming to the old local preachers there were Mr. Win. Clarke, Mr. Ledbury, Mr. Richardson, and Dr. Smith, (father of Dr. Starkie Smith, J.P.), who was Alderman, Mayor, and magistrate of Warrington. He (Mr. Ford) sometimes when he thought of those old faces, thought of the lines" 0 for the touch of a vanished hand, the sound of a voice that is still." Mr. Ford told numerous humorous anecdotes of these old local preachers, and also of the local preachers' conveyance which was insti- tuted by the Rev. Mr. Morris. Mr. Ford was loudly cheered when he resumed his seat. His paper will be published in entenso shortly. Miss Gunton then gave a solo entitled ‘• The River of Years," after which Mr. Wm. Silcock briefly addressed the meeting. The remainder of the programme was as follows r.—Violin duet, Miss Pigot and Miss Hawes, encored; solo, Mr. James 134n11ett ; solo " Zuyder Zee" Miss Pigot ; solo, Miss M. Bennett ; and violin solo, Mr. Boothroyd. Mr. Sumrnersgill announced at the end of the meeting that a gentleman had promised 20 volumes of the neivest theological works, as a nucleus for the library. The usual votes of thanks were passed, and the meeting closed by singing the Doxology.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Our friends the Warrington Association Club, played "their big iatch" of the season on Saturday, and veri- fied my prophecy that they would be beaten. St. Elphins proved too much for them by four goals to no- thing. It doesn't sound very nice does it? Well, I would first impress upon the W. A. C. Committee the necessity for not "crying over spilt milk," and for changing their team a little bit. They lost the game chiefly through the bungling of one: or two individuals. Of course they have made several changes lately—and that is not the best way of making a good club as a rule —but it will have to be carried on now until they find one or two more players with a little more" sand" in them. For the present I suggest that the Committee keep their pluck up and their hair down.</p>
        <p>As a matter of fact I noticed that last Saturday they seemed inclined to " get their hair off." Mather played an excellent game, and so did Bannan, Thompson, and Norman. While for St. Elphin's, Cross- ley, Taylor, and Rev. T. W. Cook were a trio of splendid players.</p>
        <p>By the way I hear that a difficulty has arisen respect- ing the W.A.C's conhection with the forthcoming medal competition. They have been warned by the Association Union that unless the gate money taken in the final tie is devoted to either the Union or a charity, they cannot enter the competition. This is a rather unpleasant pre- dicament certainly? It would be much fairer to the other teams, who could all do with n little assistance, if the gate money was divided amongst them. It would, however, be a pity to preclude the W.A.C., and there- fore I hope the competition committee will arrive at some suitable agreement upon the latter, and by way of helping them, I suggest that the proceeds be handed over to'the Infirmary—a place which all flotballers should support. Failing this, I would suggest they hand tho money over to the "father" of our "chapel,"for the bene- fit of blind compositors on the road.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>MISCBLIANBOUS.</head>
        <p>MISCBLIANBOUS.</p>
        <p>The Bst. 61b. North of England Amateur Boxing Championship took place on Wednesday and Thursday nights last at the splendid rooms of the Ormonde Club, Bridgewater-st., Manchester. The en- try was not a very large one, only five choosing to send in their names. This may be accounted for as most of the light weights are waiting for purses which the various clubs in the North of England are offering. Of the five competitors the final result laid between J. Francis, of Manchester (deaf and dumb), and Tom Hackett, of the Warrington Amateur Athletic Club. The contest between them was very evenly contested, both displaying excellent capabilities for which they were warmly applauded. the referee Mr. Jack Gamble deciding in favour of Francis. It is probable that the two will meet again at tha Assault of Arms given by the W.A.A.C. on March the 17th, in the three round exhibition.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CORPORATION AND IM- PERIAL PENNY POSTAGE.</p>
        <p>The General Purposes Committee of the Warrington Corporation have resolved to forward to the Chancellor of the Exchequer and the Postmaster General the fol- lowing memorial Your Memorialists desire to repre- sent to H.M. Government the popular demand which exists for the introduction throughout her Majesty's dominions of the Penny Postage. They would evpress the hope that the serious attention of the Government be forthwith given to the powerful considerations which public bodies have from time to time advanced in sup- port of the scheme, and which are within the know- ledge of H.M. Ministers. The advantages of the system not vnly to Commerce, but in favouring the intercourse of H.M. subjects with their kinsmen at a distance, are of a character so bpneficient as to render, in the opinion of your memorialists, the present appeal deserving of exceptional attention."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>RAILWAY RATES:</head>
        <p>THE RAILWAY RATES:</p>
        <p>A PROTEST FROM WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The General Purposes Committee of the Warrington Corporation will submit the following resolution for adoption, to the Town Council at its meeting an 'Tues- day :—" That this. Council records its protest against the new classification of goods and excessive rates and charges now being exacted by the respective railway companies in the United Kingdom, and urges them to withdraw the same, and revert to the classification land rates in force prior to the 31st December last, and that in the event of any alteration being deemed neces- sary by the Companies, that the classification and rates I in operation prior to the 31st December be taken as the 1 basis of such alteration," and, also," that intim opinion of this Council, the most effectual mode of obtaining an equitable adjustment of railway rates will be the pass- ing of an Act for so extending the powers of the Board ot Trade as to give them jurisdiction to determine what an actual rate or charge should be.</p>
        <p>'7"</p>
        <p>4el.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>WESLEYAN CNAPEL, PADGATE.</head>
        <p>WESLEYAN CNAPEL, PADGATE.</p>
        <p>_ A week of special services were commenced in the above chapel, by the Rev. T. Ross; Warrington, on Sat- urday. Services have been held each evening during the week and on Tuesday afternoon an address to women was delivered. The congregation was small on Wednesday night. Mies M. Bennett sang a solo. On Thursday afternoon. Miss B. Dutton was expected to be present, but owing to her continued illness she was unable to come. An acceptable substitute was found in Mrs. Walker, of Penketh, who gave an im- pressive‘ address. There was a very large gathering of women. A large congregation assembled at night,when the Rev. T. Ross preached, and Mr. James Bennett very effectively sang" 0 what must Idoto be saved." The services were also continued on Friday night. The inte- rest is growing, and there will be services to-night and to-morrow, when the Rev. J. B. Edwards, from Dids- bury, will conduct the services.</p>
        <p>THE PROPOSED CHURCH HOUSE FOR PADGATE. A meeting, to discuss the question of the cost of building the proposed church house, was held in the Church schoolroom, Padgate, on Monday even- ing. Mr. Mather said several fanners had promised to cart the bricks and materials required free of charge, and he (the speaker) knew a retired builder who would superintend the building operations. The foundations could be dug out by the young men interested in their leisure time, and if all joined heartily in the undertaking, it need not cost more than one half of the amount contem- plated. The suggestion of Mr. Mather did not seem to meet with the general approval of the meeting.</p>
        <p>THE MAKING OF STREETS IN RUNCORN. THE POWERS OF THE COMMISSIONERS v. THE RIGHTS OF THE PROPERTY-OWNERS.</p>
        <p>The proposed making of some thirty-three streets by the Runcorn Improvement Commissioners,and the notices served upon the owners of the property abutting on the streets, has created such an amount, of discussion, that we think we shall be performing a useful public service by giving some particulars of the Act of Parliament under which the notices for their being made have been served. Under the Public Health Act of 1875, local authorities 'could only undertake the work of street making in de- fault of their not being made by the owners of the pro- perty aftetsVaitutiEs sszedu Nan orvipaso - Streets Works Act of 1892 all this is changed, and the local authority can now proceed by resolution duly passed and advertised in the local papers that they have ' prepared plans, specifications, estimates, and apportion- ments, and intend to do the work forthwith. lii the notice which is served upon the owners of the property abutting'on the streets proposed to be made, and which must be served within seven day g of the first publica- tion of the resolution .approving the plans, sections (if any), estimates, and apportionments, states that objec- tion may be taken in pursuance of section seven of the Act by written notice served upon the local authority en any of the grounds stated or specified in the section ; but such objection must be taken within one month from the date of notice, or else it is of no avail. The grounds on which objection can be taken are the follow- ing : a. That an alleged street or part of a street is not or does not form part of a street within the meaning</p>
        <p>of the Aet : b. That a street or part of a street is (in whole or in part) a highway repairable by the inhabitants at large: c. That there has been some material informality, de-</p>
        <p>-•— , , feet, or error in, or in respect of, the resolution, notice, plans, sections, or estimate: d. That the proposed works are insufficient or unrea- sonable, or that the estimated expenses are exces-</p>
        <p>sive : e. That any premises ought to be excluded from or• inserted in the provisional apportionment : f. That the provisional apportionment is incorrect in respect of some matter of fact to be specified in the objection or (where the provisional apportionment is made with regard to other considerations. than frontage as hereinafter provided) in respect of the degree of benefit to be derived by any persons, or the amount or value of any work already done by the owner or occupier of any premises. For the purposes of the Act joint tenants or tenants in common may object through one of their number autho- rised in writing under tha hands of the majority of such joint tenants or tenants in common.</p>
        <p>_ It will thus be seen that the objections which may be taken are sufficiently wide and varied as to cover all reasonable grounds, but we point out that it is only on these grounds that any complaint can be listened to. In case of objections being taken the urban or local authority may apply to the magistrates to appoint a time for hearing and determining the matter of all ob- jections made AS in the Act mentioned, and shall publish notice of the time and place appointed and must also serve copies of the notice upon the objectors. The court at the time and place appointed will hear and do- ermine the matter of all such objections. The court have power to quash in whole or in part, or may amend the resolution, plans, sections, estimates, and provisional apportionments, or any of them, on the application either of any objector or of the local authority. The court may also adjourn the hearing and direct any fur- ther notices to oe given. The cost of any proceedings ' before the coart in relation to objections are in the dis- cretion of the court, and the court has full power to direct that the whole or any part of such costs ordered to be paid by an objector or object4rs shall be paid in the first instance by the local authority, and charged as part of the expenses of the works on the premises of the objector or objectors in such proportions as may appear j ust A later section in the Act enacts that the local author- ity may include in the apportionment of expenses any premises which do not front, adjoin or abut on the street or part of the street, but access to which is obtained through a court, passage, or otherwise, and which ip the opinion of the local authority will be benefitted the works. Another section provides that the local authority, may at any time resolve to contribute the whole or a portion of the expenses of any private street works, and pay the same out of the general district rate and district fund. This latter provision will meet the case of those streets which though not made as now re- quired have been contributing to the rates for many years without having received any corresponding benefit.</p>
        <p>We have not by any means given the whole of the Private Street Warks Act of last year, our object being simply to give guidance on those points which we know are sorely exercising the minds of many of the owners of property in Runcorn, aad to point out that if objec- tion is desired to be taken to the proposed street works it can only be done within a specifini time (which ends on the 11th inst.) and in a specified manner, and that there is ample provision before a competent and impar- tial tribunal for the hearing of all objections specified in the Act. If we may venture an opinion it is that the Commis- sioners have delayed so long in the matter of street mak- ing that the Work has become of a efimewhat gigantic character and the thirty-three streets and passages scheduled by no means exhaust all the works of a like nature requiring to be done; though we are quite willing to admit that all loual authorities have been severely handicapped by the cumbrous provisions of the Public Health Act, 1875, in the matter of street making, and that the Act of last year is a great improvement and one means at least of local authorities solving the problem of how to find work for the unemployed, and the spreading of the cost over a term of years a boon which will be greatly appreciated both by property owners, and the general body of ratepayers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>W YCLIFFE CHURCH.</head>
        <p>W YCLIFFE CHURCH.</p>
        <p>On Sunday night, the Rev. John Yonge preached in the abokre church to a, large congregation. The subject of his sermon was "Christ's conipassion for men." The discourse had special reference to the P.S.A. work which the church is about to en- gage in. The rev, gentleman in speaking of the , compassion of Christ on the crowds of people who flocked to bear him, said his example needed to be emulated by his professed followers of the present day. Whilst unwilling to admit all that had been said as to the neglect of this masses by the churches, his opinion was that there was a great deal of unconcern shown by professing Christians as to the salvation of men's souls. Re was glad to say the work in which they were about to engage was to be purely evangelical, the aim of the promoters being the salvation st their fellow-men. The pleasant Sunday afternoons for men at Wycliffe Church, Bewsey-street, 'which had been postponed on account of the smallpox epidemic, last year, will be commenced to-morrow (Sunday) afternoon at three o'clock prompt. Mr. F. 11. Robinson, of Great George- st., Liverpool P.S.A., will preside, and Mr. Alderman W. Peach (late Mayor of Stafford), president of the Stafford P.S A., will explain the P.S.A. movement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>BAPTIST CHRISTIAN GUILD.</head>
        <p>BAPTIST CHRISTIAN GUILD.</p>
        <p>In Com:Motion with. "flocirally interestu% 6ivon by the Rev. E. A of Macclesfield, last evening week, upon the subject of candles, the title of the lecture being " Old friends, with new faces ; or teachings in tallow, illustrated with beautiful symbols." The lecturer had before him a table on which was displayed a very large and varied collec- tion of candles, including the old-fashionel dips, com- monly known as "sixteens," the modern fairy lights, and almost every known description of wax candles, from the one in use in almost every household, to the most elaborately hand painted one, whilst upon the floor, (being too large for the table) he had a candle, measuring sons4thing like six feet high, and about two inches thick. The candles were displayed in a manner as varied as their description. Some werej placed in handsonie surroundings, and costly stands, whilst others occupied old and battered remains of the candlesticks so well-known in our homes; others again being encased in stable lanterns, dark lanterns, and a host of other surroundings. The aim of the rev, gentleman was to derive some practical lessons from the objects presented and he did so in a most admirable manner. He com- pared the " tallow dips "and the more elaborate "com- posite " of the present day, and showed that whilst en- cased in the candle bsx, elaborate though it may be, they were doing nothing towards making the world any brighter and batter, but that being brought to dis- play their talents, they both had a good effect on their surroundings. He had candles that " sputtered " and candles with " a thief" in them, candles in dark lan- terns; candles in lanterns with cracks in for the wind, and rain; candles in lanterns all windows, bright and clean; candles that often wanted snuffing; candles with extinguishers on, that wou;cl not light ; he had the frail taper, which whilst only ocaring a small light readily lit up others, and lost nothing of its own power ; and the humble night light which burns faithfully through the long lone hours of the night. All these be showed in a most vivid manner, as portraying the various classes of Christians, and, snuffing asmoky light, carefully showed how that might become ussfu I, and of good to others; that by cleaning the dark lantern of doubt,and attendiag to the cracks and other pints of danger, and by rernov7 ing the extinguisher of prejudice, all might in their own humble or exalted spheres do just the work, give just the light, that none other could do. At the close he lighted a large number of the candles ,and showed that a body of Christians, thoegh they might be as varied as the candles before them in sect, disposition and other details, vet if unitad'aan " one" in their work they would better exhibit the spirit of the Divine Master and be far more successful is their labours. The candles, which were in themselves quite a unique collection, and proved a great attractiou were supplied by the eminent firm of Messrs: Price and Co.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>AN ACCUSATION.</head>
        <p>AN ACCUSATION.</p>
        <p>At the Reformed Chtrch of England, Bold-st., on Sunday evening, Rev. I. T. Reed took for the sub- ject of his sermon "Is the Bible alone the rule of Christian faith ? "hem: a reply to a lecture given in St. L'lttry's Roman Catholic Church, Butter- market-st. He took frr his text Matthew xv. 1-3., and a portion of Colosians ii. He quoted from the Docuty version of the New Testament. His object was to show that tae Holy Scriptures were a sufficient rule for the mly faith, in contradiction to various statements ma cla in a lecture delivered by the Rev. Father Wray, at St. Mary's Roman Cattiolic Church, in the course ol which it was said that " the Bible alone is not the ru's of faith, and cannot be the foundation of the Cliristan religion. Our Lord wrote nothing, and most of tin Apostles wrote nothing. The New Testament was notwrittea till several years after our Lord's ascension." That a rule of faith was necs- sary was admitted by b.th Romare Catholics and Protes- tants. The Roman Caholic rule of faith consisted of the Nicene Creed and tie bull of Pope Pius IV., pub- lished as a creed on Deember 9th, 1561, the first two articles of which reqlired that the apostolic and ecclesiastical traditions xi admitted and embraced, and also the Holy Scripture according to the interpretation of the Church. The Pntestant rule of faith was to the effect that " the Holy 3criptures, which is tke Word of God containeth all thilgs necessary to salvation." Mr. Reed quoted the word;of Bishop Lyons and St. Chrysos- tom, to prove th.,t on his subject the latter said " Look to no otner teacher, thm hast the oracles of God, none teaches like these." Tie preacher went on to ask what was the Roman Catholc Church doing towards the cir- culation ef the Scriptues at the present day ? They were doing absolutely nothing. They were afraid to circulate them lest the minds of the people should be opened to the truth. 'he Roman Catholic Church was really afraid of the wort of God.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>LOCAL PREACIERS' CONVERSAZIONE AT</head>
        <p>LOCAL PREACIERS' CONVERSAZIONE AT</p>
        <p>BOLD-ST.</p>
        <p>The first annual claversazinne of the Warrington• Wesleyan local preehers, was held in Bold-street Sunday School, on loaday night. The proceeds of the meeting are tozo towards the formation of a local preachers' iihary. There was a large attend- ance. Mr. H. Garnet, J.P.' was in the chair, and he V 7 as supported by Mssrs. J. Summersgill, W. Collier, E. Ford, C. George, S. doss, W. Y. Lawton and W. Hick- man. The mcetinvas opened with a hymn, followed by prayer by Mr. 4immersgill. Mr. GarnvErr briefly expressed his s.ympEby with the movement for the pro.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
