      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <p>AN APi'LETON FARMER S NOTICE TO QUIT. A TENANT WHO REFUSED TO "RIDE WITH THE YEOMANRY."</p>
        <p>We give here particulars of a matter which has excited a good deal of local interest, and which has been commented on during the week by news- papers in different parts of the country. It is the ejectment of Mr. Joseph Knowles, of Yew Tree Farm, Appleton, on the estate of Colonel Piers Egerton Warburton. The facts of the affair have been given publicly, both the tenant and the land- lord having told their own version of the story. The "Liverpool Daily Post" of Monday published an interview with both of the parties concerned. The tenant is the descendant of a long line of farmers whose names can be traced hack on the rent roll of the Arley estate, almost to the very time when the Warburton family became possessed of the land—a period of no less than 400 years. For the past few generations the Knowles family have exhibited a turn for that class of politics and religion which few Cheshire Farmers dare indulge in—they were supporters of Liberalism and Nonconformity. The late Mr. Knowles was an ardent Wesleyan and a Liberal, and he was one of the hardest workers in establishing the Appleton Thorn Wesleyan Chapel, built 40 years ago. When he died, his son, Mr. Joseph Knowles, renewed the tenancy of the farm which, by the way, is but a yearly one. In that renewal he accepted the same con- ditions as did his father, and amongst those conditions were clauses to the effect that of " boon service," the tenant would have to do four day's work per year, pro- vide a rider in the yeomanry, and every other year supply the landlord with a cheese ; further, all game on I the property, (including rabbits) should be the property 'of the landlord, who enjoyed the right to hunt. As far as we can gather, the four days boon service has not been enacted of late years, but the landlord's cart has regularly called every two years for the cheese. But it is the next stipulation in the agreement which the "notice to quit" was based upon. Regarding this Colonel Warburton said in the interview " I am simply carrying out my father's wishes. When Mr. James Knowles, who did not ride in the Yeomanry,died,his son Joseph agreed to ride in the Yeomanry,and got the farm. Not fulfilling that promising, my fitther served him with notice to quit. This notice had been issued ten months before I came into possession on my father's death, which occurred eighteen months ago. I have not served any notice upon them other than the one I served the other day calling upon thetn to quit a house they ought to have left a month ago." We have seen the agreement upon which the lease was renewed and it provided that the tenant was "To supply a tnan.and horse for the Yeomanry." Mr. Joseph Knowles says he has offered to supply a man and horse, but his offer had been refused ; his father had sent a substitute ; but the landlord required, that he should give his personal services. Mr. Knowles refused, as he termed it, "to waste ten days, kicking his heels about publichouses." And then with regard to Colonel War- burton's statement that he was only " carrying out his father's wishes," he produced the following letter "The Dene, Northwich, Cheshire, February 19, 1891.— Sir,—l was surprised to hear from Sergeant Cox that you had informed him of your intention not to join the Arley troop. You are perfectly aware of the terms under which you managed to obtain your farm last year, so if you decline to fulfil your agreement I must request my father to take steps that will be very unpleasant to us both.—Yours truly, P. EGERTON WARRIIRTON." I think," he remarked " that this shows that the Colonel had a rather interested • wish ' in the matter himself. Flis father died in 1891, and at the time that letter was written, he was a blind, flail old man." .But Mr. Knowles and his family contend that.this question of the yeomanry is not altogether the cause of their ejectment. Mr. Joseph Knowles states that his Nonconformist principles are responsible for it. These are the questions and answers to this aspect of the ques- tion, in the interview:— Do you remember the particulars of the letter which was sent to your father's executors, stating that the farm would have to be given up unless some member of the family attended the Established Church ?—Yes, very well. lam one of my father's executors, and sawthe letter, and have several witnesses who also read it. Are you sure it made reference to attending the Eng- lish Church ?—Yes, it said that the landowner desired some one resident on the farm to attend the church. There was no mention of riding in the Yeomanry. The letter accompanied the notice to quit. Why was that notice withdrawn ?-oh, I suppose they thought it might become public. The letter was taken back when the notice was withdrawn. Do you think the Rev. Mr. Ash had anything to do with it ?—No, I cannot say that I do, Butte give you an instance of the parson's attitude ti,wards Wesleyans, I may tell you that when I got my child baptised by a Nonconformist he (Mr. Ash) said I might as well have On the same aspect of the quion—thiWbonversation took place between the interviewer and Colonel War- burton : Do you know anything of the RSV. Mr. Ash and the Nonconformist tenants, or of his feeling towards the I Appleton Wesleyan body P—l know nothing about the Rev. Mr. Ash's procedure. Naturally as an English Church clergyman he would be anxious to promote the interests of his Church, and I, as a Churchman, would like alkmy tenants to he Churchmen. Bat I have tenants, I suppose of all sects. I did not know the Knowleses were Wesleyans. I knew they did not go to Church. I have not seen the Rev. Mr. Ash about the Knowles's, nor has be directly or indirectly had anything to do with their removal. I know there was great hos- tility shown while the church was being built. Can you tell me any particulars of the letter,subsequent- ly withdrawn, sent to the executors of the late Mr.Jaines Knowles, giving notice to quit unless the family at- tended the Established Church ?—I know nothing about such a letter. There was a previous notice given, but it was given a month too late; otherwise the Knowlesses would have had to leave a year ago.</p>
        <p>Mr. Knowles was under notice to quit the farm on Tuesday last, the farm having been let to a Mr. Lambert. This gentleman, according to Colonel Warburton, is a Churchman. He commenced to plough the land some days ago ; but one night last week someone imashed one of Mr. Lambert's plough-shares, and perpretrated other acts of petty damage. Mr. Knowles has no idea who the culprit is ; but Colonel Warburton says if he finds him out he shill prosecute.</p>
        <p>' The Liverpool" Echo" in a leader comments upon tileaincident as follows i---" Quens Deus yule perdere prism densentat. The action of Colonel Piers Egerton Warbur- tdn, of Arley Hall, Cheshire, in dismissing his tenant, Mr. James Knowles because he refused to ride in the Yeomanry Regiment of which the gallant colonel is commander, suggests that the divinejtat has gone forth against the " gentlemen of England," and that the Local Government bill, which by a curious synchronism, , is to be introduced by Mr. Fowler almost simultaneously with the publication of Mr. Knowles's wrongs to the world, is to be the instrument by which the decree is to be carried into execution. The story would be an in- credible one were it not confirmed out of the very mouth of this egregioas specimen of the landlord class. The Warhurtons are a famous old Cheshire race. Their names occurs in Shirley's" Noblemen and Gentlemen of England" as that of a family that has held land in Cheshire in unbroken male succession since the time of Henry VII. The fact is evidence no less of the vigour than that of thet antiquity of their race, the self-willed energy of which, at any rate, shows no symptom of i decay n the person of its present representative. But, as often happens on these old county estates, the name of Knerles occurs in the Arley tenant-roll almost as far back as that of the owner of the estate. From sire to son they have paid rent to the Squire of Arley for generations, and it is only natural that relations Of almost feudal respect and regard should have existed between them. The squires, indeed, seem to have forgotten, or to have failed to heed, the passage of time, and the changes which it brings in its train, to such a degree as to have endeavoured to maintain by bree obligations on the part of the tenant which have long been out of date. Two hundred and fifty years ago, when the, Warburton of the day rode (as the head of such a family may be presumed to have done) to the standard of Charles 11. at Worcester„ it was only natural that he should order out his tenantry and dependants to ride at his back, and, saving those of them whose sympathies' like those of the present Mr. James Knowles, would probably have been with the Roundheads, it is unlikely that any of them would have failed to respond to the call. We do not live, however, in the middle ages, and farming now no longer estab- lishes a feudal or quasi-feudal relation between landlord and tenant. It has become a mere matter of business, in which each of the contracting parties has a perfect right to safeguard his legitimate interests, but no more. In requiring his tenantry to join the yeomanry under, pain of beingtutned out of farms which they and their an- cestors have occupied for centuries, Colonel Warburton Is making an unjustifiable and, in the long run, futile attempt to set back the hand of the dial for a couple of centuries or more. In so doing, he gains nothing worth speaking of fr himself; while he arouses against his " order " a feeling of hostility the depth and strength of which he evidently only very imperfectly appreci- ates. Colonel Warburton denies that the notice to quit which be served on Mr. Knowles had any connection with the refusal of the latter, who is a Wesleyan, to attend the services of the Established Church ; on this point Mr. Knowles's account of the mat- ter differs from his landlord. But both seemed agreed that the refusal of the tenant to waste ten days of his time "kicking his heels," as be expresses it, "about public-houses at a personal loss to himself of about ten pounds annually is the prime occasion of the termination of his tenancy. Colonel Warburton attempts to defend himself by stating that he is merely carrying out his de- beased's uncles wishes, but the plea only makes his case ' worse. Much may be condoned in an octogenarian brought up in the narrow prejedices of the anti-reform period which would never be permitted in a man of the preSent generation, and it was Col. Warburton's duty,if he found so unjust a condition in existence of his estate,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <p>to abrogate it, instead of enforcing it, as he is doing, to the bitter end. The oddest feaLure of the case is that Colonel Warburton himselt should fail to see what a blow he is delivertng by such unwarrantable tyranny at lordlordispn as an institution. It is to be presumed that his object in thus asserting its extremest form his "right to do what he will with his own" is to maintain and not to destroy his rights. He will find that his dismissal of Mr. Knowles will supply a pregnant teat to the land reformers and agitators throughout the kingdom, and that he has but hastened the day when those rights,real or supposed, will be subjected to severe curtailment and strict limitation."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>A" SCENE" AT THE SALE.</head>
        <p>A" SCENE" AT THE SALE.</p>
        <p>The sale of Mr. Knowles' stock was arranged for Tue S- - afternoon. Some 'bus loads of people left Warring- ton for Yew Tree Farm,arnongst them were several WOll known Warrington Liberals and Nonconformists. Upon arriving at the farm it was found that there were. farmers present from all parts of Cheshire ; the probable' number being 300. Mr. !!Mainwaring of Warrington, was the auctioneer, and before opening the sale, he men- tioned that he was employed by Mr. Knowles to sell hire up ; it was always unpleasant to sell a man up, partien- larly in this case, and he hoped they would buy well. The stook, which was in excellent condition, sold well. I and realized about £l,OOO. The auction did not go off in a quiet, ordinary way. There were many persons present who felt it necessary to give expression to their opinions, and about the farm in every direction could be observed groups of farmers discussing the facts of the case. About four o'clock, however, quite a " scene " occurred. Near a large hay shed, Mr. Bruche- mite, the socialistic advocate and lodging-house keeper of Warrington, was haranguing a small crowd of farmers and labourers. He had launched forth upon a tirade against the English land laws, and said the system of yearly agreement, under which English farmers worked was a "rotten" one ; and he told them that even in France and Scotland, they had land by the seven 14, or 21 years lease. Several farmers lustily cheered him here, and agreed with what be said ; and admitted that they wanted reform. He then gave his opinions pretty freely about Mr. Knowles " notice to quit "and also about the general wrongs under which Cheshire farmers laboured. In those opinions he was joined by almost all those present, and it was a rather uncommon sight to see even the Tory farmers applauding the argu- ments of a socialist. In a short time Alr.Dennis Donnella* of the Warrington Home Rule Party came on to the scene and made a speech dealing with the grievances of the English and Irish farmers. At this juncture Mr. Do son of the Warrington Board of Guardians appeared on the scene, and for about half-an-bour a most extraor- dinary debate was carried on by him, several farmers, and Mr. Brucbemite and Mr. Donnellan. Strong oPin- ions were freely passed and the discussion was proceed- ing when it was discovered that almost tbe whole of the people had left the auctioneer for the meeting. As considerable amount of business had to be transacted, Mr. Knowles and the auctioneer asked the speakers to desist until after the sale. The gathering then broke trP and the debate was not renewed. Mrs. Knowles, senior, who is a real lady " democrat" told our reporter that she considered the treatment of her son had been Most dnjust, and she had advised 'Ann to remain until he was evicted. They had taken a house near the Wesleyan Chapel—off the Arley estate—Which they were going to inhabit that night. All the Warrington Wesleyan local preachers who visited the Appleton Chapel used to put up at Yew Tree Farm, the Knowles being most hospitable and generous entertainers,</p>
        <p>, THE FUTURE. The Northwich “Chronicle publishes an intervieW which its representatives has had with Col. Warburton. The following conversation is reported as having- taken place in the course of the interview: Interviewer: I suppose that in the case of any nevr tenant coming to the estate you make it a requiremens that he shall be a Churchman ? Col. Warburton: I shall in future. The Interviewer: And also the Yeomanry? Col. Warburton: Yes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>THE SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>FIRE AT SALTPOR'r. A store shed and a fitters shop at Saltport on the Manchester Ship Canal were destroyed by fire on hforl• day morning. About five e'clock the watchman on duty at the port discovered the buildings to be on fire, and e 0 they contained stores of an inflamable nature, the fire spread rapidly, and it was at one time feared that the large warehouse would also be involved in the destruc- tion. As this wooden structure covered about B,o°° casks of resin, the utmost alarm prevailed for some time* but the workmen shortly arrived to commence their daily labours, and they worked with well directed allci untiring energy until the flames 'were eetinguished eight o'clock. Fortunately there was an abundance 0 water at hand. The warehouse escaped serious injury. The origin of the outbreak is unknown, but it is sur; mised that a spark from a passing locomotive was th_ cause of the mischief. The damage is estimated a' between £2OO and £3OO. 1V1.,A.113, LT ra 'IC&gt; YOUTIa AT. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Heber Worthington, aged 16 years,of 21, English-row; Runcorn, met with a fearful death on the Ship Cana' railway, on Monday night. He was employed as s pbunter in the traffic department, at Wlgg's Wharf Bottom Locks, where material for the Old Quay Work! are transhipped from wagons to flats and vice versa. S' 7 30 the deceased and his mate, Alfred Ellison, horse driver, York-st., were shunting an empty wagon, Wheu , the deceased applied the brake, and the block failing tki° grip properly, the deceased slipped and fell on t ' metals. One of the wheels passed over the upper p 01; tion of his head and smashed his skull. Death Iva instantaneous. Constables Booth and Ridgway, be/ Informed of the occurrence, took the ambulance W th„. spot and conveyed the unfortunate youth to his hsh3l.o". A portion of his brains was found three feet from tho body was lying. Mr. R. Dobson, deputy coroasi; I held the Inquest at the Police Station, on Tuesday afr„e noon, and evidence bearing out the above facts ha°',". J been given, a verdict of "Accidental death" turned.</p>
